{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296876190,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b767ddb76433a86e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git -C /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research branch --show-current && ls"},"output":"issue-recursive-descent-order-research\nAGENTS.md\nbuild.rs\nCargo.lock\nCargo.toml\nCLAUDE.md\nclippy.toml\nCONTEXT.md\ndocs\ndraft.md\nexamples\njustfile\nplans\nprelude.toy\nREADME.md\nresearch-log\nruntime\nsite\nsrc\ntests\n","metadata":{"output":"issue-recursive-descent-order-research\nAGENTS.md\nbuild.rs\nCargo.lock\nCargo.toml\nCLAUDE.md\nclippy.toml\nCONTEXT.md\ndocs\ndraft.md\nexamples\njustfile\nplans\nprelude.toy\nREADME.md\nresearch-log\nruntime\nsite\nsrc\ntests\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git -C /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research branch --show-current && ls","time":{"start":1788296876185,"end":1788296876188}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me start by reading AGENTS.md and understanding the task.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ecccebf001CoxHRiZQqWFjBx","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05eccc780001HIvFIZUfjh8hMg"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296877689,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b7b698662995c525","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/AGENTS.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/AGENTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Working agreement for AI agents\n2: \n3: This repository is a language-design project, mostly prose and design reasoning rather than\n4: code. Two things follow: how something is written is part of the work, and where an idea came\n5: from matters as much as whether it works.\n6: \n7: Run `just test` to run the test suite (`cargo nextest run`); `just` alone lists other recipes.\n8: \n9: # Writing\n10: \n11: The goal is not to pass as human-written. It's that the usual tells are downstream of real\n12: defects, and fixing the defect removes the tell for free. Uniform section lengths mean attention\n13: wasn't allocated. Comments that restate the code mean nothing was observed. A Roadmap heading\n14: over an empty list means the template was filled in rather than the document written. Chase the\n15: defect, not the fingerprint.\n16: \n17: ## The rule most of this reduces to\n18: \n19: Detail should track how much the thing deserves. The hard part gets four paragraphs; the obvious\n20: part gets a line, or nothing. Asymmetry is what attention looks like on the page.\n21: \n22: ## Prose and documentation\n23: \n24: Write what you observed, not what should be true. \"Fails on inputs with a BOM\" is worth more\n25: than three paragraphs correctly describing the happy path.\n26: \n27: Stop when you're done. No closing summary, no \"Key takeaways,\" no paragraph restating the\n28: section above it. If the document is short enough to read, it's short enough not to need a\n29: recap.\n30: \n31: Name the thing, not its label. \"Q8\" and \"option 3\" tell a reader nothing and cost them a lookup;\n32: \"the vectorizability question\" says what is being discussed. A short identifier is fine as the\n33: *heading* of the thing it names, so that it can be referred to and linked, but it is not a\n34: substitute for saying what the reference is about.\n35: \n36: Cross-references inside a document are links. If one section points at another, the reader\n37: should be able to follow it, which means the target is a heading and the reference is real\n38: markdown. A reference that only names a section leaves the reader to search for it.\n39: \n40: Add a heading when a reader needs to navigate past something. Six headings over 400 words is\n41: furniture, not structure. Same for horizontal rules: they separate genuinely unrelated material,\n42: which is rare inside one document.\n43: \n44: Don't write a section you can't fill. A 200-line script doesn't need Contributing, Roadmap,\n45: Acknowledgements, or a badge row.\n46: \n47: Bold-lead bullets (`**Term**: gloss`) are fine two or three at a time. A screen of them is either\n48: a table or a paragraph that hasn't admitted it yet. Tables are for data with two dimensions; a\n49: list of things is a list.\n50: \n51: No emoji as section markers or status indicators. If the project already uses them, match the\n52: project.\n53: \n54: Some phrases to cut on sight, because they consume a sentence without adding one: \"It's worth\n55: noting that,\" \"It's important to remember,\" \"not just X, but Y,\" \"in today's fast-paced world,\"\n56: \"delve into,\" \"leverage\" where \"use\" works, and the adjective stack of robust / seamless /\n57: comprehensive / powerful applied to things whose robustness has not been measured.\n58: \n59: ## Comments\n60: \n61: Comment the why. The what is already there in the code, in a form that can't drift out of date.\n62: \n63: ```python\n64: # Retry with backoff: the upstream returns 502 for ~30s after a deploy.\n65: ```\n66: \n67: not\n68: \n69: ```python\n70: # Retry the request\n71: ```\n72: \n73: Docstring length should track surprise, not function length. A three-line helper whose name says\n74: what it does needs nothing. A three-line helper that must be called before `connect()` or it\n75: silently no-ops needs a sentence saying so.\n76: \n77: No banner comments dividing a file into decorated sections. If the file needs sections that\n78: badly, it needs to be two files.\n79: \n80: ## Code shape\n81: \n82: Build for callers that exist. If nothing calls `from_dict`, don't write `from_dict` because\n83: `to_dict` exists. Symmetry is not a requirement.\n84: \n85: Don't guard against states that can't happen. A null check on a value constructed two lines above\n86: is noise, and it trains readers to skim past the checks that matter. Where a real failure is\n87: possible, prefer failing loudly over swallowing it into a default.\n88: \n89: Short names are fine in short scopes. `i`, `n`, `ctx`, `df` are the local dialect and everyone\n90: reads them faster than `current_row_index`. Reach for the long name when the scope is long or the\n91: meaning is genuinely non-obvious.\n92: \n93: Match the surrounding file, including conventions this guide dislikes. Consistency with the\n94: codebase outranks consistency with this document. Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch,\n95: and don't fold a style cleanup into a behavior change.\n96: \n97: Don't put emoji or checkmarks in console output unless the project already does.\n98: \n99: ## Tests\n100: \n101: Test behavior that matters, not the shape of the implementation. A test file that mirrors the\n102: source file function-for-function is usually testing that the code was typed, not that it works.\n103: \n104: Every fixed bug gets the test that would have caught it. That test is worth more than the whole\n105: happy-path suite.\n106: \n107: Don't assert things that cannot fail. Asserting that a constructor set the field you just passed\n108: it will never once be red.\n109: \n110: The corpus is one YAML file per case in `tests/corpus/`, holding `program`, an optional `input`,\n111: either the expected `output` or `refuses: true`, and any extra checks the case asks for -- today\n112: only `snapshot`, a list of backends whose emitted code gets pinned as well.\n113: \n114: `node_types` is different: a case never writes it, `tests/tag_corpus.rs` does, on every run. It's\n115: the AST shapes (`src/tags.rs`, drawn from `tir::Kind`) the program exercises, as dotted paths --\n116: `arith.add`, `selection.narrow`, `projection` -- and it is what the site's case tree browses by.\n117: \n118: Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true`\n119: means they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business. A\n120: snapshot is the exception on top, for a claim the output cannot carry. A program that does not\n121: compile is not a corpus case: nothing about it differs per backend, so it stays in step_*.rs\n122: where the message is an insta snapshot.\n123: \n124: Use block scalars (`|`) so nothing is coerced by YAML's scalar rules. Unknown keys, unknown\n125: backend names, and a case with both or neither of `output` and `refuses` are all errors, because\n126: a case that asks for nothing looks exactly like a case that passes.\n127: \n128: ## Uncertainty\n129: \n130: Say what you're unsure about and what would settle it. This is only useful if it's selective:\n131: hedging everything equally carries no information, and it reads as insurance rather than honesty.\n132: \n133: Never invent an API signature. If you can't verify a call, say the call is unverified, or check\n134: it. A plausible-looking wrong argument is the most expensive thing in this document, because it\n135: survives review. It looks exactly like knowledge.\n136: \n137: Don't announce compliance. No \"As requested,\" no \"I've carefully ensured,\" no summary of your own\n138: diligence at the end of a PR description. Describe the change and why.\n139: \n140: ## Typography\n141: \n142: Plain ASCII everywhere: code, identifiers, commit messages, config, and prose. Straight quotes,\n143: `--` not an em dash, three periods not a single-character ellipsis, `->` not an arrow glyph. This\n144: is about grep, diffs, and terminals. Characters a person would not type on a keyboard are noise\n145: in a git repo. A person's name is whatever they spell it as, and is not subject to this.\n146: \n147: If you find yourself reaching for `--` more than once or twice a page, a run of dashes is a\n148: paragraph that hasn't picked its sentence boundaries. Use a period, a colon, or parentheses.\n149: \n150: ## What not to do in the name of this guide\n151: \n152: Don't manufacture the appearance of human authorship. Specifically: no deliberate typos, no\n153: invented TODO or HACK comments, no fabricated dated notes or initials, no commented-out code that\n154: was never live, no artificial inconsistency between files, and no performed uncertainty about\n155: things you're actually confident in.\n156: \n157: All of these are false claims about how the artifact came to be, embedded in the artifact. That's\n158: worse than any stylistic tell, and it's the failure mode this guide is most likely to induce if\n159: read carelessly.\n160: \n161: # Provenance\n162: \n163: Four categories, describing where content came from rather than who typed it.\n164: \n165: **Human-written.** Copied verbatim from what a human wrote, or edited only superficially:\n166: formatting, typos, wrapping. The human produced the text itself.\n167: \n168: **Human-authored.** The content is the logical interpretation of what a human said. The test: if\n169: the human's instruction had been pasted into the file instead of the expanded result, would it\n170: carry the same value? If someone with sufficient expertise, human or agent, would derive\n171: substantially this result from that instruction, it is human-authored.\n172: \n173: **Anything produced by an exploratory loop counts as human-authored**, where an agent proposes\n174: options and a human makes the design decisions. That holds even when large stretches of the\n175: resulting text were generated verbatim by the agent.\n176: \n177: **Derived.** Follows from constraints already present: existing patterns in this repo, a decision\n178: recorded earlier, or a cited external source. When the existing constraints leave essentially one\n179: reasonable option, taking it is derived, not invented. If the constraint itself came from a\n180: human, the result is human-authored instead.\n181: \n182: **Agent-invented.** A real decision an agent made on its own. Something is agent-invented only\n183: when all of these hold:\n184: \n185: - it did not come out of a human authoring or exploration loop,\n186: - it was not forced by existing patterns or constraints,\n187: - it is not obvious, in the sense that there was more than one reasonable option,\n188: - it did not come from a cited source.\n189: \n190: Carve this down to the **minimal surface** that actually meets those tests. A section can be\n191: human-authored overall while one specific choice inside it was purely the agent's call, and that\n192: choice is what to name. The purpose is to make visible which decisions no human has actually\n193: made, so a reader can tell a requirement from an accident.\n194: \n195: Consulting a source does not by itself make a result derived. An agent may research a source and\n196: then decide something well beyond it.\n197: \n198: ## Correcting provenance\n199: \n200: Provenance is a fact about how a file came to be, not a property recoverable from the file\n201: afterwards. It has to be recorded when the commit is made or it is gone.\n202: \n203: **Do not classify inherited files confidently.** If a file arrives already written and you do not\n204: know its authoring history, the honest entry is that the history is unknown. Nothing in a\n205: finished artifact distinguishes text a human wrote from text an agent wrote, and guessing\n206: produces a false record that looks like a true one.\n207: \n208: **If you get provenance wrong, do not rewrite history.** Add a follow-up commit that names the\n209: bad commit and states the correction. Both entries stay in the log. A rewritten history is a\n210: worse record than a corrected one, because it hides that the mistake was possible.\n211: \n212: ## Authority ordering\n213: \n214: This is the practical reason the categories exist. When two parts of the repository conflict,\n215: human-written and human-authored content wins, derived content yields to it, and agent-invented\n216: content has the least authority: a placeholder that survived, not a decision.\n217: \n218: If you are changing something human-authored because agent-invented content elsewhere assumed\n219: otherwise, you have it backwards. Change the invented part, or raise the conflict.\n220: \n221: # Committing\n222: \n223: Agents may commit in this repository, and must add the `Co-Authored-By` trailer when they do.\n224: \n225: The subject line says what changed. The body says why, or what was ruled out, plus the provenance\n226: lines that apply. Omit categories that are empty rather than writing \"none\". A bulleted inventory\n227: of every file touched duplicates the diff.\n228: \n229: ```\n230: Add cardinality types to the design draft\n231: \n232: Human-authored: the Vec/Stream split and the indexing-promise distinction\n233: Derived: jq operator semantics (jq 1.7 manual)\n234: Agent-invented: the `fold` block syntax\n235: \n236: Co-Authored-By: <agent identifier>\n237: ```\n238: \n239: A commit with no agent `Co-Authored-By` trailer is entirely human-authored and human-written.\n240: That is the default assumption, so never add the trailer to a commit you did not contribute to.\n241: \n242: ## Citing external material\n243: \n244: When content draws on an external source, name it, in the commit message and in the file itself\n245: where a reader would want it. \"Derived from the jq manual\" is enough; a link is better.\n246: \n247: This matters most where the language deliberately borrows from an existing one. A reader should\n248: be able to tell \"jq does it this way\" from \"we decided this.\"\n249: \n250: # Limits\n251: \n252: ## Stop and ask\n253: \n254: Flag these to a human and wait for explicit authorization. Flag before writing to files, not\n255: after:\n256: \n257: - Licensing or copyright, where the license is unclear, restrictive, or unknown\n258: - Security or access: credentials, keys, attack surface, or permissions you were not clearly\n259:   granted\n260: - Personal information, meaning anything identifying a person, from any source\n261: - Anything else you would want a human to weigh\n262: \n263: Explain the specific concern rather than asking generically, and do not proceed on silence.\n264: \n265: ## Never include\n266: \n267: Copyrighted material from other repositories. Reference and describe freely; do not copy source\n268: text. Concepts and documented behavior are fine to describe in your own words.\n269: \n270: Personal information or secrets about anyone, the repository owner included: names, contact\n271: details, credentials, private paths, internal hostnames.\n272: \n273: Paths or details from unrelated private repositories or systems. Design rationale should stand on\n274: its own without them.\n275: \n276: ---\n277: \n278: Delete anything here you disagree with. Every rule earns its place by making the repository more\n279: useful to the next person who opens it, and if a rule stops doing that it has no other argument\n280: in its favor. A guide nobody follows costs more than no guide, because it turns every review into\n281: an argument about the document instead of the code.\n\n(End of file - total 281 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Working agreement for AI agents\n\nThis repository is a language-design project, mostly prose and design reasoning rather than\ncode. Two things follow: how something is written is part of the work, and where an idea came\nfrom matters as much as whether it works.\n\nRun `just test` to run the test suite (`cargo nextest run`); `just` alone lists other recipes.\n\n# Writing\n\nThe goal is not to pass as human-written. It's that the usual tells are downstream of real\ndefects, and fixing the defect removes the tell for free. Uniform section lengths mean attention\nwasn't allocated. Comments that restate the code mean nothing was observed. A Roadmap heading\nover an empty list means the template was filled in rather than the document written. Chase the\ndefect, not the fingerprint.\n\n## The rule most of this reduces to\n\nDetail should track how much the thing deserves. The hard part gets four paragraphs; the obvious\npart gets a line, or nothing. Asymmetry is what attention looks like on the page.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/AGENTS.md","text":"# Working agreement for AI agents\n\nThis repository is a language-design project, mostly prose and design reasoning rather than\ncode. Two things follow: how something is written is part of the work, and where an idea came\nfrom matters as much as whether it works.\n\nRun `just test` to run the test suite (`cargo nextest run`); `just` alone lists other recipes.\n\n# Writing\n\nThe goal is not to pass as human-written. It's that the usual tells are downstream of real\ndefects, and fixing the defect removes the tell for free. Uniform section lengths mean attention\nwasn't allocated. Comments that restate the code mean nothing was observed. A Roadmap heading\nover an empty list means the template was filled in rather than the document written. Chase the\ndefect, not the fingerprint.\n\n## The rule most of this reduces to\n\nDetail should track how much the thing deserves. The hard part gets four paragraphs; the obvious\npart gets a line, or nothing. Asymmetry is what attention looks like on the page.\n\n## Prose and documentation\n\nWrite what you observed, not what should be true. \"Fails on inputs with a BOM\" is worth more\nthan three paragraphs correctly describing the happy path.\n\nStop when you're done. No closing summary, no \"Key takeaways,\" no paragraph restating the\nsection above it. If the document is short enough to read, it's short enough not to need a\nrecap.\n\nName the thing, not its label. \"Q8\" and \"option 3\" tell a reader nothing and cost them a lookup;\n\"the vectorizability question\" says what is being discussed. A short identifier is fine as the\n*heading* of the thing it names, so that it can be referred to and linked, but it is not a\nsubstitute for saying what the reference is about.\n\nCross-references inside a document are links. If one section points at another, the reader\nshould be able to follow it, which means the target is a heading and the reference is real\nmarkdown. A reference that only names a section leaves the reader to search for it.\n\nAdd a heading when a reader needs to navigate past something. Six headings over 400 words is\nfurniture, not structure. Same for horizontal rules: they separate genuinely unrelated material,\nwhich is rare inside one document.\n\nDon't write a section you can't fill. A 200-line script doesn't need Contributing, Roadmap,\nAcknowledgements, or a badge row.\n\nBold-lead bullets (`**Term**: gloss`) are fine two or three at a time. A screen of them is either\na table or a paragraph that hasn't admitted it yet. Tables are for data with two dimensions; a\nlist of things is a list.\n\nNo emoji as section markers or status indicators. If the project already uses them, match the\nproject.\n\nSome phrases to cut on sight, because they consume a sentence without adding one: \"It's worth\nnoting that,\" \"It's important to remember,\" \"not just X, but Y,\" \"in today's fast-paced world,\"\n\"delve into,\" \"leverage\" where \"use\" works, and the adjective stack of robust / seamless /\ncomprehensive / powerful applied to things whose robustness has not been measured.\n\n## Comments\n\nComment the why. The what is already there in the code, in a form that can't drift out of date.\n\n```python\n# Retry with backoff: the upstream returns 502 for ~30s after a deploy.\n```\n\nnot\n\n```python\n# Retry the request\n```\n\nDocstring length should track surprise, not function length. A three-line helper whose name says\nwhat it does needs nothing. A three-line helper that must be called before `connect()` or it\nsilently no-ops needs a sentence saying so.\n\nNo banner comments dividing a file into decorated sections. If the file needs sections that\nbadly, it needs to be two files.\n\n## Code shape\n\nBuild for callers that exist. If nothing calls `from_dict`, don't write `from_dict` because\n`to_dict` exists. Symmetry is not a requirement.\n\nDon't guard against states that can't happen. A null check on a value constructed two lines above\nis noise, and it trains readers to skim past the checks that matter. Where a real failure is\npossible, prefer failing loudly over swallowing it into a default.\n\nShort names are fine in short scopes. `i`, `n`, `ctx`, `df` are the local dialect and everyone\nreads them faster than `current_row_index`. Reach for the long name when the scope is long or the\nmeaning is genuinely non-obvious.\n\nMatch the surrounding file, including conventions this guide dislikes. Consistency with the\ncodebase outranks consistency with this document. Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch,\nand don't fold a style cleanup into a behavior change.\n\nDon't put emoji or checkmarks in console output unless the project already does.\n\n## Tests\n\nTest behavior that matters, not the shape of the implementation. A test file that mirrors the\nsource file function-for-function is usually testing that the code was typed, not that it works.\n\nEvery fixed bug gets the test that would have caught it. That test is worth more than the whole\nhappy-path suite.\n\nDon't assert things that cannot fail. Asserting that a constructor set the field you just passed\nit will never once be red.\n\nThe corpus is one YAML file per case in `tests/corpus/`, holding `program`, an optional `input`,\neither the expected `output` or `refuses: true`, and any extra checks the case asks for -- today\nonly `snapshot`, a list of backends whose emitted code gets pinned as well.\n\n`node_types` is different: a case never writes it, `tests/tag_corpus.rs` does, on every run. It's\nthe AST shapes (`src/tags.rs`, drawn from `tir::Kind`) the program exercises, as dotted paths --\n`arith.add`, `selection.narrow`, `projection` -- and it is what the site's case tree browses by.\n\nEvery case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true`\nmeans they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business. A\nsnapshot is the exception on top, for a claim the output cannot carry. A program that does not\ncompile is not a corpus case: nothing about it differs per backend, so it stays in step_*.rs\nwhere the message is an insta snapshot.\n\nUse block scalars (`|`) so nothing is coerced by YAML's scalar rules. Unknown keys, unknown\nbackend names, and a case with both or neither of `output` and `refuses` are all errors, because\na case that asks for nothing looks exactly like a case that passes.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nSay what you're unsure about and what would settle it. This is only useful if it's selective:\nhedging everything equally carries no information, and it reads as insurance rather than honesty.\n\nNever invent an API signature. If you can't verify a call, say the call is unverified, or check\nit. A plausible-looking wrong argument is the most expensive thing in this document, because it\nsurvives review. It looks exactly like knowledge.\n\nDon't announce compliance. No \"As requested,\" no \"I've carefully ensured,\" no summary of your own\ndiligence at the end of a PR description. Describe the change and why.\n\n## Typography\n\nPlain ASCII everywhere: code, identifiers, commit messages, config, and prose. Straight quotes,\n`--` not an em dash, three periods not a single-character ellipsis, `->` not an arrow glyph. This\nis about grep, diffs, and terminals. Characters a person would not type on a keyboard are noise\nin a git repo. A person's name is whatever they spell it as, and is not subject to this.\n\nIf you find yourself reaching for `--` more than once or twice a page, a run of dashes is a\nparagraph that hasn't picked its sentence boundaries. Use a period, a colon, or parentheses.\n\n## What not to do in the name of this guide\n\nDon't manufacture the appearance of human authorship. Specifically: no deliberate typos, no\ninvented TODO or HACK comments, no fabricated dated notes or initials, no commented-out code that\nwas never live, no artificial inconsistency between files, and no performed uncertainty about\nthings you're actually confident in.\n\nAll of these are false claims about how the artifact came to be, embedded in the artifact. That's\nworse than any stylistic tell, and it's the failure mode this guide is most likely to induce if\nread carelessly.\n\n# Provenance\n\nFour categories, describing where content came from rather than who typed it.\n\n**Human-written.** Copied verbatim from what a human wrote, or edited only superficially:\nformatting, typos, wrapping. The human produced the text itself.\n\n**Human-authored.** The content is the logical interpretation of what a human said. The test: if\nthe human's instruction had been pasted into the file instead of the expanded result, would it\ncarry the same value? If someone with sufficient expertise, human or agent, would derive\nsubstantially this result from that instruction, it is human-authored.\n\n**Anything produced by an exploratory loop counts as human-authored**, where an agent proposes\noptions and a human makes the design decisions. That holds even when large stretches of the\nresulting text were generated verbatim by the agent.\n\n**Derived.** Follows from constraints already present: existing patterns in this repo, a decision\nrecorded earlier, or a cited external source. When the existing constraints leave essentially one\nreasonable option, taking it is derived, not invented. If the constraint itself came from a\nhuman, the result is human-authored instead.\n\n**Agent-invented.** A real decision an agent made on its own. Something is agent-invented only\nwhen all of these hold:\n\n- it did not come out of a human authoring or exploration loop,\n- it was not forced by existing patterns or constraints,\n- it is not obvious, in the sense that there was more than one reasonable option,\n- it did not come from a cited source.\n\nCarve this down to the **minimal surface** that actually meets those tests. A section can be\nhuman-authored overall while one specific choice inside it was purely the agent's call, and that\nchoice is what to name. The purpose is to make visible which decisions no human has actually\nmade, so a reader can tell a requirement from an accident.\n\nConsulting a source does not by itself make a result derived. An agent may research a source and\nthen decide something well beyond it.\n\n## Correcting provenance\n\nProvenance is a fact about how a file came to be, not a property recoverable from the file\nafterwards. It has to be recorded when the commit is made or it is gone.\n\n**Do not classify inherited files confidently.** If a file arrives already written and you do not\nknow its authoring history, the honest entry is that the history is unknown. Nothing in a\nfinished artifact distinguishes text a human wrote from text an agent wrote, and guessing\nproduces a false record that looks like a true one.\n\n**If you get provenance wrong, do not rewrite history.** Add a follow-up commit that names the\nbad commit and states the correction. Both entries stay in the log. A rewritten history is a\nworse record than a corrected one, because it hides that the mistake was possible.\n\n## Authority ordering\n\nThis is the practical reason the categories exist. When two parts of the repository conflict,\nhuman-written and human-authored content wins, derived content yields to it, and agent-invented\ncontent has the least authority: a placeholder that survived, not a decision.\n\nIf you are changing something human-authored because agent-invented content elsewhere assumed\notherwise, you have it backwards. Change the invented part, or raise the conflict.\n\n# Committing\n\nAgents may commit in this repository, and must add the `Co-Authored-By` trailer when they do.\n\nThe subject line says what changed. The body says why, or what was ruled out, plus the provenance\nlines that apply. Omit categories that are empty rather than writing \"none\". A bulleted inventory\nof every file touched duplicates the diff.\n\n```\nAdd cardinality types to the design draft\n\nHuman-authored: the Vec/Stream split and the indexing-promise distinction\nDerived: jq operator semantics (jq 1.7 manual)\nAgent-invented: the `fold` block syntax\n\nCo-Authored-By: <agent identifier>\n```\n\nA commit with no agent `Co-Authored-By` trailer is entirely human-authored and human-written.\nThat is the default assumption, so never add the trailer to a commit you did not contribute to.\n\n## Citing external material\n\nWhen content draws on an external source, name it, in the commit message and in the file itself\nwhere a reader would want it. \"Derived from the jq manual\" is enough; a link is better.\n\nThis matters most where the language deliberately borrows from an existing one. A reader should\nbe able to tell \"jq does it this way\" from \"we decided this.\"\n\n# Limits\n\n## Stop and ask\n\nFlag these to a human and wait for explicit authorization. Flag before writing to files, not\nafter:\n\n- Licensing or copyright, where the license is unclear, restrictive, or unknown\n- Security or access: credentials, keys, attack surface, or permissions you were not clearly\n  granted\n- Personal information, meaning anything identifying a person, from any source\n- Anything else you would want a human to weigh\n\nExplain the specific concern rather than asking generically, and do not proceed on silence.\n\n## Never include\n\nCopyrighted material from other repositories. Reference and describe freely; do not copy source\ntext. Concepts and documented behavior are fine to describe in your own words.\n\nPersonal information or secrets about anyone, the repository owner included: names, contact\ndetails, credentials, private paths, internal hostnames.\n\nPaths or details from unrelated private repositories or systems. Design rationale should stand on\nits own without them.\n\n---\n\nDelete anything here you disagree with. Every rule earns its place by making the repository more\nuseful to the next person who opens it, and if a rule stops doing that it has no other argument\nin its favor. A guide nobody follows costs more than no guide, because it turns every review into\nan argument about the document instead of the code.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":281,"totalLines":281,"truncated":false}},"title":"AGENTS.md","time":{"start":1788296877676,"end":1788296877685}},"id":"prt_05eccd07e001d1R9dyLcbUFDvS","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05eccc780001HIvFIZUfjh8hMg"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788296877700,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05eccd680001J7g2r6gPGPkcCv","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","messageID":"msg_05eccc780001HIvFIZUfjh8hMg","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":18085,"input":17943,"output":126,"reasoning":16,"cache":{"write":0,"read":0}},"cost":0.001191855}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788296878369,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05eccd9190010HtwC3M6jtivTE","messageID":"msg_05eccd68c001j1y6ttbkoEow42","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296879366,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-976e976290bb8aa9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/questions.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Open questions\n2: \n3: The design question tracker. It lived in draft.md until the [draft-split\n4: plan](draft-split.md) moved it out; the numbering is unchanged, so Q17 still means what it\n5: meant wherever an ADR, a research-log note, or a plan cited it.\n6: \n7: Status is one of OPEN (no preferred answer), LEANING (a preferred answer exists but is not\n8: committed), BLOCKED (waits on another question), or SETTLED (answered, and the answer is\n9: written down somewhere a reader can reach from here). Add new ones at the bottom and keep the\n10: numbers stable, since other documents cite them.\n11: \n12: Settled questions stay in the table. A tracker that only lists what is unresolved cannot be\n13: checked for completeness, and the settled entries are what stop a decision being relitigated.\n14: What a settled entry stops carrying is the argument: once an ADR or a decision section exists\n15: to point at, the detail collapses to a line and a link, and that record answers the question\n16: from then on. Where no such record exists yet -- Q20 is the only one today -- the entry keeps\n17: its detail, because collapsing it would delete the only copy.\n18: \n19: | # | Question | Status |\n20: |---|---|---|\n21: | [Q1](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) | Streams: first-class values, or evaluation-level multiplicity? | SETTLED, evaluation-level and typed: `Stream<T>` is the effect layer's type, not a value type |\n22: | [Q2](#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit) | Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit? | OPEN |\n23: | [Q3](#q3-what-symbol-replaces--for-the-record-forming-update) | What symbol replaces `=` for the record-forming update? | LEANING, blocked on Q2 |\n24: | [Q4](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) | Can the type express ordering over heterogeneous streams? | OPEN, but the shape is decided (ADR 0008: Kleene patterns in effect position); enums now supply tagged alternation, leaving the matcher surface and spelling |\n25: | [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends) | Stream-lowering strategy across the three backends | OPEN in general; all seven backends stream the fused pipeline shape, so only lowering beyond that shape remains |\n26: | [Q6](#q6-does-a-reconciler-belong-in-the-language-or-a-library) | Does a reconciler belong in the language or a library? | OPEN |\n27: | [Q7](#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes) | Does `..` promise depth-first order, or only the set of nodes? | OPEN |\n28: | [Q8](#q8-is-vectorizability-visible-in-the-type-system-or-a-silent-optimization) | Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization? | OPEN |\n29: | [Q9](#q9-are-vectors-multidimensional-with--as-projection) | Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection? | OPEN, may merge with Q2 |\n30: | [Q10](#q10-is-uniqueness-analysis-in-scope-for-deciding-when-a-lens-materializes) | Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes? | LEANING yes, compiler-internal: see the privileged-references sketch |\n31: | [Q11](#q11-how-does-the-querytransformation-split-manifest-in-the-type-system) | How does the query/transformation split manifest in the type system? | SETTLED |\n32: | [Q12](#q12-on-a-type-mismatch-does-field-access-error-yield-null-or-something-third) | On a type mismatch, does field access error, yield null, or something third? | SETTLED |\n33: | [Q13](#q13-does-the-layer-shift-run-only-one-way-with-no-value-to-effect-operator) | Does the layer shift run only one way, with no value-to-effect operator? | LEANING yes, and now load-bearing: born-at-sources, dies-at-exits is the `Stream<T>` typing rule |\n34: | [Q14](#q14-does-select-return-a-masked-view-a-selection-vector-or-a-copy) | Does `select` return a masked view, a selection vector, or a copy? | OPEN |\n35: | [Q15](#q15-backend-llvm-via-inkwell-cranelift-or-both) | Backend: LLVM via inkwell, Cranelift, or both? | SETTLED, LLVM via inkwell, built and running |\n36: | [Q16](#q16-string-representation-given-wtf-16-on-the-js-target) | String representation, given WTF-16 on the JS target | OPEN, decides the string API permanently |\n37: | [Q17](#q17-is-there-a-dense-tensor-type-constructed-explicitly) | Is there a dense tensor type, constructed explicitly? | LEANING yes |\n38: | [Q18](#q18-does--on-a-rank-2-tensor-yield-rows-or-scalars) | Does `.[]` on a rank-2 tensor yield rows or scalars? | LEANING rows |\n39: | [Q19](#q19-how-are-nulls-carried-in-a-dense-typed-buffer) | How are nulls carried in a dense typed buffer? | LEANING, Arrow validity bitmask |\n40: | [Q20](#q20-how-are-blocking-operators-sort-group_by-joins-classified) | How are blocking operators (`sort`, `group_by`, joins) classified? | SETTLED, a trait with no lawful stream instance |\n41: | [Q21](#q21-what-guarantees-batch-size-is-unobservable-over-a-batched-stream) | What guarantees batch size is unobservable over a batched stream? | LEANING, the trait law that ops commute with reification |\n42: | [Q22](#q22-are-dense-and-masked-vectors-distinguishable-in-the-type) | Are dense and masked vectors distinguishable in the type? | OPEN, Q14 from the other side |\n43: | [Q23](#q23-what-primitive-set-is-the-standard-library-defined-over) | What primitive set is the standard library defined over? | LEANING, the parallel basis |\n44: | [Q24](#q24-are-compile-time-macros-a-first-class-concept) | Are compile-time macros a first-class concept? | OPEN, not yet evaluated |\n45: | [Q25](#q25-does-the-language-have-union-types) | Does the language have union types? | PARTLY SETTLED: closed nominal sums exist (enums); anonymous structural unions remain an absence |\n46: | [Q26](#q26-is-jsxs-children-slot-a-closed-per-site-union-or-an-open-one) | Is JSX's children slot a closed per-site union, or an open one? | OPEN, deliberately deferred to last |\n47: | [Q27](#q27-does-pattern-matching-need-a-separate-matcher-type-distinct-from-result) | Does pattern matching need a separate `Matcher` type, distinct from `Result`? | SETTLED |\n48: | [Q28](#q28-does-deep-matching-need-cross-match-unification-of-logic-variables) | Does deep matching need cross-match unification of logic variables? | OPEN |\n49: | [Q29](#q29-what-is-the-default-discriminant-convention-for-a-derived-enum-codec) | What is the default discriminant convention for a derived enum codec? | SUPERSEDED: the enum decision made the single-key wrapper the value itself, not a codec default |\n50: | [Q30](#q30-do-the-base-functor-generics-double-as-parser-combinators-across-trees-strings-and-streams) | Do the base-functor generics double as parser combinators, across trees, strings, and streams? | LEANING yes, implementation split still open |\n51: | [Q31](#q31-does-a-friendlier-string-pattern-language-belong-in-the-language-and-what-regex-flavor-does-it-extend-to) | Does a friendlier string-pattern language belong in the language, and what regex flavor does it extend to? | OPEN |\n52: | [Q32](#q32-does-the-dimension-model-subsume-the-effect-layer) | Does the dimension model subsume the effect layer? | OPEN, and it may dissolve Q13 rather than answer it |\n53: | [Q33](#q33-does-a-spread-slot-in-a-call-give-partial-application) | Does a spread slot in a call give partial application? | OPEN, and only expressible because arguments are a record |\n54: | [Q34](#q34-do-named-types-exist-and-is-a-name-an-alias-or-an-identity) | Do named types exist, and is a name an alias or an identity? | OPEN for records; enums decided identity for themselves, and enum declarations are the first declaration form |\n55: | [Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) | What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return? | OPEN; `jsonlines` is now a top-level-only sink with no result type, which removes a placeholder answer without deciding the question |\n56: | [Q36](#q36-does-a-real-module-system-need-imports-multiple-files-and-enforced-privacy) | Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy? | OPEN, one always-on prelude file exists; nothing beyond it does |\n57: | [Q37](#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model) | How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model? | RULED (gh:145): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (matches IEEE). Printing format is still open per-backend conformance work; tracked at board row `float-build` |\n58: \n59: [Multidimensional vectors](#q9-are-vectors-multidimensional-with--as-projection) is the one\n60: question still capable of changing [the two-layer\n61: section](../draft.md#the-core-idea-two-layers), now that\n62: [streams](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) are settled, so it\n63: should be resolved before that section is treated as stable.\n64: \n65: ## Question detail\n66: \n67: ### Q1. Streams: first-class values, or evaluation-level multiplicity?\n68: \n69: SETTLED: evaluation-level and typed. `Stream<T>` is the effect layer's type, second-class and\n70: consumed exactly once, not a value type. Recorded in\n71: [ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).\n72: \n73: ### Q2. Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit?\n74: \n75: Cartesian (jq today), zip\n76: (vectorized, with broadcast), or neither by default with explicit `cross` and `zip`?\n77: \n78: Vec concatenation specifically is decided, without touching the rest of this question. Revised\n79: 2026-08-30 (oddities round, kantord/toylang#97): `+` on two `Vec`s of the same element type now\n80: concatenates them -- the add-trait reading -- so `[1, 2] + [3]` is `[1, 2, 3]`, settling this\n81: half of Q2 in favor of concatenation over cartesian or zip. The named builtin this superseded,\n82: `concat(vv: Vec<Vec<T>>) -> Vec<T>`, existed specifically so adding it would not decide this\n83: question (see [named functions kept an open question\n84: open](../research-log/named-functions-kept-an-open-question-open.md)); it survives under the name\n85: `flatten` for the case `+` cannot cover, an outer `Vec` whose length is not known at the call\n86: site. The general question -- what any *other* operator means when both operands are Vecs --\n87: is still open.\n88: \n89: Composite equality is settled without touching it. `==` on a record or an enum compares\n90: structurally, and is refused outright when the type carries a Vec anywhere inside it, so a\n91: `Vec`-typed record field never quietly acquires whole-value semantics\n92: ([the equality decision](../draft.md#decided-equality-on-a-composite-is-structural-and-stops-at-a-vec)).\n93: \n94: ### Q3. What symbol replaces `=` for the record-forming update?\n95: \n96: ### Q4. Can the type express ordering over heterogeneous streams?\n97: \n98: Subsumes the older cardinality-versus-\n99: order thread, which asked whether the type system should track *how many* values an\n100: expression produces or *in what order* the kinds arrive. Those turned out to be the same\n101: question asked from two sides, so they are tracked here as one. The cardinality half is the\n102: cheaper and more decidable option, and it catches the failure that actually bites, which is\n103: multiplicity leaking into a position wanting exactly one value. The order half is what the\n104: rest of this entry is about.\n105: If a stream is \"some `A`s, then some `B`s\", can the type say so? One approach is *regular\n106: expressions over types*, the same\n107: algebra as string regexes but with types as the alphabet, so a pattern denotes a set of\n108: permitted value-sequences: `Seq<A,B>` = `A* B*`, `Alt<A,B>` = `(A|B)*`, `Star<A>` = `A*`.\n109: Three primitives suffice (Kleene's theorem), it is decidable, and unlike full session types\n110: it needs no *linear types*, a discipline requiring each value be consumed exactly once,\n111: which is powerful but infects the whole system. Unpacking one item is then the **derivative**\n112: of the pattern: given that an `A` was just consumed, what remains? Open: how type tagging is\n113: represented so the runtime and type-level guarantees stay symmetrical.\n114: \n115: An exploration after [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed)\n116: committed to this shape without settling the open parts; it is recorded as\n117: [ADR 0008](../docs/adr/0008-stream-protocols-are-kleene-patterns.md). The load-bearing findings:\n118: the linearity objection above is obsolete, since the streams decision introduced exactly-once\n119: consumption scoped to one second-class type, which is all a protocol type needs; `Opt`, `Vec`,\n120: and `Stream` are already this algebra (`?` and `*` on the value side, `*` on the effect side),\n121: so the pattern constructors extend the cardinality table rather than joining it; the empty\n122: pattern is `Seq`'s unit, which makes a payload-free end cost nothing and collapses any\n123: \"stream plus end slot\" primitive back into `Star<T>`; a closing message is `Seq<Star<T>, E>`\n124: and several ways to end is `Alt` in terminal position, which types mid-stream failure and\n125: makes errors structurally terminal. The soundness condition that keeps all of it second-class:\n126: a `Stream` never appears under a value constructor, and may appear freely under pattern\n127: constructors. Still blocking: union types ([Q25](#q25-does-the-language-have-union-types)),\n128: discriminants ([Q29](#q29-what-is-the-default-discriminant-convention-for-a-derived-enum-codec)),\n129: the matcher surface ([Q27](#q27-does-pattern-matching-need-a-separate-matcher-type-distinct-from-result)),\n130: and the spelling question (patterns inside the constructor versus outer combinators).\n131: \n132: ### Q5. Stream-lowering strategy across the three backends\n133: \n134: Lua has true coroutines, JavaScript has generators, native\n135: has neither for free. Previously recorded as needing to be decided before any backend is\n136: written, which turned out to be false: three backends exist without it, because nothing in\n137: them streams. Then recorded as blocking any backend that *streams*, which the fused\n138: `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop showed is also false: all seven backends now stream that pipeline\n139: shape as a plain read/transform/write loop, no coroutines or generators involved, because a\n140: straight-line pipeline never needs to suspend. What the question still covers is lowering\n141: beyond that shape -- a stream consumed by something that is not the tail of its own loop --\n142: where the coroutine/generator/state-machine choice becomes real.\n143: \n144: ### Q6. Does a reconciler belong in the language or a library?\n145: \n146: ### Q7. Does `..` promise depth-first order, or only the set of nodes?\n147: \n148: On a flat columnar layout,\n149: \"every node at every depth\" is \"every element of every buffer\", which is embarrassingly\n150: parallel. The dependent part is not the traversal but the *order*, since the flat layout is\n151: not in depth-first order. jq promises the order. If this language only promises the set,\n152: recursive descent becomes one of the cheapest operators rather than one of the most\n153: expensive. This is not only a performance question: a jq-derived language that is fast\n154: everywhere except recursive descent has a positioning problem, because `..` is one of the two\n155: things people reach for jq to do.\n156: \n157: ### Q8. Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization?\n158: \n159: Reporting it\n160: means a second effect alongside cardinality, and a visible fast-path/slow-path distinction in\n161: signatures. Hiding it makes performance unpredictable in exactly the way this design is\n162: trying to avoid. Note the two effects are orthogonal: `select` changes cardinality and\n163: vectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way and cannot\n164: vectorize at all.\n165: \n166: ### Q9. Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection?\n167: \n168: See the TODO and response in the\n169: cardinality section. Unifies indexing with iteration, but disturbs the claim that there are\n170: exactly two layer shifters, and per-dimension cardinality only describes rectangular data\n171: while JSON is ragged.\n172: \n173: ### Q10. Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes?\n174: \n175: Deciding when a projection lens can materialize instead\n176:  of staying a view requires knowing no other reference to the source survives. That is\n177:  linearity or uniqueness typing, the machinery deliberately avoided in [the ordering question](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams).\n178: \n179: ### Q11. How does the query/transformation split manifest in the type system?\n180: \n181: SETTLED: it does not need to. `map` and `select` are the same operation with the multiplicity\n182: stored in different places, so the split never reaches the type system; see [the two-layer\n183: section](../draft.md#the-core-idea-two-layers).\n184: \n185: ### Q12. On a type mismatch, does field access error, yield null, or something third?\n186: \n187: SETTLED: something third. Field access desugars to a lens with three distinguishable outcomes\n188: -- a value, a specific absence, and a specific error; see [the field-access\n189: section](../draft.md#field-access-is-a-lens).\n190: \n191: ### Q13. Does the layer shift run only one way, with no value-to-effect operator?\n192: \n193: If effect multiplicity is born only from\n194:  streaming sources and dies only into values through `[...]`, then no value-to-effect\n195:  operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys nothing. LEANING\n196:  toward yes. This decides [the streams question](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) with it, since the only thing that would break it is a value\n197:  with genuinely unknown extent, which is what a first-class stream value would be. The streams\n198:  question is now settled the compatible way, and this lifecycle -- born at `inputs`/`lines`,\n199:  dead at `collect` or a sink -- became the `Stream<T>` typing rule, so reversing this lean now\n200:  means amending that decision too.\n201: \n202: ### Q14. Does `select` return a masked view, a selection vector, or a copy?\n203: \n204: See the section on\n205:  whether a value-layer `select` copies. A bitmask breaks `Vec`'s constant-time indexing\n206:  promise, a selection vector keeps it and pays memory per survivor, and either view pins its\n207:  whole source buffer alive.\n208: \n209: ### Q15. Backend: LLVM via inkwell, Cranelift, or both?\n210: \n211: SETTLED: LLVM via inkwell, built and running. Recorded in\n212: [ADR 0005](../docs/adr/0005-llvm-via-inkwell-for-the-native-backend.md).\n213: \n214: ### Q16. String representation, given WTF-16 on the JS target\n215: \n216: The three\n217:  options are WTF-16 everywhere, UTF-8 everywhere with the JavaScript-shaped API emulated, or\n218:  designing the difference away by never exposing code-unit indexing or length. Only the third\n219:  is cheap on both sides. It has to be decided early because it constrains the string API\n220:  permanently.\n221: \n222: ### Q17. Is there a dense tensor type, constructed explicitly?\n223: \n224: `@f32` as a narrowing constructor\n225:  that hard-fails rather than an inference, with `reshape` attaching shape. It is also the\n226:  second number type, a deliberate lossy exit from the `f64` commitment.\n227: \n228: ### Q18. Does `.[]` on a rank-2 tensor yield rows or scalars?\n229: \n230: NumPy and APL both yield rows,\n231:  which makes `map` rank-polymorphic and gives row sums as `map(fold(add; 0))` with no new\n232:  syntax. Then rank-1 yields scalars and full linearization needs a separate flattening view.\n233: \n234: ### Q19. How are nulls carried in a dense typed buffer?\n235: \n236: JSON has null and an `f32` buffer does\n237:  not. NaN as a sentinel collides with genuine NaN. Arrow's separate validity bitmask solves\n238:  it and brings zero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB and pandas.\n239: \n240: ### Q20. How are blocking operators (`sort`, `group_by`, joins) classified?\n241: \n242: `sort`, `group_by` and joins are one value in and\n243:  one value out, so the per-element cardinality mapping does not describe them. They need the\n244:  whole input before producing anything and are parallelizable by other means. The\n245:  kernel-admissibility result covers elementwise filters only, and this is the gap it leaves.\n246: \n247: ### Q21. What guarantees batch size is unobservable over a batched stream?\n248: \n249: Argued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from) rather than\n250: here, since it arrived with that material. The leaning is the trait law that operations commute\n251: with reification, which is what makes a batch boundary invisible to a program.\n252: \n253: ### Q22. Are dense and masked vectors distinguishable in the type?\n254: \n255: Argued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), where it\n256: appears as the observation that a masked view and a dense buffer have different launch\n257: preconditions. The same question as [what select returns](#q14-does-select-return-a-masked-view-a-selection-vector-or-a-copy), approached from\n258: the layout side rather than the operator side.\n259: \n260: ### Q23. What primitive set is the standard library defined over?\n261: \n262: Argued in [the primitive set cannot be fold and recursion](../draft.md#the-primitive-set-cannot-be-fold-and-recursion). The leaning is the\n263: parallel basis, with `fold` and general recursion available but not the thing everything else\n264: is defined over.\n265: ### Q24. Are compile-time macros a first-class concept?\n266: \n267: A macro would be a function that runs at compile time and transforms the compiler's own\n268: representation of a program, which means that representation has to be a type the language\n269: defines rather than an implementation detail the compiler happens to have. Fully compile-time, as\n270: in Rust, with no runtime field.\n271: \n272: The syntax idea is decorator-style, as in Python, where the same notation can attach either an\n273: ordinary closure or a macro. That the two look alike is the point worth checking: it is either\n274: the feature's main convenience or its main trap, since one runs when the program runs and the\n275: other runs while it is being compiled, and the principle about writing crossings down applies to\n276: that boundary too.\n277: \n278: Not evaluated. Recorded so it is not rediscovered.\n279: \n280: ### Q25. Does the language have union types?\n281: \n282: There is no sum type. `Alt<A,B>` appears only inside the regular-expressions-over-types sketch for\n283: stream ordering, and `Json` stands in wherever a value might be several things, which makes it\n284: the permissive escape hatch principle 1 says it should not be.\n285: \n286: The gap surfaced from asking what `.[]` on a heterogeneous record would even produce. With a union\n287: it is `Str | Int`; without one there is no answer. That question is settled on other grounds, but\n288: the absence it exposed is not, and heterogeneous data is not a corner of a data language.\n289: \n290: Related: an alternation over types is also what the ordering question needs, so these may be one\n291: piece of machinery rather than two.\n292: \n293: Partly settled by [the enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native): closed nominal\n294: sums now exist, and they serve both this question's motivating case (heterogeneous data) and the\n295: ordering question's `Alt` (a stream of several message kinds is `Stream<SomeEnum>`). What\n296: remains absent is the anonymous structural union, `Str | Int` with no declaration -- a\n297: different feature with a different justification, still an absence rather than a decision.\n298: \n299: ### Q26. Is JSX's children slot a closed per-site union, or an open one?\n300: \n301: Sketch: a creator function taking a `Record` of strictly-typed attrs (this is just `Field`\n302: in the existing sense, no new machinery) plus a `Dimension` of children. The children slot needs\n303: an element type, and that is where the interesting question lives.\n304: \n305: React's `ReactNode` is open: any function shaped like a creator function is accepted, unconstrained\n306: at the definition site. That is the same escape hatch [Q25](#q25-does-the-language-have-union-types)\n307: already names and principle 1 rejects -- an unconstrained union is `Json` with a different label.\n308: \n309: The alternative is closed and inferred per call site: each JSX expression's children type is\n310: `Alt<T1, T2, ...>` built from whatever is literally nested there, checkable and exhaustive, with\n311: no shared vocabulary required across call sites. The cost is fragmentation: a function written to\n312: accept \"a list of children\" can only accept the exact union inferred at its own call site, not\n313: children built elsewhere out of a different but compatible set of node kinds. Parametric\n314: polymorphism over the union narrows that gap but does not close it.\n315: \n316: Deliberately last: the right answer depends on the rest of the design (how this interacts with\n317: [Q24](#q24-are-compile-time-macros-a-first-class-concept), and how JSX trees actually get passed\n318: between functions in practice), not on this slot in isolation.\n319: \n320: ### Q27. Does pattern matching need a separate `Matcher` type, distinct from `Result`?\n321: \n322: SETTLED yes: matchers are first-class, tagged, and or-composable, derived per type under the\n323: capital name (kantord/toylang#47). See [the enum decision's construction and naming\n324: section](../draft.md#construction-and-naming) and\n325: [guides/matching.md](../docs/guides/matching.md).\n326: \n327: ### Q28. Does deep matching need cross-match unification of logic variables?\n328: \n329: OPEN. `..` composed with a matcher already finds a shape anywhere in a tree without naming its\n330: path, and `as` already binds one submatch to a name for reuse within the same arm. Neither needs\n331: unification. What would: finding a node `A` and a separate node `B` elsewhere such that `B`\n332: refers to `A`, which is full Prolog-style unification with backtracking over bindings, not a\n333: bigger version of `as`. See [Pattern matching is decoding](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding).\n334: \n335: ### Q29. What is the default discriminant convention for a derived enum codec?\n336: \n337: SUPERSEDED: there is no derived codec picking a representation, because the representation\n338: *is* the value. [ADR 0009](../docs/adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)\n339: records the decision, and why the tag-field and shape-matched alternatives lost.\n340: \n341: ### Q30. Do the base-functor generics double as parser combinators, across trees, strings, and streams?\n342: \n343: LEANING yes. `Seq`, `Alt`, `Star`, and `Opt` are already in the document as [the regex-over-types algebra](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams)\n344: and as the shape [Pattern matching is decoding](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding) builds `Matcher<T>` from; naming them as parser\n345: combinators only makes the precedent explicit (Hutton and Meijer; Wadler; parsing with\n346: derivatives). OPEN: whether this is one trait with implementations that differ by receiver (a\n347: parsed tree needs no backtracking, a string needs an actual parsing engine), the same shape as\n348: [`Field<K>`](../draft.md#field-access-is-a-lens), and if so what law the implementations have to share. See\n349: [One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams](../draft.md#one-combinator-algebra-for-trees-strings-and-streams).\n350: \n351: ### Q31. Does a friendlier string-pattern language belong in the language, and what regex flavor does it extend to?\n352: \n353: OPEN. A URL-route-style syntax with named, typed captures composing through the existing\n354: `int(.)`-style codec syntax is one candidate, with Swift's `Regex` builder and route-pattern DSLs\n355: such as Express's `path-to-regexp` as the closest prior art. [The arm-list's `//` semantics](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding) already\n356: commit any such language to ordered, PEG-style choice, which is compatible with PCRE/Perl-style\n357: regex and not with POSIX leftmost-longest regex, so \"extends to regular expressions\" needs to\n358: name which flavor. See\n359: [One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams](../draft.md#one-combinator-algebra-for-trees-strings-and-streams).\n360: \n361: ### Q32. Does the dimension model subsume the effect layer?\n362: \n363: The two-layer section says multiplicity lives either in a value or in evaluation, and\n364: [the one-way shift](../draft.md#proposal-the-layer-shift-only-runs-one-way) narrows that to\n365: effect multiplicity being born from streaming input and never from a value. The dimension\n366: proposal says something that may be the same thing in different words: a value has an ordered\n367: list of dimensions, and a spec says what happens to each.\n368: \n369: Put them together and a `Stream` looks like a value with a dimension whose extent is not known\n370: yet. The spec vocabulary already covers it without a second layer: keep and narrow are\n371: streamable, since neither has to consume anything to know what it did, and collapse is not. That\n372: distinction is written down in the dimension proposal and it is exactly the `Vec` and `Stream`\n373: difference.\n374: \n375: If that holds, there is one layer with a refinement rather than two layers, and the question\n376: stops being which direction the shift runs and becomes whether anything shifts at all.\n377: \n378: Three things would follow, and they are what makes this worth settling rather than leaving\n379: implicit:\n380: \n381: `map` stays primitive rather than becoming sugar for `[ .[] | f ]` later, since the thing it\n382: would be sugar for never comes back.\n383: \n384: `.[]` stays inert on a `Vec` permanently. Keeping every index of a known extent changes nothing,\n385: and no future feature makes it change something.\n386: \n387: The two-layer framing that opens this document becomes a description of a special case rather\n388: than the organising idea.\n389: \n390: Not proposed, because the two-layer section is load-bearing and this has not been worked through\n391: against an actual streaming input. What prompted it: prototype 1 has no effect layer, and that\n392: is not a departure from the design but what the design predicts for a program that reads one\n393: whole value and hands one back. Whether the layer returns with streaming, or whether streaming\n394: turns out to be a dimension, is the open part.\n395: \n396: ### Q33. Does a spread slot in a call give partial application?\n397: \n398: Functions are unary and several arguments travel as one record, which makes a question available\n399: that a positional language would have to answer with arity counting. If a call may leave a slot\n400: open -- spelled `...` for now -- what comes back is a function expecting the fields that were\n401: not supplied:\n402: \n403: ```\n404: join {with: \", \", ...}\n405: ```\n406: \n407: `join` takes `{over: Vec<Str>, with: Str}`, so supplying `with` leaves a function from\n408: `{over: Vec<Str>}` to `Str`. The remaining parameter is **the complement of what was given**,\n409: computed structurally rather than by position, so there is no question of which argument was\n410: skipped and no need for placeholders in the other slots.\n411: \n412: What makes this worth recording rather than dismissing is that it is not a feature bolted onto\n413: the call syntax; it falls out of arguments already being a record. Partial application in a\n414: positional language has to invent a convention for \"this one, not that one\". Here the convention\n415: is subtraction on field names, which the type system already does.\n416: \n417: Open, and roughly in dependency order:\n418: \n419: - **Does it need first-class functions?** The language has none: `Sig` is a parameter and a\n420:   result, and there is no function type in the type grammar. A partial application evaluates to a\n421:   function, so it needs one to have a type. That is the real cost, and it is much larger than the\n422:   syntax.\n423: - **What does the residual type look like?** `{over: Vec<Str>} -> Str` needs an arrow in the type\n424:   grammar, which is the same thing the previous point asks for.\n425: - **Is `...` the right spelling?** It reads as \"and the rest\", which is right, but the token is\n426:   unused and could go to a spread that *supplies* fields instead -- `{...defaults, n: 1}` --\n427:   and those two meanings would collide.\n428: - **Does supplying nothing mean anything?** `join {...}` would be `join` itself, which is either\n429:   a harmless identity or a sign the spelling proves too much.\n430: - **Does it interact with the dimension model at all?** A record literal does not distribute, so\n431:   presumably not, but partial application inside `map` is exactly where it would be used most.\n432: \n433: Blocked on first-class functions, which nothing else currently needs.\n434: \n435: ### Q34. Do named types exist, and is a name an alias or an identity?\n436: \n437: Deferred when record literals were settled, on the grounds that a literal synthesises its own\n438: type and so forecloses nothing. What has since turned up is that the *constructor* for a named\n439: type already works, which changes what the question costs without changing what it asks.\n440: \n441: A constructor is a unary function from the structural record to the named type, and that shape\n442: exists today:\n443: \n444: ```\n445: fn User(c: {name: Str, age: Int}) -> {name: Str, age: Int} = c\n446: \n447: User {name: \"ada\", age: 36}\n448: ```\n449: \n450: `User {...}` parses and reaches the checker, which rejects it only because no function of that\n451: name is defined. Type names and expression names already resolve by separate paths, so there is\n452: no ambiguity to invent a rule for.\n453: \n454: Free, then: the spelling, and the semantics, since a constructor being a unary function over a\n455: record is the same decision already made for arguments generally.\n456: \n457: Not free, in rough order of how much they decide:\n458: \n459: - **Destructuring.** Does `.name` on a `User` see through the name, or does getting a field\n460:   out need an explicit step? Nothing about this direction falls out, and it is what decides\n461:   whether an identity is pleasant or a tax.\n462: - **The declaration form.** `Type::from_name` knows three names and there is no `type X = ...`.\n463: - **One namespace or two.** The checker looks a call up in `sigs`, so a type declaration\n464:   introducing a constructor would put type names and function names in one namespace. That is\n465:   probably right and should be chosen rather than arrived at.\n466: - **Alias or identity.** The question proper, and the only part the above does not touch. An\n467:   alias abbreviates a type the tutorial's annotation shows is worth abbreviating; an identity\n468:   makes two same-shaped records refuse to interchange, which is a different feature with a\n469:   different justification.\n470: \n471: Worth being explicit that cheapness is not an argument. What is recorded here is that the cost of\n472: identity is lower than it looked, not that the language wants it.\n473: \n474: [The enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native) has since answered every bullet for\n475: enums specifically: they are identities (exhaustiveness requires it), `enum` is the declaration\n476: form, and variant constructors land in the value namespace with bare-until-ambiguous\n477: resolution. Records are deliberately not carried along; the alias-or-identity question stays\n478: open for them, and this entry now tracks only that half.\n479: \n480: ### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n481: \n482: This document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\n483: transforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\n484: here rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\n485: far has been about values coming *in*.\n486: \n487: What exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\n488: type-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\n489: no side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n490: \n491: What it does not answer:\n492: \n493: - **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n494:   keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n495:   Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n496:   the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n497: - **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n498:   agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n499:   language means six backends must agree on it.\n500: - **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\n501:   to, which means the printer is the only specification of the format.\n502: - **Does a stream of outputs exist at all**, or does a program produce one value whose rendering\n503:   happens to be long? jq answers the first; the design so far assumes the second without saying\n504:   so.\n505: \n506: Blocked on the same thing as [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends): a\n507: program that writes as it goes is a program with an effect layer.\n508: \n509: The fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend\n510: level, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect\n511: layer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than\n512: answered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink,\n513: legal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type. That removes the old\n514: placeholder (`jsonlines(...) : Str`, a type claiming the whole output exists as one value)\n515: without deciding whether stdout is a value, an effect, or something a program returns into.\n516: Everything in the bullet list above remains open.\n517: \n518: ### Q36. Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy?\n519: \n520: One file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\n521: program receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\n522: all of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\n523: file to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\n524: not \"private to the prelude,\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\n525: calling a private helper.\n526: \n527: None of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\n528: written codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\n529: exactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\n530: internal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n531: \n532: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n533: \n534: The representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\n535: JavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\n536: agreement harness, which checks bytes.\n537: \n538: - **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n539:   do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n540:   points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n541:   ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n542:   this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n543:   [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n544: - **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n545:   are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n546:   something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n547:   admits values its own output cannot carry.\n548: - **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n549:   IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n550:   behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n551: \n552: None of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\n553: way `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.\n\n(End of file - total 553 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Open questions\n\nThe design question tracker. It lived in draft.md until the [draft-split\nplan](draft-split.md) moved it out; the numbering is unchanged, so Q17 still means what it\nmeant wherever an ADR, a research-log note, or a plan cited it.\n\nStatus is one of OPEN (no preferred answer), LEANING (a preferred answer exists but is not\ncommitted), BLOCKED (waits on another question), or SETTLED (answered, and the answer is\nwritten down somewhere a reader can reach from here). Add new ones at the bottom and keep the\nnumbers stable, since other documents cite them.\n\nSettled questions stay in the table. A tracker that only lists what is unresolved cannot be\nchecked for completeness, and the settled entries are what stop a decision being relitigated.\nWhat a settled entry stops carrying is the argument: once an ADR or a decision section exists\nto point at, the detail collapses to a line and a link, and that record answers the question\nfrom then on. Where no such record exists yet -- Q20 is the only one today -- the entry keeps\nits detail, because collapsing it would delete the only copy.\n\n| # | Question | Status |\n|---|---|---|","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/questions.md","text":"# Open questions\n\nThe design question tracker. It lived in draft.md until the [draft-split\nplan](draft-split.md) moved it out; the numbering is unchanged, so Q17 still means what it\nmeant wherever an ADR, a research-log note, or a plan cited it.\n\nStatus is one of OPEN (no preferred answer), LEANING (a preferred answer exists but is not\ncommitted), BLOCKED (waits on another question), or SETTLED (answered, and the answer is\nwritten down somewhere a reader can reach from here). Add new ones at the bottom and keep the\nnumbers stable, since other documents cite them.\n\nSettled questions stay in the table. A tracker that only lists what is unresolved cannot be\nchecked for completeness, and the settled entries are what stop a decision being relitigated.\nWhat a settled entry stops carrying is the argument: once an ADR or a decision section exists\nto point at, the detail collapses to a line and a link, and that record answers the question\nfrom then on. Where no such record exists yet -- Q20 is the only one today -- the entry keeps\nits detail, because collapsing it would delete the only copy.\n\n| # | Question | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Q1](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) | Streams: first-class values, or evaluation-level multiplicity? | SETTLED, evaluation-level and typed: `Stream<T>` is the effect layer's type, not a value type |\n| [Q2](#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit) | Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit? | OPEN |\n| [Q3](#q3-what-symbol-replaces--for-the-record-forming-update) | What symbol replaces `=` for the record-forming update? | LEANING, blocked on Q2 |\n| [Q4](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) | Can the type express ordering over heterogeneous streams? | OPEN, but the shape is decided (ADR 0008: Kleene patterns in effect position); enums now supply tagged alternation, leaving the matcher surface and spelling |\n| [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends) | Stream-lowering strategy across the three backends | OPEN in general; all seven backends stream the fused pipeline shape, so only lowering beyond that shape remains |\n| [Q6](#q6-does-a-reconciler-belong-in-the-language-or-a-library) | Does a reconciler belong in the language or a library? | OPEN |\n| [Q7](#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes) | Does `..` promise depth-first order, or only the set of nodes? | OPEN |\n| [Q8](#q8-is-vectorizability-visible-in-the-type-system-or-a-silent-optimization) | Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization? | OPEN |\n| [Q9](#q9-are-vectors-multidimensional-with--as-projection) | Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection? | OPEN, may merge with Q2 |\n| [Q10](#q10-is-uniqueness-analysis-in-scope-for-deciding-when-a-lens-materializes) | Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes? | LEANING yes, compiler-internal: see the privileged-references sketch |\n| [Q11](#q11-how-does-the-querytransformation-split-manifest-in-the-type-system) | How does the query/transformation split manifest in the type system? | SETTLED |\n| [Q12](#q12-on-a-type-mismatch-does-field-access-error-yield-null-or-something-third) | On a type mismatch, does field access error, yield null, or something third? | SETTLED |\n| [Q13](#q13-does-the-layer-shift-run-only-one-way-with-no-value-to-effect-operator) | Does the layer shift run only one way, with no value-to-effect operator? | LEANING yes, and now load-bearing: born-at-sources, dies-at-exits is the `Stream<T>` typing rule |\n| [Q14](#q14-does-select-return-a-masked-view-a-selection-vector-or-a-copy) | Does `select` return a masked view, a selection vector, or a copy? | OPEN |\n| [Q15](#q15-backend-llvm-via-inkwell-cranelift-or-both) | Backend: LLVM via inkwell, Cranelift, or both? | SETTLED, LLVM via inkwell, built and running |\n| [Q16](#q16-string-representation-given-wtf-16-on-the-js-target) | String representation, given WTF-16 on the JS target | OPEN, decides the string API permanently |\n| [Q17](#q17-is-there-a-dense-tensor-type-constructed-explicitly) | Is there a dense tensor type, constructed explicitly? | LEANING yes |\n| [Q18](#q18-does--on-a-rank-2-tensor-yield-rows-or-scalars) | Does `.[]` on a rank-2 tensor yield rows or scalars? | LEANING rows |\n| [Q19](#q19-how-are-nulls-carried-in-a-dense-typed-buffer) | How are nulls carried in a dense typed buffer? | LEANING, Arrow validity bitmask |\n| [Q20](#q20-how-are-blocking-operators-sort-group_by-joins-classified) | How are blocking operators (`sort`, `group_by`, joins) classified? | SETTLED, a trait with no lawful stream instance |\n| [Q21](#q21-what-guarantees-batch-size-is-unobservable-over-a-batched-stream) | What guarantees batch size is unobservable over a batched stream? | LEANING, the trait law that ops commute with reification |\n| [Q22](#q22-are-dense-and-masked-vectors-distinguishable-in-the-type) | Are dense and masked vectors distinguishable in the type? | OPEN, Q14 from the other side |\n| [Q23](#q23-what-primitive-set-is-the-standard-library-defined-over) | What primitive set is the standard library defined over? | LEANING, the parallel basis |\n| [Q24](#q24-are-compile-time-macros-a-first-class-concept) | Are compile-time macros a first-class concept? | OPEN, not yet evaluated |\n| [Q25](#q25-does-the-language-have-union-types) | Does the language have union types? | PARTLY SETTLED: closed nominal sums exist (enums); anonymous structural unions remain an absence |\n| [Q26](#q26-is-jsxs-children-slot-a-closed-per-site-union-or-an-open-one) | Is JSX's children slot a closed per-site union, or an open one? | OPEN, deliberately deferred to last |\n| [Q27](#q27-does-pattern-matching-need-a-separate-matcher-type-distinct-from-result) | Does pattern matching need a separate `Matcher` type, distinct from `Result`? | SETTLED |\n| [Q28](#q28-does-deep-matching-need-cross-match-unification-of-logic-variables) | Does deep matching need cross-match unification of logic variables? | OPEN |\n| [Q29](#q29-what-is-the-default-discriminant-convention-for-a-derived-enum-codec) | What is the default discriminant convention for a derived enum codec? | SUPERSEDED: the enum decision made the single-key wrapper the value itself, not a codec default |\n| [Q30](#q30-do-the-base-functor-generics-double-as-parser-combinators-across-trees-strings-and-streams) | Do the base-functor generics double as parser combinators, across trees, strings, and streams? | LEANING yes, implementation split still open |\n| [Q31](#q31-does-a-friendlier-string-pattern-language-belong-in-the-language-and-what-regex-flavor-does-it-extend-to) | Does a friendlier string-pattern language belong in the language, and what regex flavor does it extend to? | OPEN |\n| [Q32](#q32-does-the-dimension-model-subsume-the-effect-layer) | Does the dimension model subsume the effect layer? | OPEN, and it may dissolve Q13 rather than answer it |\n| [Q33](#q33-does-a-spread-slot-in-a-call-give-partial-application) | Does a spread slot in a call give partial application? | OPEN, and only expressible because arguments are a record |\n| [Q34](#q34-do-named-types-exist-and-is-a-name-an-alias-or-an-identity) | Do named types exist, and is a name an alias or an identity? | OPEN for records; enums decided identity for themselves, and enum declarations are the first declaration form |\n| [Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) | What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return? | OPEN; `jsonlines` is now a top-level-only sink with no result type, which removes a placeholder answer without deciding the question |\n| [Q36](#q36-does-a-real-module-system-need-imports-multiple-files-and-enforced-privacy) | Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy? | OPEN, one always-on prelude file exists; nothing beyond it does |\n| [Q37](#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model) | How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model? | RULED (gh:145): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (matches IEEE). Printing format is still open per-backend conformance work; tracked at board row `float-build` |\n\n[Multidimensional vectors](#q9-are-vectors-multidimensional-with--as-projection) is the one\nquestion still capable of changing [the two-layer\nsection](../draft.md#the-core-idea-two-layers), now that\n[streams](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) are settled, so it\nshould be resolved before that section is treated as stable.\n\n## Question detail\n\n### Q1. Streams: first-class values, or evaluation-level multiplicity?\n\nSETTLED: evaluation-level and typed. `Stream<T>` is the effect layer's type, second-class and\nconsumed exactly once, not a value type. Recorded in\n[ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).\n\n### Q2. Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit?\n\nCartesian (jq today), zip\n(vectorized, with broadcast), or neither by default with explicit `cross` and `zip`?\n\nVec concatenation specifically is decided, without touching the rest of this question. Revised\n2026-08-30 (oddities round, kantord/toylang#97): `+` on two `Vec`s of the same element type now\nconcatenates them -- the add-trait reading -- so `[1, 2] + [3]` is `[1, 2, 3]`, settling this\nhalf of Q2 in favor of concatenation over cartesian or zip. The named builtin this superseded,\n`concat(vv: Vec<Vec<T>>) -> Vec<T>`, existed specifically so adding it would not decide this\nquestion (see [named functions kept an open question\nopen](../research-log/named-functions-kept-an-open-question-open.md)); it survives under the name\n`flatten` for the case `+` cannot cover, an outer `Vec` whose length is not known at the call\nsite. The general question -- what any *other* operator means when both operands are Vecs --\nis still open.\n\nComposite equality is settled without touching it. `==` on a record or an enum compares\nstructurally, and is refused outright when the type carries a Vec anywhere inside it, so a\n`Vec`-typed record field never quietly acquires whole-value semantics\n([the equality decision](../draft.md#decided-equality-on-a-composite-is-structural-and-stops-at-a-vec)).\n\n### Q3. What symbol replaces `=` for the record-forming update?\n\n### Q4. Can the type express ordering over heterogeneous streams?\n\nSubsumes the older cardinality-versus-\norder thread, which asked whether the type system should track *how many* values an\nexpression produces or *in what order* the kinds arrive. Those turned out to be the same\nquestion asked from two sides, so they are tracked here as one. The cardinality half is the\ncheaper and more decidable option, and it catches the failure that actually bites, which is\nmultiplicity leaking into a position wanting exactly one value. The order half is what the\nrest of this entry is about.\nIf a stream is \"some `A`s, then some `B`s\", can the type say so? One approach is *regular\nexpressions over types*, the same\nalgebra as string regexes but with types as the alphabet, so a pattern denotes a set of\npermitted value-sequences: `Seq<A,B>` = `A* B*`, `Alt<A,B>` = `(A|B)*`, `Star<A>` = `A*`.\nThree primitives suffice (Kleene's theorem), it is decidable, and unlike full session types\nit needs no *linear types*, a discipline requiring each value be consumed exactly once,\nwhich is powerful but infects the whole system. Unpacking one item is then the **derivative**\nof the pattern: given that an `A` was just consumed, what remains? Open: how type tagging is\nrepresented so the runtime and type-level guarantees stay symmetrical.\n\nAn exploration after [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed)\ncommitted to this shape without settling the open parts; it is recorded as\n[ADR 0008](../docs/adr/0008-stream-protocols-are-kleene-patterns.md). The load-bearing findings:\nthe linearity objection above is obsolete, since the streams decision introduced exactly-once\nconsumption scoped to one second-class type, which is all a protocol type needs; `Opt`, `Vec`,\nand `Stream` are already this algebra (`?` and `*` on the value side, `*` on the effect side),\nso the pattern constructors extend the cardinality table rather than joining it; the empty\npattern is `Seq`'s unit, which makes a payload-free end cost nothing and collapses any\n\"stream plus end slot\" primitive back into `Star<T>`; a closing message is `Seq<Star<T>, E>`\nand several ways to end is `Alt` in terminal position, which types mid-stream failure and\nmakes errors structurally terminal. The soundness condition that keeps all of it second-class:\na `Stream` never appears under a value constructor, and may appear freely under pattern\nconstructors. Still blocking: union types ([Q25](#q25-does-the-language-have-union-types)),\ndiscriminants ([Q29](#q29-what-is-the-default-discriminant-convention-for-a-derived-enum-codec)),\nthe matcher surface ([Q27](#q27-does-pattern-matching-need-a-separate-matcher-type-distinct-from-result)),\nand the spelling question (patterns inside the constructor versus outer combinators).\n\n### Q5. Stream-lowering strategy across the three backends\n\nLua has true coroutines, JavaScript has generators, native\nhas neither for free. Previously recorded as needing to be decided before any backend is\nwritten, which turned out to be false: three backends exist without it, because nothing in\nthem streams. Then recorded as blocking any backend that *streams*, which the fused\n`jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop showed is also false: all seven backends now stream that pipeline\nshape as a plain read/transform/write loop, no coroutines or generators involved, because a\nstraight-line pipeline never needs to suspend. What the question still covers is lowering\nbeyond that shape -- a stream consumed by something that is not the tail of its own loop --\nwhere the coroutine/generator/state-machine choice becomes real.\n\n### Q6. Does a reconciler belong in the language or a library?\n\n### Q7. Does `..` promise depth-first order, or only the set of nodes?\n\nOn a flat columnar layout,\n\"every node at every depth\" is \"every element of every buffer\", which is embarrassingly\nparallel. The dependent part is not the traversal but the *order*, since the flat layout is\nnot in depth-first order. jq promises the order. If this language only promises the set,\nrecursive descent becomes one of the cheapest operators rather than one of the most\nexpensive. This is not only a performance question: a jq-derived language that is fast\neverywhere except recursive descent has a positioning problem, because `..` is one of the two\nthings people reach for jq to do.\n\n### Q8. Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization?\n\nReporting it\nmeans a second effect alongside cardinality, and a visible fast-path/slow-path distinction in\nsignatures. Hiding it makes performance unpredictable in exactly the way this design is\ntrying to avoid. Note the two effects are orthogonal: `select` changes cardinality and\nvectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way and cannot\nvectorize at all.\n\n### Q9. Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection?\n\nSee the TODO and response in the\ncardinality section. Unifies indexing with iteration, but disturbs the claim that there are\nexactly two layer shifters, and per-dimension cardinality only describes rectangular data\nwhile JSON is ragged.\n\n### Q10. Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes?\n\nDeciding when a projection lens can materialize instead\n of staying a view requires knowing no other reference to the source survives. That is\n linearity or uniqueness typing, the machinery deliberately avoided in [the ordering question](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams).\n\n### Q11. How does the query/transformation split manifest in the type system?\n\nSETTLED: it does not need to. `map` and `select` are the same operation with the multiplicity\nstored in different places, so the split never reaches the type system; see [the two-layer\nsection](../draft.md#the-core-idea-two-layers).\n\n### Q12. On a type mismatch, does field access error, yield null, or something third?\n\nSETTLED: something third. Field access desugars to a lens with three distinguishable outcomes\n-- a value, a specific absence, and a specific error; see [the field-access\nsection](../draft.md#field-access-is-a-lens).\n\n### Q13. Does the layer shift run only one way, with no value-to-effect operator?\n\nIf effect multiplicity is born only from\n streaming sources and dies only into values through `[...]`, then no value-to-effect\n operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys nothing. LEANING\n toward yes. This decides [the streams question](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) with it, since the only thing that would break it is a value\n with genuinely unknown extent, which is what a first-class stream value would be. The streams\n question is now settled the compatible way, and this lifecycle -- born at `inputs`/`lines`,\n dead at `collect` or a sink -- became the `Stream<T>` typing rule, so reversing this lean now\n means amending that decision too.\n\n### Q14. Does `select` return a masked view, a selection vector, or a copy?\n\nSee the section on\n whether a value-layer `select` copies. A bitmask breaks `Vec`'s constant-time indexing\n promise, a selection vector keeps it and pays memory per survivor, and either view pins its\n whole source buffer alive.\n\n### Q15. Backend: LLVM via inkwell, Cranelift, or both?\n\nSETTLED: LLVM via inkwell, built and running. Recorded in\n[ADR 0005](../docs/adr/0005-llvm-via-inkwell-for-the-native-backend.md).\n\n### Q16. String representation, given WTF-16 on the JS target\n\nThe three\n options are WTF-16 everywhere, UTF-8 everywhere with the JavaScript-shaped API emulated, or\n designing the difference away by never exposing code-unit indexing or length. Only the third\n is cheap on both sides. It has to be decided early because it constrains the string API\n permanently.\n\n### Q17. Is there a dense tensor type, constructed explicitly?\n\n`@f32` as a narrowing constructor\n that hard-fails rather than an inference, with `reshape` attaching shape. It is also the\n second number type, a deliberate lossy exit from the `f64` commitment.\n\n### Q18. Does `.[]` on a rank-2 tensor yield rows or scalars?\n\nNumPy and APL both yield rows,\n which makes `map` rank-polymorphic and gives row sums as `map(fold(add; 0))` with no new\n syntax. Then rank-1 yields scalars and full linearization needs a separate flattening view.\n\n### Q19. How are nulls carried in a dense typed buffer?\n\nJSON has null and an `f32` buffer does\n not. NaN as a sentinel collides with genuine NaN. Arrow's separate validity bitmask solves\n it and brings zero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB and pandas.\n\n### Q20. How are blocking operators (`sort`, `group_by`, joins) classified?\n\n`sort`, `group_by` and joins are one value in and\n one value out, so the per-element cardinality mapping does not describe them. They need the\n whole input before producing anything and are parallelizable by other means. The\n kernel-admissibility result covers elementwise filters only, and this is the gap it leaves.\n\n### Q21. What guarantees batch size is unobservable over a batched stream?\n\nArgued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from) rather than\nhere, since it arrived with that material. The leaning is the trait law that operations commute\nwith reification, which is what makes a batch boundary invisible to a program.\n\n### Q22. Are dense and masked vectors distinguishable in the type?\n\nArgued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), where it\nappears as the observation that a masked view and a dense buffer have different launch\npreconditions. The same question as [what select returns](#q14-does-select-return-a-masked-view-a-selection-vector-or-a-copy), approached from\nthe layout side rather than the operator side.\n\n### Q23. What primitive set is the standard library defined over?\n\nArgued in [the primitive set cannot be fold and recursion](../draft.md#the-primitive-set-cannot-be-fold-and-recursion). The leaning is the\nparallel basis, with `fold` and general recursion available but not the thing everything else\nis defined over.\n### Q24. Are compile-time macros a first-class concept?\n\nA macro would be a function that runs at compile time and transforms the compiler's own\nrepresentation of a program, which means that representation has to be a type the language\ndefines rather than an implementation detail the compiler happens to have. Fully compile-time, as\nin Rust, with no runtime field.\n\nThe syntax idea is decorator-style, as in Python, where the same notation can attach either an\nordinary closure or a macro. That the two look alike is the point worth checking: it is either\nthe feature's main convenience or its main trap, since one runs when the program runs and the\nother runs while it is being compiled, and the principle about writing crossings down applies to\nthat boundary too.\n\nNot evaluated. Recorded so it is not rediscovered.\n\n### Q25. Does the language have union types?\n\nThere is no sum type. `Alt<A,B>` appears only inside the regular-expressions-over-types sketch for\nstream ordering, and `Json` stands in wherever a value might be several things, which makes it\nthe permissive escape hatch principle 1 says it should not be.\n\nThe gap surfaced from asking what `.[]` on a heterogeneous record would even produce. With a union\nit is `Str | Int`; without one there is no answer. That question is settled on other grounds, but\nthe absence it exposed is not, and heterogeneous data is not a corner of a data language.\n\nRelated: an alternation over types is also what the ordering question needs, so these may be one\npiece of machinery rather than two.\n\nPartly settled by [the enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native): closed nominal\nsums now exist, and they serve both this question's motivating case (heterogeneous data) and the\nordering question's `Alt` (a stream of several message kinds is `Stream<SomeEnum>`). What\nremains absent is the anonymous structural union, `Str | Int` with no declaration -- a\ndifferent feature with a different justification, still an absence rather than a decision.\n\n### Q26. Is JSX's children slot a closed per-site union, or an open one?\n\nSketch: a creator function taking a `Record` of strictly-typed attrs (this is just `Field`\nin the existing sense, no new machinery) plus a `Dimension` of children. The children slot needs\nan element type, and that is where the interesting question lives.\n\nReact's `ReactNode` is open: any function shaped like a creator function is accepted, unconstrained\nat the definition site. That is the same escape hatch [Q25](#q25-does-the-language-have-union-types)\nalready names and principle 1 rejects -- an unconstrained union is `Json` with a different label.\n\nThe alternative is closed and inferred per call site: each JSX expression's children type is\n`Alt<T1, T2, ...>` built from whatever is literally nested there, checkable and exhaustive, with\nno shared vocabulary required across call sites. The cost is fragmentation: a function written to\naccept \"a list of children\" can only accept the exact union inferred at its own call site, not\nchildren built elsewhere out of a different but compatible set of node kinds. Parametric\npolymorphism over the union narrows that gap but does not close it.\n\nDeliberately last: the right answer depends on the rest of the design (how this interacts with\n[Q24](#q24-are-compile-time-macros-a-first-class-concept), and how JSX trees actually get passed\nbetween functions in practice), not on this slot in isolation.\n\n### Q27. Does pattern matching need a separate `Matcher` type, distinct from `Result`?\n\nSETTLED yes: matchers are first-class, tagged, and or-composable, derived per type under the\ncapital name (kantord/toylang#47). See [the enum decision's construction and naming\nsection](../draft.md#construction-and-naming) and\n[guides/matching.md](../docs/guides/matching.md).\n\n### Q28. Does deep matching need cross-match unification of logic variables?\n\nOPEN. `..` composed with a matcher already finds a shape anywhere in a tree without naming its\npath, and `as` already binds one submatch to a name for reuse within the same arm. Neither needs\nunification. What would: finding a node `A` and a separate node `B` elsewhere such that `B`\nrefers to `A`, which is full Prolog-style unification with backtracking over bindings, not a\nbigger version of `as`. See [Pattern matching is decoding](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding).\n\n### Q29. What is the default discriminant convention for a derived enum codec?\n\nSUPERSEDED: there is no derived codec picking a representation, because the representation\n*is* the value. [ADR 0009](../docs/adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)\nrecords the decision, and why the tag-field and shape-matched alternatives lost.\n\n### Q30. Do the base-functor generics double as parser combinators, across trees, strings, and streams?\n\nLEANING yes. `Seq`, `Alt`, `Star`, and `Opt` are already in the document as [the regex-over-types algebra](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams)\nand as the shape [Pattern matching is decoding](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding) builds `Matcher<T>` from; naming them as parser\ncombinators only makes the precedent explicit (Hutton and Meijer; Wadler; parsing with\nderivatives). OPEN: whether this is one trait with implementations that differ by receiver (a\nparsed tree needs no backtracking, a string needs an actual parsing engine), the same shape as\n[`Field<K>`](../draft.md#field-access-is-a-lens), and if so what law the implementations have to share. See\n[One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams](../draft.md#one-combinator-algebra-for-trees-strings-and-streams).\n\n### Q31. Does a friendlier string-pattern language belong in the language, and what regex flavor does it extend to?\n\nOPEN. A URL-route-style syntax with named, typed captures composing through the existing\n`int(.)`-style codec syntax is one candidate, with Swift's `Regex` builder and route-pattern DSLs\nsuch as Express's `path-to-regexp` as the closest prior art. [The arm-list's `//` semantics](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding) already\ncommit any such language to ordered, PEG-style choice, which is compatible with PCRE/Perl-style\nregex and not with POSIX leftmost-longest regex, so \"extends to regular expressions\" needs to\nname which flavor. See\n[One combinator algebra for trees, strings, and streams](../draft.md#one-combinator-algebra-for-trees-strings-and-streams).\n\n### Q32. Does the dimension model subsume the effect layer?\n\nThe two-layer section says multiplicity lives either in a value or in evaluation, and\n[the one-way shift](../draft.md#proposal-the-layer-shift-only-runs-one-way) narrows that to\neffect multiplicity being born from streaming input and never from a value. The dimension\nproposal says something that may be the same thing in different words: a value has an ordered\nlist of dimensions, and a spec says what happens to each.\n\nPut them together and a `Stream` looks like a value with a dimension whose extent is not known\nyet. The spec vocabulary already covers it without a second layer: keep and narrow are\nstreamable, since neither has to consume anything to know what it did, and collapse is not. That\ndistinction is written down in the dimension proposal and it is exactly the `Vec` and `Stream`\ndifference.\n\nIf that holds, there is one layer with a refinement rather than two layers, and the question\nstops being which direction the shift runs and becomes whether anything shifts at all.\n\nThree things would follow, and they are what makes this worth settling rather than leaving\nimplicit:\n\n`map` stays primitive rather than becoming sugar for `[ .[] | f ]` later, since the thing it\nwould be sugar for never comes back.\n\n`.[]` stays inert on a `Vec` permanently. Keeping every index of a known extent changes nothing,\nand no future feature makes it change something.\n\nThe two-layer framing that opens this document becomes a description of a special case rather\nthan the organising idea.\n\nNot proposed, because the two-layer section is load-bearing and this has not been worked through\nagainst an actual streaming input. What prompted it: prototype 1 has no effect layer, and that\nis not a departure from the design but what the design predicts for a program that reads one\nwhole value and hands one back. Whether the layer returns with streaming, or whether streaming\nturns out to be a dimension, is the open part.\n\n### Q33. Does a spread slot in a call give partial application?\n\nFunctions are unary and several arguments travel as one record, which makes a question available\nthat a positional language would have to answer with arity counting. If a call may leave a slot\nopen -- spelled `...` for now -- what comes back is a function expecting the fields that were\nnot supplied:\n\n```\njoin {with: \", \", ...}\n```\n\n`join` takes `{over: Vec<Str>, with: Str}`, so supplying `with` leaves a function from\n`{over: Vec<Str>}` to `Str`. The remaining parameter is **the complement of what was given**,\ncomputed structurally rather than by position, so there is no question of which argument was\nskipped and no need for placeholders in the other slots.\n\nWhat makes this worth recording rather than dismissing is that it is not a feature bolted onto\nthe call syntax; it falls out of arguments already being a record. Partial application in a\npositional language has to invent a convention for \"this one, not that one\". Here the convention\nis subtraction on field names, which the type system already does.\n\nOpen, and roughly in dependency order:\n\n- **Does it need first-class functions?** The language has none: `Sig` is a parameter and a\n  result, and there is no function type in the type grammar. A partial application evaluates to a\n  function, so it needs one to have a type. That is the real cost, and it is much larger than the\n  syntax.\n- **What does the residual type look like?** `{over: Vec<Str>} -> Str` needs an arrow in the type\n  grammar, which is the same thing the previous point asks for.\n- **Is `...` the right spelling?** It reads as \"and the rest\", which is right, but the token is\n  unused and could go to a spread that *supplies* fields instead -- `{...defaults, n: 1}` --\n  and those two meanings would collide.\n- **Does supplying nothing mean anything?** `join {...}` would be `join` itself, which is either\n  a harmless identity or a sign the spelling proves too much.\n- **Does it interact with the dimension model at all?** A record literal does not distribute, so\n  presumably not, but partial application inside `map` is exactly where it would be used most.\n\nBlocked on first-class functions, which nothing else currently needs.\n\n### Q34. Do named types exist, and is a name an alias or an identity?\n\nDeferred when record literals were settled, on the grounds that a literal synthesises its own\ntype and so forecloses nothing. What has since turned up is that the *constructor* for a named\ntype already works, which changes what the question costs without changing what it asks.\n\nA constructor is a unary function from the structural record to the named type, and that shape\nexists today:\n\n```\nfn User(c: {name: Str, age: Int}) -> {name: Str, age: Int} = c\n\nUser {name: \"ada\", age: 36}\n```\n\n`User {...}` parses and reaches the checker, which rejects it only because no function of that\nname is defined. Type names and expression names already resolve by separate paths, so there is\nno ambiguity to invent a rule for.\n\nFree, then: the spelling, and the semantics, since a constructor being a unary function over a\nrecord is the same decision already made for arguments generally.\n\nNot free, in rough order of how much they decide:\n\n- **Destructuring.** Does `.name` on a `User` see through the name, or does getting a field\n  out need an explicit step? Nothing about this direction falls out, and it is what decides\n  whether an identity is pleasant or a tax.\n- **The declaration form.** `Type::from_name` knows three names and there is no `type X = ...`.\n- **One namespace or two.** The checker looks a call up in `sigs`, so a type declaration\n  introducing a constructor would put type names and function names in one namespace. That is\n  probably right and should be chosen rather than arrived at.\n- **Alias or identity.** The question proper, and the only part the above does not touch. An\n  alias abbreviates a type the tutorial's annotation shows is worth abbreviating; an identity\n  makes two same-shaped records refuse to interchange, which is a different feature with a\n  different justification.\n\nWorth being explicit that cheapness is not an argument. What is recorded here is that the cost of\nidentity is lower than it looked, not that the language wants it.\n\n[The enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native) has since answered every bullet for\nenums specifically: they are identities (exhaustiveness requires it), `enum` is the declaration\nform, and variant constructors land in the value namespace with bare-until-ambiguous\nresolution. Records are deliberately not carried along; the alias-or-identity question stays\nopen for them, and this entry now tracks only that half.\n\n### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n\nThis document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\ntransforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\nhere rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\nfar has been about values coming *in*.\n\nWhat exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\ntype-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\nno side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n\nWhat it does not answer:\n\n- **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n  keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n  Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n  the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n- **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n  agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n  language means six backends must agree on it.\n- **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\n  to, which means the printer is the only specification of the format.\n- **Does a stream of outputs exist at all**, or does a program produce one value whose rendering\n  happens to be long? jq answers the first; the design so far assumes the second without saying\n  so.\n\nBlocked on the same thing as [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends): a\nprogram that writes as it goes is a program with an effect layer.\n\nThe fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend\nlevel, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect\nlayer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than\nanswered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink,\nlegal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type. That removes the old\nplaceholder (`jsonlines(...) : Str`, a type claiming the whole output exists as one value)\nwithout deciding whether stdout is a value, an effect, or something a program returns into.\nEverything in the bullet list above remains open.\n\n### Q36. Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy?\n\nOne file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\nprogram receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\nall of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\nfile to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\nnot \"private to the prelude,\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\ncalling a private helper.\n\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n  are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n  something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n  admits values its own output cannot carry.\n- **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n  IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n  behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n\nNone of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\nway `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":553,"totalLines":553,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788296879358,"end":1788296879364}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read the key files: plans/questions.md Q7, plans/board.yaml row, draft.md's 'Query is search' section, and the plans directory.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eccdb86001rTrLDDelFuQL2k","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05eccd68c001j1y6ttbkoEow42"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296879980,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-af3b17082f025f91","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/board.yaml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/board.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n2:   issue: gh:152\n3:   title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet -- \"explore all of these alternatives (especially my original preference) to see if either version would be problematic etc\"; round 3 needs to stress-test each option (esp. the Msg(...) call-form sketch) for concrete problems before re-asking'\n4:   kind: decide\n5:   needs: []\n6:   prio: 3\n7:   status: todo\n8: \n9: - id: declare-terminator-build\n10:   issue: gh:153\n11:   title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n12:   kind: build\n13:   needs: []\n14:   prio: 3\n15:   status: delegated\n16: \n17: - id: jsonlines-tail-marker-build\n18:   issue: gh:154\n19:   title: Implement the |> tail-pipeline marker for sink calls, restricted to outermost position (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n20:   kind: build\n21:   needs: [sink-kind-build]\n22:   prio: 3\n23:   status: delegated\n24: \n25: - id: dsv-rfc4180-quoting-build\n26:   issue: gh:136\n27:   title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): dsv(delim) needs RFC 4180 quoted-field handling (embedded delimiters, doubled quotes, embedded newlines) -- replace the raw byte-split shipped in gh:136 with a real stateful scanner'\n28:   kind: build\n29:   needs: []\n30:   prio: 3\n31:   status: delegated\n32: \n33: - id: dsv-partials-migration\n34:   issue: gh:136\n35:   title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n36:   kind: build\n37:   needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n38:   prio: 5\n39:   status: todo\n40: \n41: - id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n42:   issue: gh:93\n43:   title: Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3's correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling)\n44:   kind: build\n45:   needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n46:   prio: 3\n47:   status: delegated\n48: \n49: - id: variant-types-flip\n50:   issue: gh:165\n51:   title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n52:   kind: build\n53:   needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n54:   prio: 2\n55:   model: fable\n56:   status: todo\n57: \n58: - id: shell-out-build\n59:   issue: gh:158\n60:   title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n61:   kind: build\n62:   needs: []\n63:   status: delegated\n64: \n65: - id: stdin-redesign-build\n66:   issue: gh:159\n67:   title: Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative, gh:159 -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build)\n68:   kind: build\n69:   needs: []\n70:   model: fable\n71:   status: delegated\n72: \n73: - id: trait-interface-research\n74:   title: 'Ruled (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff): declared interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped) is the direction for ad hoc polymorphism. Research spike before design: scope the minimal checker mechanism (trait declaration, impl-for-type, static dispatch) and whether it truly requires user-facing generics or can start monomorphic (one impl per concrete type, no generic trait bounds). Write findings to plans/.'\n75:   kind: build\n76:   needs: []\n77:   prio: 3\n78:   status: todo\n79: \n80: - id: trait-interface-design\n81:   title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research'\n82:   kind: decide\n83:   needs: [trait-interface-research]\n84:   status: todo\n85: \n86: - id: mutation-semantics-spike\n87:   title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n88:   kind: build\n89:   needs: []\n90:   prio: 4\n91:   status: todo\n92: \n93: - id: float-build\n94:   issue: gh:149\n95:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\n96:   kind: build\n97:   needs: []\n98:   status: delegated\n99: \n100: - id: benchmark-suite-build\n101:   title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\n102:   kind: build\n103:   needs: []\n104:   prio: 4\n105:   status: todo\n106: \n107: - id: lines-utf8-posix-research\n108:   issue: gh:102\n109:   title: 'Round 2 answer (question lines-non-utf8-byte): no pick -- maintainer wants deeper research beyond plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201 before choosing refuse/carry-through/lossy-replace: what is most correct, most secure, most POSIX-compliant, most common across existing Unix line-oriented tools, and what would match user expectations. Write findings to plans/.'\n110:   kind: build\n111:   needs: []\n112:   prio: 3\n113:   status: todo\n114: \n115: - id: lines-utf8-edge\n116:   issue: gh:102\n117:   title: 'Pin what a non-UTF-8 byte arriving via lines does, on every backend. Round 1 research (plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201) covered per-backend mechanics only; round 2 answer asked for correctness/security/POSIX-compliance/common-practice research first (see lines-utf8-posix-research) before round 3 re-asks with the three options (refuse / carry-where-possible / uniform lossy replacement).'\n118:   kind: decide\n119:   needs: [lines-utf8-posix-research]\n120:   status: todo\n121: \n122: - id: retire-ternary-build\n123:   issue: gh:155\n124:   title: Remove the ternary, replace every corpus/example/doc use with the equivalent match form (ternary-vs-match ruling)\n125:   kind: build\n126:   needs: []\n127:   prio: 2\n128:   status: delegated\n129: \n130: - id: search-layer-build\n131:   title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n132:   kind: build\n133:   needs: []\n134:   status: todo\n135: \n136: - id: sort-by-max-by\n137:   title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n138:   kind: build\n139:   needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n140:   status: todo\n141: \n142: - id: param-destructure-build\n143:   title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n144:   kind: build\n145:   needs: []\n146:   status: todo\n147: \n148: - id: bigint-tracking\n149:   issue: gh:112\n150:   title: Arbitrary-precision integers -- tracked, deliberately unscheduled\n151:   kind: build\n152:   needs: []\n153:   prio: 5\n154:   status: todo\n155: \n156: - id: js-node-web-split-build\n157:   issue: gh:160\n158:   title: Build the JS Node/Web target split, TS type generation, builtin-replacement escape hatch, and toylang.conf.yaml as one system (js-node-web-split-design ruling, gh:160)\n159:   kind: build\n160:   needs: []\n161:   status: todo\n162: \n163: - id: module-routing-syntax-build\n164:   issue: gh:167\n165:   title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\n166:   kind: build\n167:   needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]\n168:   prio: 5\n169:   status: todo\n170: \n171: - id: erlang-target-consideration\n172:   issue: gh:163\n173:   title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\n174:   kind: decide\n175:   needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\n176:   prio: 5\n177:   status: todo\n178: \n179: - id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\n180:   issue: gh:170\n181:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n182:   kind: build\n183:   needs: []\n184:   prio: 4\n185:   status: todo\n186: \n187: - id: variant-checker-capital-first\n188:   issue: gh:156\n189:   title: Checker rejects enum variant declarations that don't start with a capital letter, per the curly-only-pattern ruling (gh:156 ruling)\n190:   kind: build\n191:   needs: []\n192:   status: delegated\n193: \n194: - id: match-type-wrapper-experiment\n195:   issue: gh:122\n196:   title: 'Experimentally build a Match<T> wrapper: `.` entering a `|` chain wraps as Match<T>, traits like `%` get generic impls over Match<T> per underlying T (applying only past a passing boolean guard), and an arm''s aliases become named fields on a result struct the match produces -- see what goes wrong before ratifying (gh:122 ruling)'\n197:   kind: build\n198:   needs: []\n199:   status: todo\n200: \n201: - id: closures-first-class-functions-research\n202:   issue: gh:119\n203:   title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n204:   kind: build\n205:   needs: []\n206:   prio: 3\n207:   status: todo\n208: \n209: - id: http-query-sugar-research\n210:   title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native) before scoping a design row. Write findings to plans/.'\n211:   kind: build\n212:   needs: []\n213:   prio: 5\n214:   status: todo\n215: \n216: - id: partial-application-system-design\n217:   issue: gh:119\n218:   title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n219:   kind: decide\n220:   needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n221:   status: todo\n222: \n223: \n224: \n225: - id: draft-core-model-migration\n226:   title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n227:   kind: build\n228:   model: fable\n229:   needs: []\n230:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n231:   prio: 4\n232:   status: todo\n233: \n234: - id: draft-access-model-migration\n235:   title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n236:   kind: build\n237:   needs: []\n238:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n239:   prio: 4\n240:   status: todo\n241: \n242: - id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n243:   title: Verify the Int/Float/equality sections against ADRs 0006/0007/0010 and the reference; move what's missing, delete (draft-split)\n244:   kind: build\n245:   needs: []\n246:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n247:   prio: 4\n248:   status: todo\n249: \n250: - id: draft-records-migration\n251:   title: Migrate the three record decisions (literals-and-arguments, declared order, order-not-identity) into the record reference, delete (draft-split)\n252:   kind: build\n253:   needs: []\n254:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n255:   prio: 4\n256:   status: todo\n257: \n258: - id: draft-streams-migration\n259:   title: Migrate the five stream/stdin decision sections against ADR 0001, the streams guide, and the sources reference; delete (draft-split)\n260:   kind: build\n261:   needs: []\n262:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n263:   prio: 4\n264:   status: todo\n265: \n266: - id: draft-calls-modules-migration\n267:   title: Migrate bare application and the prelude module system into the syntax/prelude reference, delete (draft-split)\n268:   kind: build\n269:   needs: []\n270:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n271:   prio: 4\n272:   status: todo\n273: \n274: - id: draft-matching-migration\n275:   title: Migrate the matching/enums sections -- ratified parts to guides and ADR 0009, codec threads to the tracker (draft-split)\n276:   kind: build\n277:   needs: []\n278:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home, matcher-model-clarification, matcher-totality-and-alt-design]\n279:   prio: 4\n280:   status: todo\n281: \n282: - id: draft-prototype-findings-migration\n283:   title: Verify every prototype-showed finding lives in research-log or is superseded, then delete the section (draft-split)\n284:   kind: build\n285:   needs: []\n286:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n287:   prio: 4\n288:   status: todo\n289: \n290: - id: draft-str-adr\n291:   title: Record the Str contract as an ADR per the string spike's recommendation, then delete the draft's strings section (draft-split)\n292:   kind: build\n293:   needs: []\n294:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, lines-utf8-edge]\n295:   prio: 3\n296:   status: todo\n297: \n298: - id: draft-mutation-migration\n299:   title: Record the mutation decisions once they land and delete both mutation sections (draft-split)\n300:   kind: build\n301:   needs: [mutation-semantics-design, binary-op-multiplicity-design]\n302:   prio: 4\n303:   status: todo\n304: \n305: - id: binary-op-multiplicity-design\n306:   title: 'Grill binary operators over multi-valued operands (cartesian, zip, or explicit) and the record-forming update''s spelling -- Q2/Q3 (draft-split). Round 2 answers (multiplicity-and-offload-round2): Q3 RATIFIED -- option B, require One on the right of `=`, forking becomes explicit; maintainer caveat: drop the `$c`-prefixed alias in the option''s preview, \"never agreed to prefacing variable names with $\" (applies to whatever alias/binding syntax eventually lands, not specific to this row). Q2 still no pick -- maintainer leans B (zip/broadcast) or C (no default, explicit) but wants to understand how much of jq''s magic/expressive power would be lost either way before choosing; needs jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research before a round 3 re-ask.'\n307:   kind: decide\n308:   needs: [jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research]\n309:   prio: 3\n310:   status: todo\n311: \n312: - id: jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research\n313:   issue: gh:169\n314:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question binary-op-default-multiplicity): survey real jq idioms that rely on the cartesian default for `Vec op Vec` (option A in binary-op-multiplicity-design), and work out concretely what breaks or gets more verbose under a zip/broadcast default (option B) versus no default at all, explicit `zip`/`cross` (option C). Use real jq snippets and their toylang-under-B and toylang-under-C equivalents so the comparison is concrete, not abstract. Write findings to plans/.'\n315:   kind: build\n316:   needs: []\n317:   prio: 3\n318:   status: todo\n319: \n320: - id: search-and-fold-design\n321:   title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needs that research before re-asking. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n322:   kind: decide\n323:   needs: [search-choice-point-match-syntax-research]\n324:   soft: [first-search-cut]\n325:   prio: 4\n326:   status: todo\n327: \n328: - id: search-choice-point-match-syntax-research\n329:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, question search-choice-point-syntax): maintainer asks for a deeper design pass on whether the choice-point (\"branch wider, explore every alternative\") operator should be spelled parallel to toylang''s match syntax instead of a new infix operator (`;`, `branch(...)`, or folding into `//`). Survey how other languages with pattern-matching-flavored search/logic constructs (Prolog disjunction, Icon/Curry generators, list-monad comprehensions) express this, and produce 2-3 concrete syntax candidates with real toylang code examples, explicitly including a match-parallel option. Write findings to plans/.'\n330:   kind: build\n331:   needs: []\n332:   prio: 3\n333:   status: todo\n334: \n335: - id: offload-boundary-design\n336:   title: 'Grill the offload/vectorization thesis -- kernel admissibility, batch invariance, parallel basis, tensors (draft-split). Round 2 answer (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility): silent/cardinality-derived vectorizability CONFIRMED (no type-level effect); the caveat''s discoverability mechanism is B now (opt-in `--explain-offload` compiler diagnostic) with C (editor/LSP hover) as a wanted future addition, not a near-term requirement. Spun off offload-explain-flag-build for B; LSP hover left unscheduled.'\n337:   kind: decide\n338:   needs: [offload-explain-flag-build]\n339:   soft: [benchmark-goals]\n340:   prio: 5\n341:   status: todo\n342: \n343: - id: offload-explain-flag-build\n344:   issue: gh:168\n345:   title: 'Implement `--explain-offload`: an opt-in compiler diagnostic that annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why -- per the offload-boundary-design round 2 ruling (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility, option B). No signature or type-system change; a build-time flag only. LSP hover (option C) is a wanted future addition, deliberately unscheduled here.'\n346:   kind: build\n347:   needs: []\n348:   prio: 4\n349:   status: todo\n350: \n351: - id: draft-md-cleanup-review\n352:   title: Review what remains of draft.md after every split row lands; assign or delete the remainder, retire the file (draft-split)\n353:   kind: build\n354:   needs: [draft-questions-home, draft-intro-migration, draft-core-model-migration, draft-access-model-migration, draft-numbers-operators-migration, draft-records-migration, draft-streams-migration, draft-calls-modules-migration, draft-matching-migration, draft-prototype-findings-migration, draft-str-adr, draft-mutation-migration, binary-op-multiplicity-design, search-and-fold-design, offload-boundary-design]\n355:   prio: 4\n356:   status: todo\n357: \n358: - id: mail-rounds-isolation\n359:   issue: gh:164\n360:   title: Mail tab must not blank on one invalid round file -- per-round fetch isolation + visible error row\n361:   kind: build\n362:   needs: []\n363:   prio: 2\n364:   status: todo\n365: \n366: - id: http-query-sugar-design\n367:   issue: gh:171\n368:   title: 'Maintainer note (composed 2026-08-31): design minimal HTTP query support (Python-requests-like) as sugar over whatever backend already provides -- needs a grilling round on surface area before it becomes build work'\n369:   kind: decide\n370:   needs: []\n371:   prio: 4\n372:   status: todo\n373: \n374: - id: brief-phrasing-experiment\n375:   title: 'Tracked in-repo per maintainer ruling (coordinator-escalation-brief-phrasing-experiment round, 2026-09-01: gh issue create was blocked by the permission classifier; no gh issue, no retry). Maintainer note (composed 2026-08-31): generic non-informational reassurance phrasing (\"its much simpler than you think\") in dispatch briefs seems to help planning agents find better plans -- experiment on at least 10 issues, then report progress and grill on the results.'\n376:   kind: build\n377:   needs: []\n378:   prio: 5\n379:   status: todo\n\n(End of file - total 379 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n  issue: gh:152\n  title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet -- \"explore all of these alternatives (especially my original preference) to see if either version would be problematic etc\"; round 3 needs to stress-test each option (esp. the Msg(...) call-form sketch) for concrete problems before re-asking'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: declare-terminator-build\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: jsonlines-tail-marker-build\n  issue: gh:154\n  title: Implement the |> tail-pipeline marker for sink calls, restricted to outermost position (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n  kind: build","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/board.yaml","text":"- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n  issue: gh:152\n  title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet -- \"explore all of these alternatives (especially my original preference) to see if either version would be problematic etc\"; round 3 needs to stress-test each option (esp. the Msg(...) call-form sketch) for concrete problems before re-asking'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: declare-terminator-build\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: jsonlines-tail-marker-build\n  issue: gh:154\n  title: Implement the |> tail-pipeline marker for sink calls, restricted to outermost position (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sink-kind-build]\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: dsv-rfc4180-quoting-build\n  issue: gh:136\n  title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): dsv(delim) needs RFC 4180 quoted-field handling (embedded delimiters, doubled quotes, embedded newlines) -- replace the raw byte-split shipped in gh:136 with a real stateful scanner'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: dsv-partials-migration\n  issue: gh:136\n  title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n  issue: gh:93\n  title: Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3's correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: variant-types-flip\n  issue: gh:165\n  title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n  prio: 2\n  model: fable\n  status: todo\n\n- id: shell-out-build\n  issue: gh:158\n  title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: stdin-redesign-build\n  issue: gh:159\n  title: Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative, gh:159 -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  model: fable\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: trait-interface-research\n  title: 'Ruled (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff): declared interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped) is the direction for ad hoc polymorphism. Research spike before design: scope the minimal checker mechanism (trait declaration, impl-for-type, static dispatch) and whether it truly requires user-facing generics or can start monomorphic (one impl per concrete type, no generic trait bounds). Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: trait-interface-design\n  title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [trait-interface-research]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: mutation-semantics-spike\n  title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: float-build\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: benchmark-suite-build\n  title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: lines-utf8-posix-research\n  issue: gh:102\n  title: 'Round 2 answer (question lines-non-utf8-byte): no pick -- maintainer wants deeper research beyond plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201 before choosing refuse/carry-through/lossy-replace: what is most correct, most secure, most POSIX-compliant, most common across existing Unix line-oriented tools, and what would match user expectations. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: lines-utf8-edge\n  issue: gh:102\n  title: 'Pin what a non-UTF-8 byte arriving via lines does, on every backend. Round 1 research (plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201) covered per-backend mechanics only; round 2 answer asked for correctness/security/POSIX-compliance/common-practice research first (see lines-utf8-posix-research) before round 3 re-asks with the three options (refuse / carry-where-possible / uniform lossy replacement).'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [lines-utf8-posix-research]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: retire-ternary-build\n  issue: gh:155\n  title: Remove the ternary, replace every corpus/example/doc use with the equivalent match form (ternary-vs-match ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 2\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: search-layer-build\n  title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: sort-by-max-by\n  title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: param-destructure-build\n  title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: bigint-tracking\n  issue: gh:112\n  title: Arbitrary-precision integers -- tracked, deliberately unscheduled\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: js-node-web-split-build\n  issue: gh:160\n  title: Build the JS Node/Web target split, TS type generation, builtin-replacement escape hatch, and toylang.conf.yaml as one system (js-node-web-split-design ruling, gh:160)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: module-routing-syntax-build\n  issue: gh:167\n  title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: erlang-target-consideration\n  issue: gh:163\n  title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\n  issue: gh:170\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: variant-checker-capital-first\n  issue: gh:156\n  title: Checker rejects enum variant declarations that don't start with a capital letter, per the curly-only-pattern ruling (gh:156 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: match-type-wrapper-experiment\n  issue: gh:122\n  title: 'Experimentally build a Match<T> wrapper: `.` entering a `|` chain wraps as Match<T>, traits like `%` get generic impls over Match<T> per underlying T (applying only past a passing boolean guard), and an arm''s aliases become named fields on a result struct the match produces -- see what goes wrong before ratifying (gh:122 ruling)'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: closures-first-class-functions-research\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: http-query-sugar-research\n  title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native) before scoping a design row. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: partial-application-system-design\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n  status: todo\n\n\n\n- id: draft-core-model-migration\n  title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  model: fable\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-access-model-migration\n  title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n  title: Verify the Int/Float/equality sections against ADRs 0006/0007/0010 and the reference; move what's missing, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-records-migration\n  title: Migrate the three record decisions (literals-and-arguments, declared order, order-not-identity) into the record reference, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-streams-migration\n  title: Migrate the five stream/stdin decision sections against ADR 0001, the streams guide, and the sources reference; delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-calls-modules-migration\n  title: Migrate bare application and the prelude module system into the syntax/prelude reference, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-matching-migration\n  title: Migrate the matching/enums sections -- ratified parts to guides and ADR 0009, codec threads to the tracker (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home, matcher-model-clarification, matcher-totality-and-alt-design]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-prototype-findings-migration\n  title: Verify every prototype-showed finding lives in research-log or is superseded, then delete the section (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-str-adr\n  title: Record the Str contract as an ADR per the string spike's recommendation, then delete the draft's strings section (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, lines-utf8-edge]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-mutation-migration\n  title: Record the mutation decisions once they land and delete both mutation sections (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [mutation-semantics-design, binary-op-multiplicity-design]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: binary-op-multiplicity-design\n  title: 'Grill binary operators over multi-valued operands (cartesian, zip, or explicit) and the record-forming update''s spelling -- Q2/Q3 (draft-split). Round 2 answers (multiplicity-and-offload-round2): Q3 RATIFIED -- option B, require One on the right of `=`, forking becomes explicit; maintainer caveat: drop the `$c`-prefixed alias in the option''s preview, \"never agreed to prefacing variable names with $\" (applies to whatever alias/binding syntax eventually lands, not specific to this row). Q2 still no pick -- maintainer leans B (zip/broadcast) or C (no default, explicit) but wants to understand how much of jq''s magic/expressive power would be lost either way before choosing; needs jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research before a round 3 re-ask.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research\n  issue: gh:169\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question binary-op-default-multiplicity): survey real jq idioms that rely on the cartesian default for `Vec op Vec` (option A in binary-op-multiplicity-design), and work out concretely what breaks or gets more verbose under a zip/broadcast default (option B) versus no default at all, explicit `zip`/`cross` (option C). Use real jq snippets and their toylang-under-B and toylang-under-C equivalents so the comparison is concrete, not abstract. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-and-fold-design\n  title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needs that research before re-asking. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [search-choice-point-match-syntax-research]\n  soft: [first-search-cut]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-choice-point-match-syntax-research\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, question search-choice-point-syntax): maintainer asks for a deeper design pass on whether the choice-point (\"branch wider, explore every alternative\") operator should be spelled parallel to toylang''s match syntax instead of a new infix operator (`;`, `branch(...)`, or folding into `//`). Survey how other languages with pattern-matching-flavored search/logic constructs (Prolog disjunction, Icon/Curry generators, list-monad comprehensions) express this, and produce 2-3 concrete syntax candidates with real toylang code examples, explicitly including a match-parallel option. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: offload-boundary-design\n  title: 'Grill the offload/vectorization thesis -- kernel admissibility, batch invariance, parallel basis, tensors (draft-split). Round 2 answer (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility): silent/cardinality-derived vectorizability CONFIRMED (no type-level effect); the caveat''s discoverability mechanism is B now (opt-in `--explain-offload` compiler diagnostic) with C (editor/LSP hover) as a wanted future addition, not a near-term requirement. Spun off offload-explain-flag-build for B; LSP hover left unscheduled.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [offload-explain-flag-build]\n  soft: [benchmark-goals]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: offload-explain-flag-build\n  issue: gh:168\n  title: 'Implement `--explain-offload`: an opt-in compiler diagnostic that annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why -- per the offload-boundary-design round 2 ruling (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility, option B). No signature or type-system change; a build-time flag only. LSP hover (option C) is a wanted future addition, deliberately unscheduled here.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-md-cleanup-review\n  title: Review what remains of draft.md after every split row lands; assign or delete the remainder, retire the file (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [draft-questions-home, draft-intro-migration, draft-core-model-migration, draft-access-model-migration, draft-numbers-operators-migration, draft-records-migration, draft-streams-migration, draft-calls-modules-migration, draft-matching-migration, draft-prototype-findings-migration, draft-str-adr, draft-mutation-migra