{"type":"text","timestamp":1788296889434,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd031a001Wr7ENq2IH1szMd","messageID":"msg_05eccf979001HYpssGwXuWtsfu","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788296889115,"end":1788296889433}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296889439,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_693e85d2d7ce4d7bb4672405","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","offset":690,"limit":130},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n690: at least two implementations, the same shape [`Field<K>`](#field-access-is-a-lens) already has for indexable versus\n691: iterable receivers. What would need stating, and is not yet, is the law the two implementations\n692: have to share to count as the same trait -- in the spirit of\n693: [the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\n694: reification.\"\n695: \n696: The string pattern language is a specialization of exactly this algebra, not a separate feature.\n697: Spelling repetition as a named combinator call rather than a metacharacter (`mul(\"a\")` instead of\n698: `a*`) trades density for not needing a second syntax to learn, the same trade this document\n699: already made for `and`/`or`/`not` over `&&`/`||`. Named, typed captures need no new mechanism\n700: either: a capture group decoding to an `Int` is `int(.)` from [Pattern matching is decoding](#pattern-matching-is-decoding) applied to a\n701: captured substring instead of a `Json` field, so \"more specific than string\" is the existing\n702: codec syntax, not a new one.\n703: \n704: One consequence is already decided without having been meant to be. [The arm-list section](#pattern-matching-is-decoding) settled\n705: on `//`'s left-to-right, first-match-wins semantics for alternation rather than `,`'s\n706: explore-all semantics. That is exactly PEG's defining feature over classical regex/CFG\n707: alternation: ordered choice, no ambiguity, no need to explore every branch. It is compatible with\n708: PCRE/Perl-style backtracking regex, which is also priority-ordered, but not with POSIX\n709: leftmost-longest regex (`grep -E`, `awk`), which is a genuinely different alternation semantics.\n710: So \"extends to regular expressions\" should be read as \"extends to PCRE-flavored regex\"\n711: specifically -- a real, load-bearing consequence of a decision already made, not a detail to\n712: leave implicit.\n713: \n714: Closest existing prior art for the surface syntax, worth reading before inventing one from\n715: scratch: Swift's `Regex` builder (named, typed captures composed via a result-builder DSL, fully\n716: interoperable with a real regex engine) is close to what the TODO describes, and path templating\n717: in Express's `path-to-regexp` and Rails routes is close to the \"URL pattern\" framing, including\n718: their convention of embedding a raw regex inside a named segment for cases the friendly syntax\n719: cannot express (`:id(\\d+)`) -- the same \"friendly by default, escape hatch to full power\" shape\n720: being asked for here.\n721: \n722: ## Query is search\n723: \n724: **Nondeterminism** here does not mean randomness. It means an expression denotes *a set of\n725: possible answers*, and evaluation explores all of them in a fixed order. This is the sense used\n726: in Prolog and in nondeterministic automata. Formally it is the *list monad*: a filter maps one\n727: value to a list of results, and `|` chains those lists together.\n728: \n729: Two search terms used below. **Cut** means committing to what you have and abandoning the\n730: remaining alternatives. **Pruning** means discarding a branch before exploring it.\n731: \n732: | operator | search meaning |\n733: |---|---|\n734: | `\\|` | bind, so for each choice, go deeper (depth-first descent) |\n735: | `,` | choice point, so branch wider |\n736: | `empty` | dead end, backtrack |\n737: | `first(f)` | cut |\n738: | `..` | full tree traversal |\n739: | `select` | pruning |\n740: | `//` | alternatives, left to right |\n741: \n742: ```\n743: ..                                 # every node, depth-first\n744: .. | select(.kind == \"error\")      # prune to matches\n745: first(.. | select(.id == 7))       # stop at the first hit\n746: .a // .b // \"default\"\n747: ```\n748: \n749: A reified search is a result set:\n750: \n751: ```\n752: fn diagnostics(tree: Ast) -> Vec<Diag> =\n753:     [ tree.. | select(.kind == \"error\")\n754:              | {file: .loc.file, line: .loc.line, msg: .text} ]\n755: ```\n756: \n757: ## Single-pass composition\n758: \n759: A `Stream` can only be walked once, so combining several independent accumulations over one\n760: stream needs first-class support:\n761: \n762: ```\n763: fn stats(xs: Stream<Int>) -> {sum: Int, count: Int, max: Int} =\n764:     fold xs {\n765:         sum:   0    with (acc, x) -> acc + x\n766:         count: 0    with (acc, _) -> acc + 1\n767:         max:   MIN  with (acc, x) -> max(acc, x)\n768:     }\n769: ```\n770: \n771: Three folds, one iteration, one struct out. A **fold** is an accumulation over a sequence, so\n772: `sum`, `count` and `max` are all folds. The trick here is that several folds are declared\n773: independently but *run together in one pass*, usually called making folds **applicative**,\n774: meaning they combine without being sequenced. Haskell's `Control.Foldl` is the reference\n775: implementation.\n776: \n777: `Vec` does not need this, since you can simply walk it three times. The construct exists\n778: *because* `Vec` and `Stream` make different promises, which is the type system earning its keep\n779: rather than decorating.\n780: \n781: ### `reduce` and `fold` are different operations\n782: \n783: jq's `reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x)` is a left fold with an explicit accumulator. It is\n784: **order-defined**, and reassociating it changes results, so it cannot be parallelised or\n785: vectorised. Rather than annotate that away, keep two constructs:\n786: \n787: ```\n788: reduce   sequential, order-defined, CPU only.  The accumulator is threaded, and you can see it.\n789: fold     declares its operator associative and commutative.  Order is unspecified.\n790: ```\n791: \n792: The distinction then lives in the source text rather than in a pragma. A reader seeing `fold`\n793: knows the summation order is not promised; a reader seeing `reduce` knows it is. Rust draws the\n794: same line between `Iterator::sum` and `Iterator::fold`.\n795: \n796: This earns its keep on the CPU before any GPU exists. LLVM refuses to vectorise a floating-point\n797: reduction, because reassociation changes results, unless the `reassoc` flag is set on the\n798: instructions. Integer reductions vectorise freely, since integer addition really is associative.\n799: So `fold` is exactly the construct where setting `reassoc` is legitimate, and `reduce` is exactly\n800: where it is not. The language-level distinction and the compiler-level flag are the same\n801: distinction, which is principle 2 holding at the machine level.\n802: \n803: ## Backends, vectorization, and the offload boundary\n804: \n805: ### Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\n806: \n807: The strongest result in this section. A GPU kernel sublanguage is usually specified by listing\n808: what is banned. Stated in this language's own vocabulary it is not a list at all, because each\n809: cardinality corresponds to a known kernel pattern:\n810: \n811: ```\n812: One<T>       exactly one output per input   ->  elementwise map kernel\n813: Opt<T>       zero or one                    ->  stream compaction (prefix sum)\n814: fold                                        ->  reduction\n815: Stream<T>    unbounded, unknown extent      ->  NOT admissible\n816: ```\n817: \n818: So the offload boundary and the layer boundary are the same boundary. Everything in the value\n819: layer is a candidate; the effect layer is exactly what cannot be a kernel. Nothing new has to be\n\n(Showing lines 690-819 of 2495. Use offset=820 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"at least two implementations, the same shape [`Field<K>`](#field-access-is-a-lens) already has for indexable versus\niterable receivers. What would need stating, and is not yet, is the law the two implementations\nhave to share to count as the same trait -- in the spirit of\n[the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\nreification.\"\n\nThe string pattern language is a specialization of exactly this algebra, not a separate feature.\nSpelling repetition as a named combinator call rather than a metacharacter (`mul(\"a\")` instead of\n`a*`) trades density for not needing a second syntax to learn, the same trade this document\nalready made for `and`/`or`/`not` over `&&`/`||`. Named, typed captures need no new mechanism\neither: a capture group decoding to an `Int` is `int(.)` from [Pattern matching is decoding](#pattern-matching-is-decoding) applied to a\ncaptured substring instead of a `Json` field, so \"more specific than string\" is the existing\ncodec syntax, not a new one.\n\nOne consequence is already decided without having been meant to be. [The arm-list section](#pattern-matching-is-decoding) settled\non `//`'s left-to-right, first-match-wins semantics for alternation rather than `,`'s\nexplore-all semantics. That is exactly PEG's defining feature over classical regex/CFG\nalternation: ordered choice, no ambiguity, no need to explore every branch. It is compatible with\nPCRE/Perl-style backtracking regex, which is also priority-ordered, but not with POSIX\nleftmost-longest regex (`grep -E`, `awk`), which is a genuinely different alternation semantics.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","text":"at least two implementations, the same shape [`Field<K>`](#field-access-is-a-lens) already has for indexable versus\niterable receivers. What would need stating, and is not yet, is the law the two implementations\nhave to share to count as the same trait -- in the spirit of\n[the batch-invariance law](#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), but for \"matches the same shape\" rather than \"commutes with\nreification.\"\n\nThe string pattern language is a specialization of exactly this algebra, not a separate feature.\nSpelling repetition as a named combinator call rather than a metacharacter (`mul(\"a\")` instead of\n`a*`) trades density for not needing a second syntax to learn, the same trade this document\nalready made for `and`/`or`/`not` over `&&`/`||`. Named, typed captures need no new mechanism\neither: a capture group decoding to an `Int` is `int(.)` from [Pattern matching is decoding](#pattern-matching-is-decoding) applied to a\ncaptured substring instead of a `Json` field, so \"more specific than string\" is the existing\ncodec syntax, not a new one.\n\nOne consequence is already decided without having been meant to be. [The arm-list section](#pattern-matching-is-decoding) settled\non `//`'s left-to-right, first-match-wins semantics for alternation rather than `,`'s\nexplore-all semantics. That is exactly PEG's defining feature over classical regex/CFG\nalternation: ordered choice, no ambiguity, no need to explore every branch. It is compatible with\nPCRE/Perl-style backtracking regex, which is also priority-ordered, but not with POSIX\nleftmost-longest regex (`grep -E`, `awk`), which is a genuinely different alternation semantics.\nSo \"extends to regular expressions\" should be read as \"extends to PCRE-flavored regex\"\nspecifically -- a real, load-bearing consequence of a decision already made, not a detail to\nleave implicit.\n\nClosest existing prior art for the surface syntax, worth reading before inventing one from\nscratch: Swift's `Regex` builder (named, typed captures composed via a result-builder DSL, fully\ninteroperable with a real regex engine) is close to what the TODO describes, and path templating\nin Express's `path-to-regexp` and Rails routes is close to the \"URL pattern\" framing, including\ntheir convention of embedding a raw regex inside a named segment for cases the friendly syntax\ncannot express (`:id(\\d+)`) -- the same \"friendly by default, escape hatch to full power\" shape\nbeing asked for here.\n\n## Query is search\n\n**Nondeterminism** here does not mean randomness. It means an expression denotes *a set of\npossible answers*, and evaluation explores all of them in a fixed order. This is the sense used\nin Prolog and in nondeterministic automata. Formally it is the *list monad*: a filter maps one\nvalue to a list of results, and `|` chains those lists together.\n\nTwo search terms used below. **Cut** means committing to what you have and abandoning the\nremaining alternatives. **Pruning** means discarding a branch before exploring it.\n\n| operator | search meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `\\|` | bind, so for each choice, go deeper (depth-first descent) |\n| `,` | choice point, so branch wider |\n| `empty` | dead end, backtrack |\n| `first(f)` | cut |\n| `..` | full tree traversal |\n| `select` | pruning |\n| `//` | alternatives, left to right |\n\n```\n..                                 # every node, depth-first\n.. | select(.kind == \"error\")      # prune to matches\nfirst(.. | select(.id == 7))       # stop at the first hit\n.a // .b // \"default\"\n```\n\nA reified search is a result set:\n\n```\nfn diagnostics(tree: Ast) -> Vec<Diag> =\n    [ tree.. | select(.kind == \"error\")\n             | {file: .loc.file, line: .loc.line, msg: .text} ]\n```\n\n## Single-pass composition\n\nA `Stream` can only be walked once, so combining several independent accumulations over one\nstream needs first-class support:\n\n```\nfn stats(xs: Stream<Int>) -> {sum: Int, count: Int, max: Int} =\n    fold xs {\n        sum:   0    with (acc, x) -> acc + x\n        count: 0    with (acc, _) -> acc + 1\n        max:   MIN  with (acc, x) -> max(acc, x)\n    }\n```\n\nThree folds, one iteration, one struct out. A **fold** is an accumulation over a sequence, so\n`sum`, `count` and `max` are all folds. The trick here is that several folds are declared\nindependently but *run together in one pass*, usually called making folds **applicative**,\nmeaning they combine without being sequenced. Haskell's `Control.Foldl` is the reference\nimplementation.\n\n`Vec` does not need this, since you can simply walk it three times. The construct exists\n*because* `Vec` and `Stream` make different promises, which is the type system earning its keep\nrather than decorating.\n\n### `reduce` and `fold` are different operations\n\njq's `reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x)` is a left fold with an explicit accumulator. It is\n**order-defined**, and reassociating it changes results, so it cannot be parallelised or\nvectorised. Rather than annotate that away, keep two constructs:\n\n```\nreduce   sequential, order-defined, CPU only.  The accumulator is threaded, and you can see it.\nfold     declares its operator associative and commutative.  Order is unspecified.\n```\n\nThe distinction then lives in the source text rather than in a pragma. A reader seeing `fold`\nknows the summation order is not promised; a reader seeing `reduce` knows it is. Rust draws the\nsame line between `Iterator::sum` and `Iterator::fold`.\n\nThis earns its keep on the CPU before any GPU exists. LLVM refuses to vectorise a floating-point\nreduction, because reassociation changes results, unless the `reassoc` flag is set on the\ninstructions. Integer reductions vectorise freely, since integer addition really is associative.\nSo `fold` is exactly the construct where setting `reassoc` is legitimate, and `reduce` is exactly\nwhere it is not. The language-level distinction and the compiler-level flag are the same\ndistinction, which is principle 2 holding at the machine level.\n\n## Backends, vectorization, and the offload boundary\n\n### Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\n\nThe strongest result in this section. A GPU kernel sublanguage is usually specified by listing\nwhat is banned. Stated in this language's own vocabulary it is not a list at all, because each\ncardinality corresponds to a known kernel pattern:\n\n```\nOne<T>       exactly one output per input   ->  elementwise map kernel\nOpt<T>       zero or one                    ->  stream compaction (prefix sum)\nfold                                        ->  reduction\nStream<T>    unbounded, unknown extent      ->  NOT admissible\n```\n\nSo the offload boundary and the layer boundary are the same boundary. Everything in the value\nlayer is a candidate; the effect layer is exactly what cannot be a kernel. Nothing new has to be","lineStart":690,"lineEnd":819,"totalLines":2495,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788296889431,"end":1788296889437}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The board row `recursive-descent-order-research` isn't in board.yaml — it may be a row that is planned but not yet written, or the task description refers to it as an intended row. Since my task explicitly says \"board row `recursive-descent-order-research` in plans/board.yaml\" but it doesn't exist, I shouldn't add it (hard constraint: do not touch other files in plans/ besides my new one). So I just do the research and write plans/recursive-descent-order-research.md.\n\nLet me read the 'Query is search' section of draft.md and the surrounding context. Let me read draft.md around line 722.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ecd04540011HqXjkyRl71fVj","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05eccf979001HYpssGwXuWtsfu"}}
