{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296891905,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_9386457ac8c144e08820e300","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","limit":180,"offset":819},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n819: layer is a candidate; the effect layer is exactly what cannot be a kernel. Nothing new has to be\n820: invented to say which programs can run on a GPU, because the cardinality effect already says it.\n821: \n822: What still has to be excluded inside an offloaded region is the ordinary list: strings, objects,\n823: path expressions, update assignment, `error`, and `input`.\n824: \n825: #### This contradicts an earlier claim, and the earlier claim was wrong\n826: \n827: [The vectorizability question](plans/questions.md#q8-is-vectorizability-visible-in-the-type-system-or-a-silent-optimization) was argued on the grounds that cardinality and vectorizability are *orthogonal*: `select`\n828: changes cardinality and vectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way\n829: and cannot vectorize at all. If cardinality is the admissibility predicate, that counterexample\n830: has to go somewhere.\n831: \n832: It goes away, because it equivocates on `first`. There are two of them:\n833: \n834: ```\n835: first over a Stream   must short-circuit over data that has not arrived.  NOT admissible.\n836: first over a Vec      is the minimum index where the mask is set.  A reduction.  Admissible.\n837: ```\n838: \n839: So `first` is not one operation that defies the mapping. It is two operations in different\n840: layers, and only the streaming one is inadmissible, which is exactly what the `Stream` row\n841: already says. Admissibility is determined by the cardinality of what an expression *consumes*,\n842: not only by what it produces. The same resolution covers `any`, `all`, and short-circuiting\n843: `and` and `or`: over a `Vec` each is a reduction over a mask, and over a `Stream` each is an\n844: early exit.\n845: \n846: #### But the mapping needs a precision about granularity\n847: \n848: It describes the cardinality of a filter applied *per element*. It says nothing about operations\n849: on a whole collection, where `Vec -> Vec` is one value in and one value out, and the per-element\n850: reading does not apply.\n851: \n852: `sort` is the clear case. Its cardinality is one-to-one and it is not an elementwise kernel.\n853: Neither are `group_by` or a join. These are the blocking operators, they need the whole input\n854: before producing anything, and they are parallelizable by different means entirely. So the\n855: mapping classifies elementwise filters, and whole-collection operators are a separate question\n856: this document has not addressed.\n857: \n858: ### The admissible input set, and where batching comes from\n859: \n860: A cleaner statement of the boundary, and the one this document adopts. Admissibility is a\n861: property of the **type**, not of the operation:\n862: \n863: ```\n864: admissible    scalars, and anything of known cardinality\n865:               i.e. one pre-allocated buffer plus a little lens metadata\n866: inadmissible  streams.  Compile error, not a silent fallback.\n867: ```\n868: \n869: The lens metadata is what avoids copying. A projection does not have to be materialized before\n870: launch, because the kernel can recompute addresses from the lens parameters on the device. That\n871: is ordinary strided or affine indexing and it is what makes views free rather than merely cheap.\n872: \n873: Streams are rejected outright and must be materialized first. That is the design working rather\n874: than a limitation: **reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source**, so the one\n875: operator that costs memory is the one you have to write down.\n876: \n877: Processing a stream therefore means batching, and the important decision is who does it. Not the\n878: language, invisibly. **The input reader batches**, and its batching scheme appears in the type:\n879: \n880: ```\n881: stdin           Stream<Vec<T>>      batched by the reader, the batching is in the type\n882: your own source Vec<T>              you choose: one big vector, or batch it yourself\n883: ```\n884: \n885: The split between these does not need to reach the surface. It can exist **only at the type\n886: level**: one semantics stated as a trait, with a different implementation when the receiver is a\n887: `Vec` and when it is a `Stream<Vec<T>>`. The same move as `__project__` having one impl for\n888: indexable things and another for iterable ones. A user writes `map(f)` once and the compiler\n889: picks the implementation from the type.\n890: \n891: That is worth more than the ergonomics, because of what it does to batch invariance.\n892: \n893: **The law is that the operation commutes with reification.**\n894: \n895: ```\n896: op(f) . reify   ==   reify . op(f)\n897: ```\n898: \n899: Applying an operation and then collecting gives the same answer as collecting and then applying.\n900: An operation satisfying that cannot observe batching, because reifying at any point in the\n901: pipeline yields the same result. So batch invariance stops being a rule to police separately and\n902: becomes the trait's law, which both implementations have to satisfy in order to be\n903: implementations of the same thing at all. A trait without a stated law is only overloading, and\n904: this is the law.\n905: \n906: **[The blocking-operator question](plans/questions.md#q20-how-are-blocking-operators-sort-group_by-joins-classified) then answers itself.** The blocking operators are exactly those with no lawful stream\n907: implementation. Sorting each batch does not sort the stream, so `sort` cannot satisfy the law\n908: batch-locally. Its options are to have no stream impl, which is a compile error and honest, or\n909: to buffer the whole stream, which silently defeats streaming. `group_by` and joins are the same\n910: shape. So \"blocking operator\" is not a separate category that needed inventing; it is the name\n911: for a trait with a missing instance.\n912: \n913: `first` by contrast does have both, which is consistent with the resolution above: over a `Vec`\n914: it is a minimum index over a mask, over a `Stream` it short-circuits, and both give the same\n915: answer, so the law holds.\n916: \n917: **Cost still differs where the law holds, and that is fine.** `map` over a `Vec` is one launch;\n918: over `Stream<Vec<T>>` it is one per batch. Same result, different performance profile. Symmetry\n919: survives because the *type* still says which implementation was selected, so the cost difference\n920: is visible in the signature rather than hidden in the dispatch.\n921: \n922: ### The primitive set cannot be fold and recursion\n923: \n924: Functional languages usually build everything on a higher-order function plus recursion, and\n925: conventionally that function is `fold`. Every list operation is a catamorphism, which is elegant\n926: and completely sequential. Anything defined that way inherits the sequentiality of its\n927: definition, so a standard library written over `fold` cannot be vectorized no matter what the\n928: backend does. General recursion has the same problem for the same reason.\n929: \n930: So the basis has to be different. The established parallel basis, from Blelloch's work on scans\n931: as primitive parallel operations and used since by NESL and Futhark, is small:\n932: \n933: ```\n934: map          elementwise                         depth 1\n935: scan         prefix sum over an associative op   depth log n\n936: reduce       associative op                      depth log n\n937: gather       permutation by an index vector      depth 1\n938: scatter      inverse permutation                 depth 1\n939:              plus the segmented form of each\n940: ```\n941: \n942: Five operations and their segmented variants. Everything else is built from them: compaction is\n943: a scan followed by a scatter, radix sort is a sequence of scans, and partitioning is compaction\n944: by a predicate. Notice that `fold` with an arbitrary operator is *not* in the set, while `reduce`\n945: with an associative one is. That is the same line the `reduce`/`fold` split already draws, now\n946: determining what the standard library may be defined over rather than only how one operator\n947: compiles.\n948: \n949: The precise characterization of which folds belong is the **third homomorphism theorem**: a\n950: function expressible both as a left fold and as a right fold can be computed by an associative\n951: divide-and-conquer. That is exactly the condition an operator has to meet to earn a place here.\n952: \n953: Recursion splits the same way. **General recursion cannot be flattened**, but *structural*\n954: recursion over a finite structure can, by the flattening transform this document already relies\n955: on: recursion over a tree becomes segmented operations over a flat buffer plus offsets. Which is\n956: also why the segment descriptors are load-bearing rather than an implementation detail.\n957: \n958: Two consequences worth stating plainly.\n959: \n960: **The standard library should be defined over the parallel basis, with `fold` and general\n961: recursion as leaves rather than as the root.** If they sit at the root, every derived operation\n962: inherits a sequential definition and the vectorized path can only ever be a special case that\n963: the compiler recovers by accident.\n964: \n965: **The primitive set and the lawful-stream-instance predicate are the same boundary again.**\n966: Operations definable from the basis have stream implementations that satisfy the commuting law;\n967: operations needing general recursion or a non-associative fold do not. This is the third time a\n968: single distinction has done duty for what looked like separate questions, which is either the\n969: design cohering or a sign that the same idea keeps being renamed.\n970: \n971: The honest cost: expressing a computation as a scan is genuinely less obvious than expressing it\n972: as a fold. `sum` as a fold is immediate; as a scan taking the last element it is indirect. Array\n973: languages pay this and it is a real ergonomic tax, not a free win.\n974: \n975: Four things this exposes.\n976: \n977: **Batch size must not be observable.** The whole design rests on it. If a reader picks the batch\n978: size and a program can tell what it picked, results vary by input source and the semantics stop\n979: being platform-independent. So only batch-invariant operations may run over `Stream<Vec<T>>`.\n980: This is the same move as the string design: UTF-8 and UTF-16 are both allowed precisely because\n981: no program can observe which it got, and batch sizes are allowed to vary precisely because no\n982: program can observe them. Worth noticing that the same technique is now load-bearing twice.\n983: \n984: **Batching requires the associativity declaration.** A fold over a batched stream is a two-level\n985: reduction, within each batch and then across batches, and that is only sound when the operator\n986: is associative. So `fold` and `reduce` are not merely a vectorization nicety; the batched reader\n987: cannot exist without them. `reduce` over a `Stream<Vec<T>>` has to be either rejected or forced\n988: back through a single sequential pass.\n989: \n990: **Known cardinality has two meanings once views exist.** A dense `Vec` knows its extent. A\n991: mask-filtered view knows its *capacity* but its *count* needs a popcount. Both are launchable,\n992: but not by the same path: the second needs a reduction before the output buffer can be sized,\n993: which is precisely the prefix-sum step of stream compaction. So dense and masked probably need\n994: to be distinguishable in the type, since they have different launch preconditions. This is [the select-result question](plans/questions.md#q14-does-select-return-a-masked-view-a-selection-vector-or-a-copy)\n995: arriving from the other side.\n996: \n997: **What `gpu(...)` means on the other backends.** It cannot be a compile error on Lua and\n998: JavaScript without making programs platform-dependent, which the whole design is trying to\n\n(Showing lines 819-998 of 2495. Use offset=999 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"layer is a candidate; the effect layer is exactly what cannot be a kernel. Nothing new has to be\ninvented to say which programs can run on a GPU, because the cardinality effect already says it.\n\nWhat still has to be excluded inside an offloaded region is the ordinary list: strings, objects,\npath expressions, update assignment, `error`, and `input`.\n\n#### This contradicts an earlier claim, and the earlier claim was wrong\n\n[The vectorizability question](plans/questions.md#q8-is-vectorizability-visible-in-the-type-system-or-a-silent-optimization) was argued on the grounds that cardinality and vectorizability are *orthogonal*: `select`\nchanges cardinality and vectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way\nand cannot vectorize at all. If cardinality is the admissibility predicate, that counterexample\nhas to go somewhere.\n\nIt goes away, because it equivocates on `first`. There are two of them:\n\n```\nfirst over a Stream   must short-circuit over data that has not arrived.  NOT admissible.\nfirst over a Vec      is the minimum index where the mask is set.  A reduction.  Admissible.\n```\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","text":"layer is a candidate; the effect layer is exactly what cannot be a kernel. Nothing new has to be\ninvented to say which programs can run on a GPU, because the cardinality effect already says it.\n\nWhat still has to be excluded inside an offloaded region is the ordinary list: strings, objects,\npath expressions, update assignment, `error`, and `input`.\n\n#### This contradicts an earlier claim, and the earlier claim was wrong\n\n[The vectorizability question](plans/questions.md#q8-is-vectorizability-visible-in-the-type-system-or-a-silent-optimization) was argued on the grounds that cardinality and vectorizability are *orthogonal*: `select`\nchanges cardinality and vectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way\nand cannot vectorize at all. If cardinality is the admissibility predicate, that counterexample\nhas to go somewhere.\n\nIt goes away, because it equivocates on `first`. There are two of them:\n\n```\nfirst over a Stream   must short-circuit over data that has not arrived.  NOT admissible.\nfirst over a Vec      is the minimum index where the mask is set.  A reduction.  Admissible.\n```\n\nSo `first` is not one operation that defies the mapping. It is two operations in different\nlayers, and only the streaming one is inadmissible, which is exactly what the `Stream` row\nalready says. Admissibility is determined by the cardinality of what an expression *consumes*,\nnot only by what it produces. The same resolution covers `any`, `all`, and short-circuiting\n`and` and `or`: over a `Vec` each is a reduction over a mask, and over a `Stream` each is an\nearly exit.\n\n#### But the mapping needs a precision about granularity\n\nIt describes the cardinality of a filter applied *per element*. It says nothing about operations\non a whole collection, where `Vec -> Vec` is one value in and one value out, and the per-element\nreading does not apply.\n\n`sort` is the clear case. Its cardinality is one-to-one and it is not an elementwise kernel.\nNeither are `group_by` or a join. These are the blocking operators, they need the whole input\nbefore producing anything, and they are parallelizable by different means entirely. So the\nmapping classifies elementwise filters, and whole-collection operators are a separate question\nthis document has not addressed.\n\n### The admissible input set, and where batching comes from\n\nA cleaner statement of the boundary, and the one this document adopts. Admissibility is a\nproperty of the **type**, not of the operation:\n\n```\nadmissible    scalars, and anything of known cardinality\n              i.e. one pre-allocated buffer plus a little lens metadata\ninadmissible  streams.  Compile error, not a silent fallback.\n```\n\nThe lens metadata is what avoids copying. A projection does not have to be materialized before\nlaunch, because the kernel can recompute addresses from the lens parameters on the device. That\nis ordinary strided or affine indexing and it is what makes views free rather than merely cheap.\n\nStreams are rejected outright and must be materialized first. That is the design working rather\nthan a limitation: **reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source**, so the one\noperator that costs memory is the one you have to write down.\n\nProcessing a stream therefore means batching, and the important decision is who does it. Not the\nlanguage, invisibly. **The input reader batches**, and its batching scheme appears in the type:\n\n```\nstdin           Stream<Vec<T>>      batched by the reader, the batching is in the type\nyour own source Vec<T>              you choose: one big vector, or batch it yourself\n```\n\nThe split between these does not need to reach the surface. It can exist **only at the type\nlevel**: one semantics stated as a trait, with a different implementation when the receiver is a\n`Vec` and when it is a `Stream<Vec<T>>`. The same move as `__project__` having one impl for\nindexable things and another for iterable ones. A user writes `map(f)` once and the compiler\npicks the implementation from the type.\n\nThat is worth more than the ergonomics, because of what it does to batch invariance.\n\n**The law is that the operation commutes with reification.**\n\n```\nop(f) . reify   ==   reify . op(f)\n```\n\nApplying an operation and then collecting gives the same answer as collecting and then applying.\nAn operation satisfying that cannot observe batching, because reifying at any point in the\npipeline yields the same result. So batch invariance stops being a rule to police separately and\nbecomes the trait's law, which both implementations have to satisfy in order to be\nimplementations of the same thing at all. A trait without a stated law is only overloading, and\nthis is the law.\n\n**[The blocking-operator question](plans/questions.md#q20-how-are-blocking-operators-sort-group_by-joins-classified) then answers itself.** The blocking operators are exactly those with no lawful stream\nimplementation. Sorting each batch does not sort the stream, so `sort` cannot satisfy the law\nbatch-locally. Its options are to have no stream impl, which is a compile error and honest, or\nto buffer the whole stream, which silently defeats streaming. `group_by` and joins are the same\nshape. So \"blocking operator\" is not a separate category that needed inventing; it is the name\nfor a trait with a missing instance.\n\n`first` by contrast does have both, which is consistent with the resolution above: over a `Vec`\nit is a minimum index over a mask, over a `Stream` it short-circuits, and both give the same\nanswer, so the law holds.\n\n**Cost still differs where the law holds, and that is fine.** `map` over a `Vec` is one launch;\nover `Stream<Vec<T>>` it is one per batch. Same result, different performance profile. Symmetry\nsurvives because the *type* still says which implementation was selected, so the cost difference\nis visible in the signature rather than hidden in the dispatch.\n\n### The primitive set cannot be fold and recursion\n\nFunctional languages usually build everything on a higher-order function plus recursion, and\nconventionally that function is `fold`. Every list operation is a catamorphism, which is elegant\nand completely sequential. Anything defined that way inherits the sequentiality of its\ndefinition, so a standard library written over `fold` cannot be vectorized no matter what the\nbackend does. General recursion has the same problem for the same reason.\n\nSo the basis has to be different. The established parallel basis, from Blelloch's work on scans\nas primitive parallel operations and used since by NESL and Futhark, is small:\n\n```\nmap          elementwise                         depth 1\nscan         prefix sum over an associative op   depth log n\nreduce       associative op                      depth log n\ngather       permutation by an index vector      depth 1\nscatter      inverse permutation                 depth 1\n             plus the segmented form of each\n```\n\nFive operations and their segmented variants. Everything else is built from them: compaction is\na scan followed by a scatter, radix sort is a sequence of scans, and partitioning is compaction\nby a predicate. Notice that `fold` with an arbitrary operator is *not* in the set, while `reduce`\nwith an associative one is. That is the same line the `reduce`/`fold` split already draws, now\ndetermining what the standard library may be defined over rather than only how one operator\ncompiles.\n\nThe precise characterization of which folds belong is the **third homomorphism theorem**: a\nfunction expressible both as a left fold and as a right fold can be computed by an associative\ndivide-and-conquer. That is exactly the condition an operator has to meet to earn a place here.\n\nRecursion splits the same way. **General recursion cannot be flattened**, but *structural*\nrecursion over a finite structure can, by the flattening transform this document already relies\non: recursion over a tree becomes segmented operations over a flat buffer plus offsets. Which is\nalso why the segment descriptors are load-bearing rather than an implementation detail.\n\nTwo consequences worth stating plainly.\n\n**The standard library should be defined over the parallel basis, with `fold` and general\nrecursion as leaves rather than as the root.** If they sit at the root, every derived operation\ninherits a sequential definition and the vectorized path can only ever be a special case that\nthe compiler recovers by accident.\n\n**The primitive set and the lawful-stream-instance predicate are the same boundary again.**\nOperations definable from the basis have stream implementations that satisfy the commuting law;\noperations needing general recursion or a non-associative fold do not. This is the third time a\nsingle distinction has done duty for what looked like separate questions, which is either the\ndesign cohering or a sign that the same idea keeps being renamed.\n\nThe honest cost: expressing a computation as a scan is genuinely less obvious than expressing it\nas a fold. `sum` as a fold is immediate; as a scan taking the last element it is indirect. Array\nlanguages pay this and it is a real ergonomic tax, not a free win.\n\nFour things this exposes.\n\n**Batch size must not be observable.** The whole design rests on it. If a reader picks the batch\nsize and a program can tell what it picked, results vary by input source and the semantics stop\nbeing platform-independent. So only batch-invariant operations may run over `Stream<Vec<T>>`.\nThis is the same move as the string design: UTF-8 and UTF-16 are both allowed precisely because\nno program can observe which it got, and batch sizes are allowed to vary precisely because no\nprogram can observe them. Worth noticing that the same technique is now load-bearing twice.\n\n**Batching requires the associativity declaration.** A fold over a batched stream is a two-level\nreduction, within each batch and then across batches, and that is only sound when the operator\nis associative. So `fold` and `reduce` are not merely a vectorization nicety; the batched reader\ncannot exist without them. `reduce` over a `Stream<Vec<T>>` has to be either rejected or forced\nback through a single sequential pass.\n\n**Known cardinality has two meanings once views exist.** A dense `Vec` knows its extent. A\nmask-filtered view knows its *capacity* but its *count* needs a popcount. Both are launchable,\nbut not by the same path: the second needs a reduction before the output buffer can be sized,\nwhich is precisely the prefix-sum step of stream compaction. So dense and masked probably need\nto be distinguishable in the type, since they have different launch preconditions. This is [the select-result question](plans/questions.md#q14-does-select-return-a-masked-view-a-selection-vector-or-a-copy)\narriving from the other side.\n\n**What `gpu(...)` means on the other backends.** It cannot be a compile error on Lua and\nJavaScript without making programs platform-dependent, which the whole design is trying to","lineStart":819,"lineEnd":998,"totalLines":2495,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788296891893,"end":1788296891903}},"id":"prt_05ecd0dee001ega1W1mtMP06gj","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd0471001qxgxHciXYIowEF"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296891908,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_e06cc1bd9c97411b970d1a7d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","pattern":"columnar|flat buffer|flat layout|depth-first|depth first"},"output":"Found 35 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/src/check/mod.rs:\n  Line 1576:             // fields stay index-for-index aligned, which is what the columnar backends key on.\n\n  Line 2610: /// not care; the native backend and the columnar Vec layout read it by position, off whichever\n\n  Line 3072:     // order is what the printer and the columnar layouts key on, and both read it off the\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/docs/overview/vision.md:\n  Line 24: thesis (columnar, vectorized, faster than jq's boxed-iterator-per-step ceiling) is a\n\n  Line 26: [recursive descent's cost](../../plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes)\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/enums.md:\n  Line 71: columnar layout cannot represent directly. This is the dense-union problem (Arrow solves it\n\n  Line 80: the columnar-vectorization thesis this backend exists to prove is argued in the draft but not\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md:\n  Line 127: Except that under the columnar model `select` does not either. A predicate over a batch is a\n\n  Line 177: It simplifies [the recursive-descent ordering question](plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes). If `..` is value-layer, producing a collection of nodes, then whether it\n\n  Line 178: promises depth-first order is an ordinary question about how a value is ordered, rather than a\n\n  Line 190: columnar model means by producing a bitmask rather than branching. It also makes `select` a\n\n  Line 554: depth-first traversal today; composing it with a matcher as the predicate is the whole feature:\n\n  Line 562: This does raise the stakes on [whether `..` promises order](plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes). Before, the order it walks in was\n\n  Line 734: | `\\|` | bind, so for each choice, go deeper (depth-first descent) |\n\n  Line 743: ..                                 # every node, depth-first\n\n  Line 955: on: recursion over a tree becomes segmented operations over a flat buffer plus offsets. Which is\n\n  Line 1385: a language whose performance argument is columnar and vectorised, that is the wrong thing to\n\n  Line 1969: a record back as one packed blob, not columnar data, so `tl_read_inputs` spreads it into the\n\n  Line 2171: what a value prints in and what the native/Go columnar layouts key on, and it is meant to become\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/board.yaml:\n  Line 321:   title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needs that research before re-asking. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/prototype_1_5.md:\n  Line 61: columnar question becomes concrete, because choosing a layout for `Vec<T>` is choosing whether a\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/prototype_1_5_step_6.md:\n  Line 27: striding and no copy. Under AoS it is a gather loop. The draft's columnar material, Q7's\n\n  Line 28: \"recursive descent is embarrassingly parallel on a flat layout\" and Q8's vectorizability all\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/questions.md:\n  Line 27: | [Q7](#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes) | Does `..` promise depth-first order, or only the set of nodes? | OPEN |\n\n  Line 146: ### Q7. Does `..` promise depth-first order, or only the set of nodes?\n\n  Line 148: On a flat columnar layout,\n\n  Line 150: parallel. The dependent part is not the traversal but the *order*, since the flat layout is\n\n  Line 151: not in depth-first order. jq promises the order. If this language only promises the set,\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/runtime/toylang.c:\n  Line 856:  * hands back a record as one packed blob rather than already-columnar data.\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/research-log/vec-of-enum-falls-into-the-boxed-default-nobody-chose.md:\n  Line 25: versus Arrow-style tag buffer plus per-variant child columns (columnar, vectorisable, more\n\n  Line 36: and the *columnar* dense union (a tag buffer plus per-variant child columns, Arrow's answer)\n\n  Line 41: columnar layout's payoff has no customer. The gather boundary from\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/research-log/the-lowering-needs-types-the-checker-already-computed.md:\n  Line 44: next one, since anything columnar needs to know the element type to lay it out.\n\n  Line 53: This note guessed the next type-dependent lowering would be columnar layout. It was output.\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/research-log/one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md:\n  Line 68: hands a record back as one packed blob, not columnar data, the same shape `vec_lit`'s own input\n","metadata":{"matches":35,"truncated":false},"title":"columnar|flat buffer|flat layout|depth-first|depth first","time":{"start":1788296891881,"end":1788296891907}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have the core framing: `..` is listed as \"full tree traversal\" with \"every node, depth-first\". Q7 says the question is whether `..` promises depth-first order or only the set of nodes. On a flat columnar layout, \"every node at every depth\" is \"every element of every buffer\", embarrassingly parallel. The dependent part is not the traversal but the *order*. jq promises order.\n\nLet me read more context around the 'Query is search' section and its neighbors — particularly anything about the columnar layout / vectorization, and the \"flat buffers\" and \"offload boundary\". Let me also look at how the design describes the columnar layout. Let me read draft.md sections about vectorization, the admissible input set, flat layout etc.\n\nLet me grep for \"columnar\", \"flat buffer\", \"depth-first\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ecd0de0001rHtgL13YqK56Ve","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd0471001qxgxHciXYIowEF"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788296891922,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd0e0c001766kblPrgFYNLG","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","messageID":"msg_05ecd0471001qxgxHciXYIowEF","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":43579,"input":2256,"output":162,"reasoning":201,"cache":{"write":0,"read":40960}},"cost":0.00086734}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788296893024,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd125c0012tOnCUGT5XQH2h","messageID":"msg_05ecd0e1d001BMFV0AIBIhjUvN","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296893936,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c1f6e677064947bab20fdcae","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","limit":90,"offset":110},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n110: the effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\n111: and everything with statically known extent stays a value, which is also exactly what\n112: vectorizes. But the claim \"exactly two layer shifters\" does not survive it unchanged.\n113: \n114: --> but is this a true contradiction, or is this somethign that actually a trait that is implemented\n115: over everythign that has the index trait, and separately over anythign that has the iteration trait?\n116: \n117: but perhaps the implementations on both can actuallyb e slightly different, as there may be some edge cases that do not work in both\n118: \n119: RESPONSE (agent). The trait framing works, and it resolves more than it claims. Not \"this is\n120: not a contradiction\" but \"`.[]` was never the layer shifter in the first place.\"\n121: \n122: Follow it through. If `__project__` has an impl for indexable things and another for iterable\n123: ones, then `Vec[]` returns a `Vec` view and stays in the value layer. So `.[]` does not create\n124: effect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n125: `..`, and `select`.\n126: \n127: Except that under the columnar model `select` does not either. A predicate over a batch is a\n128: mask, and masking a `Vec` gives a `Vec`. So it also stays in the value layer.\n129: \n130: Keep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\n131: unknown because it has not arrived yet. That is a much smaller and much more defensible notion\n132: than \"anything with more than one value\", and it would mean there is one layer shifter rather\n133: than two, running one way, `[...]` collapsing a stream into a value.\n134: \n135: The two impls differing is fine and expected. `Vec[3]` is constant time and yields a view;\n136: `Stream[3]` consumes three items and cannot yield a view. Different cost, different guarantee,\n137: same trait. Symmetry is satisfied because the type says which one you got.\n138: \n139: ### PROPOSAL: the layer shift only runs one way\n140: \n141: If `.[]` is no longer the reflect operator, the obvious next move is to find a replacement. Two\n142: candidates came up, and there may be a third that is better than both.\n143: \n144: **Projection yields an iterator of lenses.** Fails for the reason it was raised with: an\n145: iterator does not carry its length in its type, so cardinality is lost the moment you have one.\n146: Recovering it would need the length in the type, `Iter<T, n>`, which is dependent typing.\n147: \n148: **Keep `.[]` as the lossy layer shifter, add separate syntax for the cardinality-preserving\n149: lens.** This works, and it is honest: these are two different operations that jq conflates only\n150: because jq has neither lenses nor cardinality tracking. But it puts the *default* on the wrong\n151: side. The familiar, short, habit-forming spelling would be the one that throws information away,\n152: and the information-preserving one would need a deliberate reach. That inverts principle 1.\n153: \n154: **Third option: there is no value-to-effect shifter, because it would only destroy\n155: information.** Ask what converting a `Vec` into effect multiplicity actually buys. Effect-layer\n156: multiplicity exists to describe extent that is not yet known. A `Vec` already knows its extent.\n157: Degrading it forgets that and returns nothing in exchange, since laziness cannot help with data\n158: that is already materialized.\n159: \n160: Under this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\n161: arrived yet, and *dies* into values through `[...]`. It never arises from a value. There is one\n162: layer shifter, running one direction, and the absence of the other direction is not a gap.\n163: \n164: Check it against real pipelines. `vec | map(f) | select(p)` is value layer throughout. `first(vec\n165: | select(p))` is `Vec -> Vec -> Opt`, still value layer. `stdin.lines | select(p) | [...]` is\n166: born effect, collapsed to a value. Writing a `Vec` to stdout iterates internally and needs no\n167: type-level stream. Avoiding an intermediate materialization is fusion, which is a compiler\n168: concern and does not change the type.\n169: \n170: Two consequences if this holds.\n171: \n172: It answers [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity). If effect multiplicity is only ever born from I/O and only ever collapses into\n173: values, there is no need for a `Stream<T>` *type* at all. `Stream` becomes an effect annotation\n174: on an expression rather than a type constructor, which is the evaluation-level answer arriving\n175: for a fourth time.\n176: \n177: It simplifies [the recursive-descent ordering question](plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes). If `..` is value-layer, producing a collection of nodes, then whether it\n178: promises depth-first order is an ordinary question about how a value is ordered, rather than a\n179: question about evaluation strategy.\n180: \n181: The case that would break it: any value with genuinely unknown extent. That is exactly what a\n182: first-class `Stream` value would be, so [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) and this proposal stand or fall together.\n183: \n184: ### Does a value-layer `select` copy?\n185: \n186: Asked of `vec | map(f) | select(p)`, which the section above claims never leaves the value\n187: layer. If `select` returns a `Vec`, does it allocate a filtered one?\n188: \n189: No. It returns the original buffer plus a **mask**, so no elements move. That is what the\n190: columnar model means by producing a bitmask rather than branching. It also makes `select` a\n191: projection, projected by a boolean mask instead of an index vector, which brings it under the\n192: same `__project__` trait as `[]`.\n193: \n194: But it breaks the promise that defines `Vec`. `Vec` is \"indexing without iteration\", and\n195: indexing a mask-filtered view is not constant time: finding the seventh surviving element means\n196: finding the seventh set bit, which is a rank query, and rank is O(n) unless you build an index\n197: for it.\n198: \n199: The two standard representations trade off differently, and both are used:\n\n(Showing lines 110-199 of 2495. Use offset=200 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"the effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\nand everything with statically known extent stays a value, which is also exactly what\nvectorizes. But the claim \"exactly two layer shifters\" does not survive it unchanged.\n\n--> but is this a true contradiction, or is this somethign that actually a trait that is implemented\nover everythign that has the index trait, and separately over anythign that has the iteration trait?\n\nbut perhaps the implementations on both can actuallyb e slightly different, as there may be some edge cases that do not work in both\n\nRESPONSE (agent). The trait framing works, and it resolves more than it claims. Not \"this is\nnot a contradiction\" but \"`.[]` was never the layer shifter in the first place.\"\n\nFollow it through. If `__project__` has an impl for indexable things and another for iterable\nones, then `Vec[]` returns a `Vec` view and stays in the value layer. So `.[]` does not create\neffect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n`..`, and `select`.\n\nExcept that under the columnar model `select` does not either. A predicate over a batch is a\nmask, and masking a `Vec` gives a `Vec`. So it also stays in the value layer.\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","text":"the effect layer would then arise only from genuinely unknown cardinality (I/O, `select`, `..`),\nand everything with statically known extent stays a value, which is also exactly what\nvectorizes. But the claim \"exactly two layer shifters\" does not survive it unchanged.\n\n--> but is this a true contradiction, or is this somethign that actually a trait that is implemented\nover everythign that has the index trait, and separately over anythign that has the iteration trait?\n\nbut perhaps the implementations on both can actuallyb e slightly different, as there may be some edge cases that do not work in both\n\nRESPONSE (agent). The trait framing works, and it resolves more than it claims. Not \"this is\nnot a contradiction\" but \"`.[]` was never the layer shifter in the first place.\"\n\nFollow it through. If `__project__` has an impl for indexable things and another for iterable\nones, then `Vec[]` returns a `Vec` view and stays in the value layer. So `.[]` does not create\neffect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n`..`, and `select`.\n\nExcept that under the columnar model `select` does not either. A predicate over a batch is a\nmask, and masking a `Vec` gives a `Vec`. So it also stays in the value layer.\n\nKeep pulling and the effect layer shrinks to one thing: genuine streaming input whose length is\nunknown because it has not arrived yet. That is a much smaller and much more defensible notion\nthan \"anything with more than one value\", and it would mean there is one layer shifter rather\nthan two, running one way, `[...]` collapsing a stream into a value.\n\nThe two impls differing is fine and expected. `Vec[3]` is constant time and yields a view;\n`Stream[3]` consumes three items and cannot yield a view. Different cost, different guarantee,\nsame trait. Symmetry is satisfied because the type says which one you got.\n\n### PROPOSAL: the layer shift only runs one way\n\nIf `.[]` is no longer the reflect operator, the obvious next move is to find a replacement. Two\ncandidates came up, and there may be a third that is better than both.\n\n**Projection yields an iterator of lenses.** Fails for the reason it was raised with: an\niterator does not carry its length in its type, so cardinality is lost the moment you have one.\nRecovering it would need the length in the type, `Iter<T, n>`, which is dependent typing.\n\n**Keep `.[]` as the lossy layer shifter, add separate syntax for the cardinality-preserving\nlens.** This works, and it is honest: these are two different operations that jq conflates only\nbecause jq has neither lenses nor cardinality tracking. But it puts the *default* on the wrong\nside. The familiar, short, habit-forming spelling would be the one that throws information away,\nand the information-preserving one would need a deliberate reach. That inverts principle 1.\n\n**Third option: there is no value-to-effect shifter, because it would only destroy\ninformation.** Ask what converting a `Vec` into effect multiplicity actually buys. Effect-layer\nmultiplicity exists to describe extent that is not yet known. A `Vec` already knows its extent.\nDegrading it forgets that and returns nothing in exchange, since laziness cannot help with data\nthat is already materialized.\n\nUnder this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\narrived yet, and *dies* into values through `[...]`. It never arises from a value. There is one\nlayer shifter, running one direction, and the absence of the other direction is not a gap.\n\nCheck it against real pipelines. `vec | map(f) | select(p)` is value layer throughout. `first(vec\n| select(p))` is `Vec -> Vec -> Opt`, still value layer. `stdin.lines | select(p) | [...]` is\nborn effect, collapsed to a value. Writing a `Vec` to stdout iterates internally and needs no\ntype-level stream. Avoiding an intermediate materialization is fusion, which is a compiler\nconcern and does not change the type.\n\nTwo consequences if this holds.\n\nIt answers [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity). If effect multiplicity is only ever born from I/O and only ever collapses into\nvalues, there is no need for a `Stream<T>` *type* at all. `Stream` becomes an effect annotation\non an expression rather than a type constructor, which is the evaluation-level answer arriving\nfor a fourth time.\n\nIt simplifies [the recursive-descent ordering question](plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes). If `..` is value-layer, producing a collection of nodes, then whether it\npromises depth-first order is an ordinary question about how a value is ordered, rather than a\nquestion about evaluation strategy.\n\nThe case that would break it: any value with genuinely unknown extent. That is exactly what a\nfirst-class `Stream` value would be, so [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) and this proposal stand or fall together.\n\n### Does a value-layer `select` copy?\n\nAsked of `vec | map(f) | select(p)`, which the section above claims never leaves the value\nlayer. If `select` returns a `Vec`, does it allocate a filtered one?\n\nNo. It returns the original buffer plus a **mask**, so no elements move. That is what the\ncolumnar model means by producing a bitmask rather than branching. It also makes `select` a\nprojection, projected by a boolean mask instead of an index vector, which brings it under the\nsame `__project__` trait as `[]`.\n\nBut it breaks the promise that defines `Vec`. `Vec` is \"indexing without iteration\", and\nindexing a mask-filtered view is not constant time: finding the seventh surviving element means\nfinding the seventh set bit, which is a rank query, and rank is O(n) unless you build an index\nfor it.\n\nThe two standard representations trade off differently, and both are used:","lineStart":110,"lineEnd":199,"totalLines":2495,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788296893906,"end":1788296893933}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read the sections around lines 127, 177-190, 554-562 of draft.md — they directly discuss recursive descent order.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ecd15ca001HCiP1ueGI6Fkum","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd0e1d001BMFV0AIBIhjUvN"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296893936,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e4dcc8f8aba6422abef6795e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","limit":50,"offset":540},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n540: size, only one of which this idea actually needs.\n541: \n542: Naming a subset of fields against `Json` costs nothing extra, because `Json`'s fields were already\n543: `Opt<Json>` -- structurally open -- before matching existed. `{name: str(.name), age: int(.age)}`\n544: builds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\n545: because the pattern never claimed those other keys mattered. No rest-marker is needed on this\n546: side; openness already said \"there may be more.\"\n547: \n548: Naming a subset of fields against a value of an already-closed type is a different claim, and does\n549: need a marker, borrowed from Rust: `User { name, .. } -> ...`. Without `..`, leaving fields out of\n550: a match against a closed type should be a compile error by default, the same exhaustiveness\n551: guarantee an enum match gets. `..` is what turns \"I forgot a field\" into \"I meant to ignore it.\"\n552: \n553: Path-independent matching is already expressible with what the document has. [`..`](#query-is-search) is full\n554: depth-first traversal today; composing it with a matcher as the predicate is the whole feature:\n555: \n556: ```\n557: tree.. | int(.) -> .        # every Int anywhere in the tree, whatever its path\n558: ```\n559: \n560: No new operator, because the entire point of `..` was already \"I don't want to name the path.\"\n561: \n562: This does raise the stakes on [whether `..` promises order](plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes). Before, the order it walks in was\n563: mostly a performance question. Once `..` is the mechanism for \"find this shape somewhere,\" two\n564: runs disagreeing on order is a correctness problem for whatever consumes the result, not only a\n565: speed one. Worth folding into that question's detail rather than opening a new one.\n566: \n567: \"Aliasing\" a submatch so later code can refer to it by name is also already in the document, just\n568: not yet generalized. `(\"red\",\"blue\") as $c` in [the record-forming-update section](#undecided-what-to-call-the-record-forming-update) already binds a\n569: whole expression's result to a name; extending `as` to a matched submatch is the same binding,\n570: applied one level deeper:\n571: \n572: ```\n573: {x: int(.x), y: int(.y)} as point -> point.x + point.y\n574: ```\n575: \n576: This is an as-pattern (Rust and Haskell both have one), not new machinery.\n577: \n578: What \"aliasing\" does not cover, and should not be assumed to, is unifying two *separate* deep\n579: matches against each other -- finding a node `A` somewhere and a node `B` elsewhere such that `B`\n580: refers to `A`. [Query is search](#query-is-search) already grounds this document's nondeterminism in Prolog's\n581: vocabulary, so the connection is real, but that feature is full logic-variable unification with\n582: backtracking over bindings, which is a much larger commitment than binding one submatch to one\n583: name. Tracked below as its own open question rather than folded into this one.\n584: \n585: #### The constructor should be free, and that reverses a dependency claimed above\n586: \n587: TODO (user): Not every type needs a JSON codec, but every way of declaring a new type -- struct,\n588: enum -- could auto-generate at minimum the plain constructor that already exists implicitly in\n589: the declaration: the function from the declared field types to a value of the declared type. If,\n\n(Showing lines 540-589 of 2495. Use offset=590 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"size, only one of which this idea actually needs.\n\nNaming a subset of fields against `Json` costs nothing extra, because `Json`'s fields were already\n`Opt<Json>` -- structurally open -- before matching existed. `{name: str(.name), age: int(.age)}`\nbuilds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\nbecause the pattern never claimed those other keys mattered. No rest-marker is needed on this\nside; openness already said \"there may be more.\"\n\nNaming a subset of fields against a value of an already-closed type is a different claim, and does\nneed a marker, borrowed from Rust: `User { name, .. } -> ...`. Without `..`, leaving fields out of\na match against a closed type should be a compile error by default, the same exhaustiveness\nguarantee an enum match gets. `..` is what turns \"I forgot a field\" into \"I meant to ignore it.\"\n\nPath-independent matching is already expressible with what the document has. [`..`](#query-is-search) is full\ndepth-first traversal today; composing it with a matcher as the predicate is the whole feature:\n\n```\ntree.. | int(.) -> .        # every Int anywhere in the tree, whatever its path\n```\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/draft.md","text":"size, only one of which this idea actually needs.\n\nNaming a subset of fields against `Json` costs nothing extra, because `Json`'s fields were already\n`Opt<Json>` -- structurally open -- before matching existed. `{name: str(.name), age: int(.age)}`\nbuilds exactly a two-field record and silently ignores every other key in the source object,\nbecause the pattern never claimed those other keys mattered. No rest-marker is needed on this\nside; openness already said \"there may be more.\"\n\nNaming a subset of fields against a value of an already-closed type is a different claim, and does\nneed a marker, borrowed from Rust: `User { name, .. } -> ...`. Without `..`, leaving fields out of\na match against a closed type should be a compile error by default, the same exhaustiveness\nguarantee an enum match gets. `..` is what turns \"I forgot a field\" into \"I meant to ignore it.\"\n\nPath-independent matching is already expressible with what the document has. [`..`](#query-is-search) is full\ndepth-first traversal today; composing it with a matcher as the predicate is the whole feature:\n\n```\ntree.. | int(.) -> .        # every Int anywhere in the tree, whatever its path\n```\n\nNo new operator, because the entire point of `..` was already \"I don't want to name the path.\"\n\nThis does raise the stakes on [whether `..` promises order](plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes). Before, the order it walks in was\nmostly a performance question. Once `..` is the mechanism for \"find this shape somewhere,\" two\nruns disagreeing on order is a correctness problem for whatever consumes the result, not only a\nspeed one. Worth folding into that question's detail rather than opening a new one.\n\n\"Aliasing\" a submatch so later code can refer to it by name is also already in the document, just\nnot yet generalized. `(\"red\",\"blue\") as $c` in [the record-forming-update section](#undecided-what-to-call-the-record-forming-update) already binds a\nwhole expression's result to a name; extending `as` to a matched submatch is the same binding,\napplied one level deeper:\n\n```\n{x: int(.x), y: int(.y)} as point -> point.x + point.y\n```\n\nThis is an as-pattern (Rust and Haskell both have one), not new machinery.\n\nWhat \"aliasing\" does not cover, and should not be assumed to, is unifying two *separate* deep\nmatches against each other -- finding a node `A` somewhere and a node `B` elsewhere such that `B`\nrefers to `A`. [Query is search](#query-is-search) already grounds this document's nondeterminism in Prolog's\nvocabulary, so the connection is real, but that feature is full logic-variable unification with\nbacktracking over bindings, which is a much larger commitment than binding one submatch to one\nname. Tracked below as its own open question rather than folded into this one.\n\n#### The constructor should be free, and that reverses a dependency claimed above\n\nTODO (user): Not every type needs a JSON codec, but every way of declaring a new type -- struct,\nenum -- could auto-generate at minimum the plain constructor that already exists implicitly in\nthe declaration: the function from the declared field types to a value of the declared type. If,","lineStart":540,"lineEnd":589,"totalLines":2495,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788296893918,"end":1788296893934}},"id":"prt_05ecd15d70019chn1WhJ60TYpw","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd0e1d001BMFV0AIBIhjUvN"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788296893949,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd15fa001nPMuuk8yZW0v3p","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","messageID":"msg_05ecd0e1d001BMFV0AIBIhjUvN","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":48235,"input":4754,"output":186,"reasoning":31,"cache":{"write":0,"read":43264}},"cost":0.001040294}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788296894746,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd1913001XzrWhucDCAw0Mc","messageID":"msg_05ecd1605001HaWaOZerGD1Z4l","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296897571,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_bc73c5ac84c64601b72517ee","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/prototype_1_5_step_6.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/prototype_1_5_step_6.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Step 6: Vec, records, input, select, field access\n2: \n3: The rest of prototype 1, natively, and the step where the interesting decisions are.\n4: \n5: ## Records and input arrive here\n6: \n7: Moved from step 5, because a record cannot be built: the language has no record literal, so the\n8: only record value that exists comes from `input`. That makes records inseparable from parsing\n9: JSON inside the compiled binary, and doing it here unlocks four corpus programs rather than one.\n10: \n11: The JSON parser goes in `runtime/toylang.c` alongside the rest of the runtime. It can be\n12: narrower than a general parser, because the checker already knows the exact type the input must\n13: have and the Rust side already rejects anything that does not match before the binary ever runs.\n14: Whether the binary re-validates or trusts a pre-checked shape is open, and it decides whether\n15: `./adults < data.json` works standalone or only under the harness. It should work standalone.\n16: \n17: Acceptance is `examples/adults.toy`, built to a binary, reading the same JSON on stdin and\n18: printing `[\"ada\"]` -- agreeing with Lua and JavaScript through the step 3 harness.\n19: \n20: ## Layout: struct of arrays\n21: \n22: **Decided: struct of arrays.** A `Vec<Record>` is one array per field sharing a length, not an\n23: array of structs.\n24: \n25: The reason is not raw speed, it is that it makes the language's own operators cheap. `.name` on a\n26: `Vec<User>` under SoA is the name column: a header pointing at bytes that already exist, no\n27: striding and no copy. Under AoS it is a gather loop. The draft's columnar material, Q7's\n28: \"recursive descent is embarrassingly parallel on a flat layout\" and Q8's vectorizability all\n29: assume this layout, so choosing AoS here would have quietly cost a chunk of the design's own\n30: argument.\n31: \n32: What makes it affordable is a property of the language as it stands: **nothing extracts a single\n33: element from a `Vec`.** There is no `.[i]`, `[]` is the identity, `select` returns a `Vec`, and\n34: `.field` returns a column. So the AoS/SoA boundary -- gathering columns back into one struct --\n35: never has to be crossed, and the usual reason SoA is painful does not apply yet. It will the day\n36: an indexing operator arrives.\n37: \n38: `select` therefore compiles to a loop over indices where `.` is not a value but a cursor into the\n39: columns, so `.age >= 18` reads `ages[i]` rather than materialising a record. That is the form\n40: that vectorises, and it arrives by construction rather than as an optimisation.\n41: \n42: The one place a whole element is needed turned out to be printing, since rendering an object\n43: requires every field at once. That is one gather in the whole backend, and it exists for output\n44: rather than for any language feature. A better outcome than the general case, and a more honest\n45: claim than \"no gather ever\".\n46: \n47: Ragged nesting is the part SoA does not answer. `Vec<Vec<T>>` is a column of pointers to inner\n48: `Vec` headers, not Arrow-style offsets. Offsets are the eventual answer and the draft already\n49: says so; a pointer column is the cheap version that works and is worth naming as a known\n50: placeholder rather than a design.\n51: \n52: ## Split in two\n53: \n54: **6a: `Vec` of scalars.** Literal, `select`, printing, `Vec` through functions. Unlocks seven\n55: corpus programs. Needs no layout decision at all, since a `Vec<Int>` or `Vec<Str>` is one array\n56: either way.\n57: \n58: **6b: records, input, field access.** Where SoA actually applies, and where the JSON parser\n59: lands. Unlocks the remaining four.\n60: \n61: ## Allocation, and the decision not to make\n62: \n63: `select` allocates: it produces a new `Vec` whose length is not known until the predicate has\n64: run. So does string concatenation, from step 5.\n65: \n66: Prototype 1.5 should **leak deliberately**. Allocate with `malloc`, never free, and say so. The\n67: alternative is picking between refcounting and tracing here, and that decision belongs with the\n68: mutation model and the copy-on-write thread in the draft, which are the same decision viewed from\n69: another side. A prototype that runs one program and exits loses nothing by leaking, and a\n70: half-built refcount would be exactly the kind of deferred-work-done-badly the plan avoids\n71: elsewhere.\n72: \n73: Q14 asks whether a value-layer `select` copies or masks. This step implements the copy, because\n74: that is the simple thing, and it is the step that would produce real evidence for the question\n75: if the mask were tried instead. Worth revisiting once the harness can measure.\n76: \n77: ## Field access over a Vec\n78: \n79: The construct that forced the typed IR. Natively it is a loop that reads one field out of each\n80: element into a fresh `Vec`, with the element type known at compile time. If the typed IR from\n81: step 1 was done right, this needs no lookaside table and no runtime inspection of any value.\n82: \n83: That is the check on step 1: if anything here has to ask what a value is at runtime, the typed IR\n84: did not go far enough.\n\n(End of file - total 84 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Step 6: Vec, records, input, select, field access\n\nThe rest of prototype 1, natively, and the step where the interesting decisions are.\n\n## Records and input arrive here\n\nMoved from step 5, because a record cannot be built: the language has no record literal, so the\nonly record value that exists comes from `input`. That makes records inseparable from parsing\nJSON inside the compiled binary, and doing it here unlocks four corpus programs rather than one.\n\nThe JSON parser goes in `runtime/toylang.c` alongside the rest of the runtime. It can be\nnarrower than a general parser, because the checker already knows the exact type the input must\nhave and the Rust side already rejects anything that does not match before the binary ever runs.\nWhether the binary re-validates or trusts a pre-checked shape is open, and it decides whether\n`./adults < data.json` works standalone or only under the harness. It should work standalone.\n\nAcceptance is `examples/adults.toy`, built to a binary, reading the same JSON on stdin and\nprinting `[\"ada\"]` -- agreeing with Lua and JavaScript through the step 3 harness.\n\n## Layout: struct of arrays","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/plans/prototype_1_5_step_6.md","text":"# Step 6: Vec, records, input, select, field access\n\nThe rest of prototype 1, natively, and the step where the interesting decisions are.\n\n## Records and input arrive here\n\nMoved from step 5, because a record cannot be built: the language has no record literal, so the\nonly record value that exists comes from `input`. That makes records inseparable from parsing\nJSON inside the compiled binary, and doing it here unlocks four corpus programs rather than one.\n\nThe JSON parser goes in `runtime/toylang.c` alongside the rest of the runtime. It can be\nnarrower than a general parser, because the checker already knows the exact type the input must\nhave and the Rust side already rejects anything that does not match before the binary ever runs.\nWhether the binary re-validates or trusts a pre-checked shape is open, and it decides whether\n`./adults < data.json` works standalone or only under the harness. It should work standalone.\n\nAcceptance is `examples/adults.toy`, built to a binary, reading the same JSON on stdin and\nprinting `[\"ada\"]` -- agreeing with Lua and JavaScript through the step 3 harness.\n\n## Layout: struct of arrays\n\n**Decided: struct of arrays.** A `Vec<Record>` is one array per field sharing a length, not an\narray of structs.\n\nThe reason is not raw speed, it is that it makes the language's own operators cheap. `.name` on a\n`Vec<User>` under SoA is the name column: a header pointing at bytes that already exist, no\nstriding and no copy. Under AoS it is a gather loop. The draft's columnar material, Q7's\n\"recursive descent is embarrassingly parallel on a flat layout\" and Q8's vectorizability all\nassume this layout, so choosing AoS here would have quietly cost a chunk of the design's own\nargument.\n\nWhat makes it affordable is a property of the language as it stands: **nothing extracts a single\nelement from a `Vec`.** There is no `.[i]`, `[]` is the identity, `select` returns a `Vec`, and\n`.field` returns a column. So the AoS/SoA boundary -- gathering columns back into one struct --\nnever has to be crossed, and the usual reason SoA is painful does not apply yet. It will the day\nan indexing operator arrives.\n\n`select` therefore compiles to a loop over indices where `.` is not a value but a cursor into the\ncolumns, so `.age >= 18` reads `ages[i]` rather than materialising a record. That is the form\nthat vectorises, and it arrives by construction rather than as an optimisation.\n\nThe one place a whole element is needed turned out to be printing, since rendering an object\nrequires every field at once. That is one gather in the whole backend, and it exists for output\nrather than for any language feature. A better outcome than the general case, and a more honest\nclaim than \"no gather ever\".\n\nRagged nesting is the part SoA does not answer. `Vec<Vec<T>>` is a column of pointers to inner\n`Vec` headers, not Arrow-style offsets. Offsets are the eventual answer and the draft already\nsays so; a pointer column is the cheap version that works and is worth naming as a known\nplaceholder rather than a design.\n\n## Split in two\n\n**6a: `Vec` of scalars.** Literal, `select`, printing, `Vec` through functions. Unlocks seven\ncorpus programs. Needs no layout decision at all, since a `Vec<Int>` or `Vec<Str>` is one array\neither way.\n\n**6b: records, input, field access.** Where SoA actually applies, and where the JSON parser\nlands. Unlocks the remaining four.\n\n## Allocation, and the decision not to make\n\n`select` allocates: it produces a new `Vec` whose length is not known until the predicate has\nrun. So does string concatenation, from step 5.\n\nPrototype 1.5 should **leak deliberately**. Allocate with `malloc`, never free, and say so. The\nalternative is picking between refcounting and tracing here, and that decision belongs with the\nmutation model and the copy-on-write thread in the draft, which are the same decision viewed from\nanother side. A prototype that runs one program and exits loses nothing by leaking, and a\nhalf-built refcount would be exactly the kind of deferred-work-done-badly the plan avoids\nelsewhere.\n\nQ14 asks whether a value-layer `select` copies or masks. This step implements the copy, because\nthat is the simple thing, and it is the step that would produce real evidence for the question\nif the mask were tried instead. Worth revisiting once the harness can measure.\n\n## Field access over a Vec\n\nThe construct that forced the typed IR. Natively it is a loop that reads one field out of each\nelement into a fresh `Vec`, with the element type known at compile time. If the typed IR from\nstep 1 was done right, this needs no lookaside table and no runtime inspection of any value.\n\nThat is the check on step 1: if anything here has to ask what a value is at runtime, the typed IR\ndid not go far enough.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":84,"totalLines":84,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/prototype_1_5_step_6.md","time":{"start":1788296897566,"end":1788296897570}},"id":"prt_05ecd241c001g32YPUy7SFxkvN","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd1605001HaWaOZerGD1Z4l"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296897573,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_a794f097d79c4b1ea1cb4d65","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/docs/overview/vision.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/docs/overview/vision.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # What toylang is\n2: \n3: A compiled, statically typed language derived from the jq family -- a jq dialect that keeps\n4: jq's semantics as its reference while extending and generalizing them, aspiring to be a real\n5: language rather than a study.\n6: \n7: - **Data-oriented.** JSON is the native value model, not a library, and data orientation is\n8:   the organizing principle: transformation, selection, and querying of data are how programs\n9:   are structured, not one feature among many.\n10: - **Compiled**, with seven backends (native/LLVM, JavaScript, Lua, jq, Go, Python, Rust) --\n11:   kept as falsifiers of the design rather than as a compatibility promise\n12:   ([ADR 0002](../adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md)).\n13: - **Rust-like syntax**, aiming at a version of Go's simplicity without following it into the\n14:   absurd: where Go's refusals cost too much, ideas come from Rust instead. Enums are the\n15:   first such import.\n16: - **The ambition is general-purpose** -- eventually HTTP servers, GUIs, web frontends, shell\n17:   scripts -- but through a deliberate sequence. The beachhead is CLI data transformation,\n18:   where the dogfood test is concrete: it replaces jq in its author's own shell. Shell\n19:   scripting is the first area to expand into after that. Result-set-oriented tooling (an\n20:   editor whose buffer is a query result) stays on the long horizon, and nearer-term design\n21:   decisions must not foreclose it.\n22: \n23: Aspiring to be real puts weight on claims that a study could leave as prose: the performance\n24: thesis (columnar, vectorized, faster than jq's boxed-iterator-per-step ceiling) is a\n25: commitment that will eventually owe benchmarks, and positioning worries like\n26: [recursive descent's cost](../../plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes)\n27: are real product concerns, not rhetorical ones.\n28: \n29: The front end is written from scratch\n30: ([`plans/prototype_1.md`](../../plans/prototype_1.md)); an earlier plan to fork jaq's front\n31: end and replace only its interpreter was dropped, because jaq's parser and IR encode jq's\n32: surface syntax, which a Rust-like syntax does not survive, and the jq-conformance corpus that\n33: plan leaned on is a non-goal. jq stays a reference for semantics, not a conformance target.\n34: \n35: ## Two guiding principles\n36: \n37: **1. Do not erase boundaries.** When a structural distinction exists, keep it in the type\n38: rather than flattening it away. If a computation crosses a boundary, the crossing is written\n39: down. Corollaries appear throughout: `[...]` is an explicit operator rather than an implicit\n40: coercion, a tuple of streams beats a concatenated stream with a phantom type parameter, and\n41: `Json` is a named type rather than a permissive escape hatch.\n42: \n43: **2. Symmetry.** The type-level guarantee and the runtime guarantee must be the *same*\n44: guarantee. If the type system claims two things are distinguishable, the runtime must be able\n45: to distinguish them, and vice versa. A guarantee that exists only at one level is a bug in\n46: the design.\n47: \n48: ## Values\n49: \n50: ```\n51: null   true   42   3.14   \"text\"   [1, 2, 3]   {name: \"ada\", age: 36}\n52: ```\n53: \n54: The JSON value forms, with `Str` a real string type rather than an untyped blob.\n55: \n56: ## Two worked programs\n57: \n58: Vision, not documentation: these use features the language does not build yet (`fold {} with`,\n59: `^.`, `on \"]q\"`). They show the direction, not what runs today.\n60: \n61: Shell, counting errors per service, streaming, in constant memory:\n62: \n63: ```\n64: #!/usr/bin/env toylang\n65: stdin.lines\n66:   | parse_json?                              # skip malformed lines\n67:   | select(.level == \"ERROR\")\n68:   | fold {} with (acc, e) -> acc[e.service] += 1\n69:   | to_entries | sort_by(-.value) | .[]\n70:   | \"\\(.key)\\t\\(.value)\"\n71: ```\n72: \n73: Editor, where the buffer is a query result:\n74: \n75: ```\n76: fn view(project: Project) -> Vec<Row> =\n77:     [ project.files[]\n78:       | .diagnostics[]\n79:       | select(.severity >= WARN)\n80:       | {file: ^.path, line: .line, text: .msg} ]\n81: \n82: on \"]q\" -> cursor.next()\n83: on \"gf\" -> open(cursor.get().file, cursor.get().line)\n84: ```\n85: \n86: There is no buffer, only a reified search.\n87: \n88: ## Non-goals\n89: \n90: - JavaScript semantic compatibility. Prototype chains, `this` binding, coercion, and array\n91:   holes are explicitly not wanted.\n92: - Being jq. Compatibility with a subset of jq's *semantics* is a starting point and a test\n93:   corpus, not a constraint on the finished language.\n\n(End of file - total 93 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# What toylang is\n\nA compiled, statically typed language derived from the jq family -- a jq dialect that keeps\njq's semantics as its reference while extending and generalizing them, aspiring to be a real\nlanguage rather than a study.\n\n- **Data-oriented.** JSON is the native value model, not a library, and data orientation is\n  the organizing principle: transformation, selection, and querying of data are how programs\n  are structured, not one feature among many.\n- **Compiled**, with seven backends (native/LLVM, JavaScript, Lua, jq, Go, Python, Rust) --\n  kept as falsifiers of the design rather than as a compatibility promise\n  ([ADR 0002](../adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md)).\n- **Rust-like syntax**, aiming at a version of Go's simplicity without following it into the\n  absurd: where Go's refusals cost too much, ideas come from Rust instead. Enums are the\n  first such import.\n- **The ambition is general-purpose** -- eventually HTTP servers, GUIs, web frontends, shell\n  scripts -- but through a deliberate sequence. The beachhead is CLI data transformation,\n  where the dogfood test is concrete: it replaces jq in its author's own shell. Shell\n  scripting is the first area to expand into after that. Result-set-oriented tooling (an\n  editor whose buffer is a query result) stays on the long horizon, and nearer-term design","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-recursive-descent-order-research/docs/overview/vision.md","text":"# What toylang is\n\nA compiled, statically typed language derived from the jq family -- a jq dialect that keeps\njq's semantics as its reference while extending and generalizing them, aspiring to be a real\nlanguage rather than a study.\n\n- **Data-oriented.** JSON is the native value model, not a library, and data orientation is\n  the organizing principle: transformation, selection, and querying of data are how programs\n  are structured, not one feature among many.\n- **Compiled**, with seven backends (native/LLVM, JavaScript, Lua, jq, Go, Python, Rust) --\n  kept as falsifiers of the design rather than as a compatibility promise\n  ([ADR 0002](../adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md)).\n- **Rust-like syntax**, aiming at a version of Go's simplicity without following it into the\n  absurd: where Go's refusals cost too much, ideas come from Rust instead. Enums are the\n  first such import.\n- **The ambition is general-purpose** -- eventually HTTP servers, GUIs, web frontends, shell\n  scripts -- but through a deliberate sequence. The beachhead is CLI data transformation,\n  where the dogfood test is concrete: it replaces jq in its author's own shell. Shell\n  scripting is the first area to expand into after that. Result-set-oriented tooling (an\n  editor whose buffer is a query result) stays on the long horizon, and nearer-term design\n  decisions must not foreclose it.\n\nAspiring to be real puts weight on claims that a study could leave as prose: the performance\nthesis (columnar, vectorized, faster than jq's boxed-iterator-per-step ceiling) is a\ncommitment that will eventually owe benchmarks, and positioning worries like\n[recursive descent's cost](../../plans/questions.md#q7-does--promise-depth-first-order-or-only-the-set-of-nodes)\nare real product concerns, not rhetorical ones.\n\nThe front end is written from scratch\n([`plans/prototype_1.md`](../../plans/prototype_1.md)); an earlier plan to fork jaq's front\nend and replace only its interpreter was dropped, because jaq's parser and IR encode jq's\nsurface syntax, which a Rust-like syntax does not survive, and the jq-conformance corpus that\nplan leaned on is a non-goal. jq stays a reference for semantics, not a conformance target.\n\n## Two guiding principles\n\n**1. Do not erase boundaries.** When a structural distinction exists, keep it in the type\nrather than flattening it away. If a computation crosses a boundary, the crossing is written\ndown. Corollaries appear throughout: `[...]` is an explicit operator rather than an implicit\ncoercion, a tuple of streams beats a concatenated stream with a phantom type parameter, and\n`Json` is a named type rather than a permissive escape hatch.\n\n**2. Symmetry.** The type-level guarantee and the runtime guarantee must be the *same*\nguarantee. If the type system claims two things are distinguishable, the runtime must be able\nto distinguish them, and vice versa. A guarantee that exists only at one level is a bug in\nthe design.\n\n## Values\n\n```\nnull   true   42   3.14   \"text\"   [1, 2, 3]   {name: \"ada\", age: 36}\n```\n\nThe JSON value forms, with `Str` a real string type rather than an untyped blob.\n\n## Two worked programs\n\nVision, not documentation: these use features the language does not build yet (`fold {} with`,\n`^.`, `on \"]q\"`). They show the direction, not what runs today.\n\nShell, counting errors per service, streaming, in constant memory:\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env toylang\nstdin.lines\n  | parse_json?                              # skip malformed lines\n  | select(.level == \"ERROR\")\n  | fold {} with (acc, e) -> acc[e.service] += 1\n  | to_entries | sort_by(-.value) | .[]\n  | \"\\(.key)\\t\\(.value)\"\n```\n\nEditor, where the buffer is a query result:\n\n```\nfn view(project: Project) -> Vec<Row> =\n    [ project.files[]\n      | .diagnostics[]\n      | select(.severity >= WARN)\n      | {file: ^.path, line: .line, text: .msg} ]\n\non \"]q\" -> cursor.next()\non \"gf\" -> open(cursor.get().file, cursor.get().line)\n```\n\nThere is no buffer, only a reified search.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- JavaScript semantic compatibility. Prototype chains, `this` binding, coercion, and array\n  holes are explicitly not wanted.\n- Being jq. Compatibility with a subset of jq's *semantics* is a starting point and a test\n  corpus, not a constraint on the finished language.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":93,"totalLines":93,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/overview/vision.md","time":{"start":1788296897562,"end":1788296897572}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I have a good understanding of the framing now. Let me also read the vision.md line 24-26 which mentions the columnar/vectorized thesis and recursive descent's cost, and the prototype_1_5_step_6.md line 27-28 about \"columnar question becomes concrete... Q7's 'recursive descent is embarrassingly parallel on a flat layout'\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ecd2417001xY76BbmYSB1ay2","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd1605001HaWaOZerGD1Z4l"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788296897582,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd242b001gKNk5B2G7j7oKx","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","messageID":"msg_05ecd1605001HaWaOZerGD1Z4l","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":50995,"input":2652,"output":134,"reasoning":81,"cache":{"write":0,"read":48128}},"cost":0.000981128}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788296898420,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ecd276e0019G2OALW54huIAb","messageID":"msg_05ecd243800122YM24o6d9zhK3","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","snapshot":"9289c98f16c1ea5e20aeea4a0409a81250d5ccb2","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788296985766,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"id":"prt_05ece796c001c1EAGBXacYnPbN","messageID":"msg_05ecd243800122YM24o6d9zhK3","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","type":"text","text":"The board row doesn't exist in board.yaml yet — that's fine; I'll write the research doc per the brief. Let me read the remaining framing and then survey external sources.\n\n\n\n","time":{"start":1788296984940,"end":1788296985758}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788296985767,"sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"webfetch","callID":"call_24ff9e1aee434e568a46c4fe","state":{"status":"error","input":{"url":"https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/","format":"markdown"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788296985750,"end":1788296985762}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I now have a strong grasp of the framing. Let me now look at a couple more relevant bits:\n\n- line  ́554-565 in draft.md established that `..` composed with a matcher is \"find this shape somewhere\" and two runs disagreeing on order is a correctness problem for whatever consumes the result.\n\n- Q7's own text: flat columnar layout, \"every node at every depth\" is \"every element of every buffer\", embarrassingly parallel. The dependent part is not the traversal but the *order*.\n\n- The two-layer model's effect layer; `..` in the current design seems value-layer per the lean (line 177-179).\n\nNow, the research task:\n\nSurvey how other query/traversal languages and engines (jq, XPath, JSONPath, columnar/vectorized query engines like Arrow/DuckDB) handle recursive-descent order versus set semantics, and what a toylang-specific unordered fast path would concretely cost callers who need jq's order back (e.g. an explicit sort/first afterward). Write findings with real examples to plans/recursive-descent-order-research.md.\n\n\n\nLet me plan the research. I need real, accurate examples. Let me think carefully about each system from knowledge, and verify key claims where I can. I should be careful about accuracy — the AGENTS.md says \"Never invent an API signature\" and \"A plausible-looking wrong argument is the most expensive thing in this document.\" So I should verify things I can, and mark uncertainty where I'm not sure.\n\n\n\nLet me structure my knowledge of each system:\n\n### jq\n\n- `..` is the recursive descent operator: \"Short-hand for recurse. recurse, .., is the same as: recurse(.). recurse(f) is equivalent to ., f, f|f, f|f|f, ...\" Actually jq's `recurse` produces: input, then f(input), then f(f(input)), etc. `..` = `recurse(.)`? Actually jq docs: \"recurse(f) ... is equivalent to def recurse(f): ., f, f|f, f|f|f, ...;\". And `..` is \"Recursive Descent: `..` is short-hand for `recurse(.)`.\" Let me recall precisely from jq manual: \n\nFrom jq manual (1.6+):\n- `..`: Short-hand for `recurse(.)` — \"recursive descent\" — walk the entire structure depth-first, emitting every value including the root.\n\nExample: `..` on `[[1]]`? Actually jq: `.[]` iterates arrays/objects. `.a.b` etc. `..` emits every node in depth-first preorder? Let me recall the exact order. jq's recursive descent order is depth-first, with children in the order they appear in arrays (index order) and objects (insertion order). For `{\"a\": {\"b\": 1}, \"c\": 2}`? Hmm. Let me think about `recurse(.)`: emits `.`, then `recurse(f)` over... Actually `recurse(f)` applies f to the input, then recurses into each of the outputs? No — recurse(f) emits `., f, f|f, ...`? Hmm, I need to be precise.\n\nFrom jq manual: \"recurse(f), g)\" — \"recurse(f) emits ., f, f|f, f|f|f, ...\" — no wait, that's not right either. Let me recall the actual def in jq's builtin.jq:\n\n```jq\ndef recurse(f): def r: ., (f | r); r;\ndef recurse: recurse(.[]?);\n```\n\nHmm, I recall: `def recurse(f): def r: ., (f | r); r;`. So it emits ., then f(.), then f(f(.)),... — a linear chain, not full tree. And `recurse` (no arg) = `recurse(.[]?)` which applies `.[]?` — every value emits its children (all array elements/object values), recursively, depth-first. So `..` on `[1, [2]]` emits: the root `[1, [2]]`, then 1, then [2], then 2. Order: root, first child subtree..., etc. — standard preorder depth-first.\n\nExample: jq `[1,[2,[3]]]] | ..` yields:\n```\n[1,[2,[3]]]]\n1\n[2,[3]]]\n2\n[3]]\n3\n```\nWait — after root, `.[]?` on root yields 1 then [2,[3]]. Then recurse each. So: root; 1; then subtree [2,[3]]: emits [2,[3]], then 2, then [3], then 3. Actually `recurse(.[]?)` = `def r: , (.[]? | r); r` — emits self, then for each child (which `.[]?` yields), recurses. So order is preorder DFS: parent, then children in order, each child's subtree fully.\n\nFor objects: `.[]?` iterates values in insertion order. So depth-first preorder, children in array-index / object-insertion order.\n\nOrder is deterministic and promised. jq manual says \"The `..` operator... walks the entire structure depth-first.\"? I believe the manual says: \"Recursive Descent: `..` is short-hand for `recurse(.)`; recurse... emits the input and all its descendants, in depth-first order.\" I'm fairly confident jq promises depth-first order implicitly by its semantics (it's just an expression yielding a stream in a defined order; streams in jq are ordered — everything is ordered).\n\nAlso relevant: jq's `..` includes the root itself.\n\n `. | ..` would emit root then everything.\n\n `..` alone on input X emits X itself and all descendants.\n\n\n\nOther relevant jq ordering facts:\n- jq object iteration `.[]` yields values in insertion order (key order as written in the JSON). Actually for JSON parsed objects, jq preserves insertion order. \n- `select` preserves order (it's a filter over the stream).\n- `first(expr)` takes the first output, which relies on order.\n- `path(..)` yields the paths of all values in DFS order.\n- `unique`, `sort`, `sort_by` are explicit order-changing operators — user opt-in.\n\n\n### XPath\n\nXPath (1.0 and 2.0/3.0): the `//` abbreviation for `/descendant-or-self::node()/`. This produces nodes in **document order** — not depth-first visitation order, butthe document order of the nodes (the order the nodes appear in the serialized document). For most documents these coincide for the *set* of matching nodes but differ in details (e.g. `//foo` returns all foo elements in document order; document order is defined by the source document, and is a total order on nodes). Also relevant: XPath axes include `descendant`, `descendant-or-self`, `child`, `following`, `preceding`, `ancestor`. XPath semantics: node-set results are unordered *as a set* but always reported in document order when serialized. Actually the XPath 1.0 spec says node-sets are unordered; the spec defines \"document order\" and says that conversion of a node-set to a string etc uses document order. In practice, `//` returns nodes in document order, which is deterministic.\n\nKey point for our research: XPath distinguishes **node-set** (unordered set semantics) from **document order** (the canonical order for serializing/reporting). A query returns a set; there is a canonical order defined independently of traversal — document order — used whenever the result needs an order (serialization, string-value, position(), etc.). Position-dependent predicates `[1]`, `position()` rely on document order (actually on the context axis order: for `//foo[1]` the `[1]` selects the first foo in document order? Careful: in XPath 1.0, `//foo[1]` means every foo that is the first foo child of its parent? No — that's `//foo[1]` = `(//foo)[1]`? Actually precedence: `//foo[1]` is `descendant-or-self::node()/child::foo[1]` — the predicate applies to each foo individually in its child axis, so it selects the first foo *child of each parent*. To select the first foo in the whole document you write `(//foo)[1]`.) This is a classic XPath gotcha and directly relevant to ordering questions.\n\n\n\nXPath 2.0+ made sequences ordered explicitly;the single-axis limitation removed. Still, `//` = `/descendant-or-self::node()/` yields in document order.\n\n\n\nFor vectorized/columnar engines — this is where it gets interesting:\n\n### Apache Arrow\n\nArrow is a columnar in-memory format, not a query engine. But relevant: nested layouts via ListArray (offsets+values buffer), StructArray (child arrays per field)., and DictionaryArray. Iteration order over a ListArray/StructArray is **by row index**: arrays have a defined length and slot i; any \"flattening\" or \"explosion\" yields rows in index order. Arrow C++ has `Flatten`/`Explode`? There's `compute::...`. Actually Arrow Compute has functions like `list_flatten`? In pyarrow, `StructArray.flatten()` yields child arrays;there's `pc.list_flatten`? I recall Arrow C++ compute has `list_flatten` and also \"explode\" in the SQL sense via `pc.utf8...`. Hmm. Let me be careful — I can verify some via webfetch if needed, but maybe I can phrase generally: in Arrow, walking a nested structure depth-first is possible but is not a primitive; the format is a set of buffers with an implicit row ordering. Traversal is by index; \"every node at every depth\" would correspond to something like concatenating all buffers, but node identity/order isn't defined by the format.\n\n\n\n### DuckDB\n\nDuckDB — columnar analytical SQL engine. Recursive queries (WITH RECURSIVE) are supported but produce rows in arbitrary/unspecified order unless ORDER BY. Actually SQL semantics: a query result is a **set** (or multiset/table) with no intrinsic order; ORDER BY is the only way to get order. Recursive CTEs: DuckDB docs note that recursion... \"results are not guaranteed to be in any particular order\" — I believe DuckDB docs say recursive CTEs may return rows in any order and ORDER BY is needed. Let me recall: DuckDB blog/docs on recursive CTEs mention they don't guarantee order? I think yes — SQL standard: recursive CTE results have no defined order.\n\n\n\nBut there's a more relevant DuckDB feature: `UNNEST` — columnar explode. `UNNEST(list_col)` produces one row per element in index order within each row. And \"flattening\" nested JSON/structs via `json_extract`, `struct_pack`, etc.\n\n DuckDB's `json_each`/`json_group_array`... produce rows. The key columnar point: DuckDB processes nested structures in **row order**, not depth-first. For JSON there's `json_extract_path`, and functions to recursively flatten? There's a community `flatten`/`json_flatten`? Hmm.\n\n\n\nAlso very relevant: DuckDB has `unnest` with ordering semantics defined? In DuckDB, `UNNEST` of a list yields elements in list index order (docs: \"the elements are unnested in order\"? I think yes, but the interaction across multiple unnested columns is cartesian.\n\n\n\nMore relevant still for the \"unordered fast path\" question: **SQL has no order; ORDER BY is the explicit restore**. This is the canonical example of \"set semantics + explicit sort\" — the exact pattern the task mentions (\"an explicit sort/first afterward\").\n\n\n\n### JSONPath\n\nJSONPath (Goessner original): recursive descent is `$..name`; order is **document order**? The Goessner spec: `..` \"Recursive descent\" — \"the result is in document order\"? Actually the original JSONPath definition (Goessner 2007) says `$..book` returns all book members in document order? I recall the \"JSONPath expressions always refer to a JSON structure in the same way as XPath... `..` Recursive descent\". Order: implementations differ! This is a well-known pain point: JSONPath implementations do not agree on order (or even on whether results are deduplicated). Stefan Gössner's spec doesn't formally define order; RFC 9535 (JSONPath, 2024) — I should check what it says about ordering. I recall RFC 9535 defines \"normalized path\" and \"ordered set\"? Let me recall: RFC 9535 says query results are \"a set of nodes, each with a normalized path\", and \"the result of a query is an ordered collection of nodes, ordered by\"? Hmm — I believe RFC 9535 explicitly says nodes are returned \"in document order\" for descendant segments? Let me verify via webfetch — this is exactly the kind of thing worth getting right.\n\n\n\nLet me recall RFC 9535 (JSONPath: JSON Query Language), February  ́2024. It defines segments: child segment `[name]`, wildcard `[*]`, descendant segment `..[name]`, slice, filter. I believe it says: \"The result of a query is an ordered collection of nodes\" — and that the \"ordered collection\" preserves document order? There was debate. Actually I recall RFC 9535 states: \"An ordered collection of nodes is a sequence of nodes in document order\"? Hmm. Let me fetch it to check. Also `..` in RFC 9535: \"descendant segment: selects zero or more nodes that are descendants of the current node\"... The order: RFC says results are in \"document order\"? Let me actually fetch RFC 9535 and check. Also there's the \"JSONPath Comparison\" wiki page that shows implementation disagreements on ordering — maybe useful.\n\n\n\nLet me also recall:\n- jq order promises: definite DFS preorder, including root, deterministic.\n- XPath: `//` document order (which for matching elements is the same as a total order, distinct from traversal order conceptually; and there are cases where document order differs from the order a naive DFS would emit? For XML, document order of nodes: document order is the order nodes appear in the source; a preorder DFS over the tree emits nodes in exactly document order for element nodes? Actually yes: document order for XML is preorder DFS over the tree (with attributes before children), so they coincide for element/child traversal. But the point is different: XPath defines order independent of the query's traversal — it's a property of the document, so any query over the same document produces results in the same order; different queries with different traversal orders still yield document order for the result set. That's a key design pattern: **order attached to the data, not the traversal**.\n\n\n\n- SQL/DuckDB: result sets unordered; ORDER BY explicit. Recursive CTEs unspecified order.\n\n\n- Arrow: row-index order; flattening preserves index order; \"every node\" would need an explicit walk.\n\n- jq: order is part of stream semantics; everything is ordered; `sort` is explicit opt-in to change it.\n\n\n\nNow the toylang-specific part. The design:\n- `..` is \"full tree traversal\" in the search vocabulary table; currently depth-first per draft (\"every node, depth-first\").\n- Columnar/SoA layout: a `Vec<Record>` is struct-of-arrays; nested `Vec<Vec<T>>` is pointer columns. \"Every node at every depth\" on a flat columnar layout = \"every element of every buffer\", embarrassingly parallel.\n- Q7: if the language only promises the set, recursive descent becomes one of the cheapest operators rather than one of the most expensive.\n\nThe research must answer: what alternatives actually exist to the unordered/columnar-fast-path design? I.e., survey the design space:\n\n1. **Ordered traversal (jq today, XPath/JSONPath document order)** — DFS preorder. Cost: needs a traversal that visits nodes in a defined order; on a columnar layout this means either (a) a pointer-chase gather loop, or (b) materializing depth-first order (a permutation/index array), via sorting or a traversal pass, before the values are produced. \n\n2. **Set semantics + document/row order defined independently (XPath/JSONPath)** — results as sets; order defined by the data not the traversal. This gives an ordered answer without depth-first traversal — IF the underlying layout has an order (row index). For a tree this becomes \"which nodes, and in what order\"? Document order requires the document to define an order: row index for the top-level array; but nodes at different depths don't have a single index. Hmm — actually document order for a JSON document is well-defined (preorder), so this collapses back to DFS order for the set of all nodes. Unless you take the flattening view: order by (row, column, depth...)? \n\nActually wait. There's a genuinely different option available on a columnar layout: **define the order by the layout itself** — \"buffer order\" — i.e., the set of nodes in the order the flat buffers lay them out (e.g., all root children first, then all grandchildren grouped by column, etc.). That's an order that's cheap to produce (it's just concatenating buffers, each already in row order) but is NOT depth-first preorder. It's essentially **level-order / breadth-ish by buffer**. Actually on SoA: `Vec<Record>` = columns per field; `..` over a record's fields would emit all `.a` values (the a column), then all `.b` values... — field-major order. And over nested arrays: parent rows in row order, then... hmm, the depth structure. \n\nThe key design space:\n- **DFS preorder (jq)** — expensive on columnar layout; requires gather/traversal or a precomputed index.\n\n- **Level-order/buffer-order (cheap)** — the natural columnar order; what \"every element of every buffer\" gives; cheap (concatenate/scan buffers) but not jq-compatible; still *an* order (deterministic), so two runs agree).\n- **Set semantics (unordered)** — even cheaper potentially (no order guarantee at all; the consumer defines what it needs); jq incompatible for order-dependent consumers.\n\nThe middle option — level/buffer order — is worth naming because it isn't \"unordered\": it keeps determinism (two runs agree) while being cheap. But it has its own problem: it's not any order a caller would expect, so it's as unusable as unordered for jq-order-dependent callers, but WITHOUT being honest about it. Hmm — actually is it unusable? If order is defined (documented), callers can rely on it for deterministic matching (e.g., `first(...)` picks a definite node). It's just not jq's order, so ported jq programs would behave differently (different first hit when multiple match).\n\n\n\nWhat concretely does an unordered fast path cost callers who need jq's order back?\n\n\nScenario: `first(.. | select(.id == 7))` — \"the first node with id 7\". With DFS order, that's deterministic: the first in preorder. With unordered, `first` is meaningless (needs a definition. Options: `sort_by(path(..))` to restore DFS? In toylang terms: to restore jq-compatible DFS order from an unordered result, you'd have to (1) materialize the whole result, (2) sort it by path (lexicographic path comparison in preorder order? — careful: sorting by path lexicographically does NOT give preorder for heterogeneous shapes? For a tree, preorder order of nodes = lexicographic order of paths? Actually yes for rooted trees when children are ordered: the set of nodes in preorder corresponds exactly to lexicographic order of root-to-node paths, if sibling order is by index/key. So sorting by path restores DFS preorder. Cost: O(n log n) sort + full materialization, vs O(n) single pass. For `first`, you could compute min path = find the lexicographically smallest path among matches — which is a **reduction/min**, parallelizable! So \"first in DFS order\" = \"min path among matches\", which IS computable columnarly (the path itself would need to be tracked, though — a column of paths, or a trie). Interesting — so the fast path isn't necessarily \"give up order\"; you can define DFS order via min-of-paths and compute it as a reduction. But the reduction gives you only the *first*; producing the *whole sequence* in DFS order still needs a sort.\n\n\n\nConcretely, what callers need:\n- Programs that use `..` as the *whole feature*: \"find every node\" (e.g., diagnostics collection, tree-to-table flattening). Order matters if downstream is position-sensitive (e.g., zipping with another ordered list, printing in a defined order for diffable output, using `first`/`[0]`/`path(...)[N]`).\n- jq programs porting to toylang that use `first(.. | select(p))` or `.[0]` style take-first: with unordered semantics, first is underspecified → wrong/random results.\n\nThe task says \"what a toylang-specific unordered fast path would concretely cost callers who need jq's order back (e.g. an explicit sort/first afterward.\" So I need concrete examples: e.g.,\n\n```\ntree.. | select(.kind == \"error\")        # DFS order today: errors in source order\n```\n\nUnder unordered:\n```\n[tree.. | select(.kind == \"error\")] | sort_by(path)   # explicit restore — O(n log n), needs path column\n```\n\nor for first:\n```\nfirst(tree.. | select(.id == 7))    # today: leftmost-in-source\n# unordered alternative: need to compute min path — but \"first\" over an unordered stream is undefined\n```\n\nCosts:\n- **Materialization**: unordered `..` could stream? Hmm — on a columnar layout, producing the whole node set as a flat buffer is cheap; but if you need DFS order, you need either the full sort (n log n, or a gather with a precomputed DFS permutation — computing that permutation is itself a traversal, O(n), with pointer chasing, on the device... which is the expensive thing again, just pushed into a kernel.\n- **The `first` idiom breaks**: jq's killer idiom `first(.. | select(...))` = \"the leftmost match\"; under unordered semantics it becomes \"some match\", nondeterministic across runs — violating the design's own batch-invariance law (two runs must agree). Actually batch invariance is about batching a stream, not traversal order; but the broader determinism principle (\"the type-level guarantee and the runtime guarantee must be the same\"; \"platform-independent\") applies.\n- **Diffable/stable output**: tools that compare output across runs (e.g., snapshot tests — which this repo's corpus is! ) need determinism. Actually corpus cases: every case runs on every backend and they must all print it and agree — that is THE killer constraint: **the corpus harness requires all backends to produce the same bytes**! If `..` order were unordered, the seven backends could disagree on order → corpus cases with `..` would fail. Wait — unless each backend picks its own order deterministically and they happen to agree? No — the corpus requires agreement across backends. So the \"unordered fast path\" would need all backends to agree on *some* order anyway (or `..` could never appear in a corpus case). Actually — hmm, careful: \"unordered\" could mean \"any order, but deterministic per backend\" → corpus harness would see backends disagree → test failure. \"Unspecified order\" is exactly the failure mode the agreement harness exists to catch. So this research can point that out: **the corpus/agreement architecture itself demands a defined cross-backend order** — which is an independent, strong argument that `..` must promise *some* order, and that the question is really \"which order\", not \"order or not\". That's a genuinely valuable finding grounded in this repo's own structure.\n\n\n\nLet me double check the corpus harness claim: \"Every case runs on every backend. output means they all print it and agree.\" Yes — AGENTS.md lines 121-122. And Q35 says \"the agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse\" for errors. For output, they must agree byte-for-byte. So any corpus case exercising `..` — like a diagnostics collection program — requires every backend to produce identical bytes, which requires a defined order. Unless `..` is banned from corpus cases, which would be a huge hole (it's \"one of the two things people reach for jq to do\").\n\n\n\nAlso relevant to the maintainer's positioning concern (vision.md: \"a jq-derived language that is fast everywhere except recursive descent has a positioning problem, because `..` is one of the two things people reach for jq to do\"). The research should weigh: does an unordered fast path actually solve the positioning problem? No — it makes `..` fast but *behaviorally different* from jq, so it's still \"not jq where it matters\" for the porting audience, except now the difference is silent (output order differs, non-deterministic) rather than visible (slow). Actually worse: fast-but-wrong-order is worse than slow-but-right-order for the \"reach for jq\" use case, because the user's pipeline silently produces different results.\n\n\n\nNow, some concrete survey details to verify:\n\n1. **jq `..` exact semantics** — I should verify the preorder claim. Let me fetch jq manual (https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/) — it says `..`: \"Short-hand for recurse(.)... \" and \"recursive descent\". Let me check the exact wording and whether order is stated. jq's recurse is: `def recurse(f): def r: ., (f | r); r;`? And `..` = `recurse(.[]?)`? Hmm — actually I recall `..` is defined as `recurse(.[]?)`? Let me check. In jq's builtin.jq: \n\n```\ndef recurse(f): def r: , (f|r); r;\ndef recurse: recurse(.[]?);\ndef .. as recurse(.[]?)? \n```\n\nHmm. There's a subtlety: `..` includes the root. jq manual example: `.[] | ..`? The manual's example for `..`:\n\n```\n[1, [2]] | ..\n=> [1, [2]], 1, [2], 2]?\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure of exact example. Let me fetch the manual to verify.\n\n2. **RFC 9535 JSONPath order** — fetch to check whether it mandates document order or leaves order implementation-defined.\n\n\n\n3. **XPath document order** — well established; I can cite XPath 1.0 spec (W3C). \"document order\" defined; node-sets unordered but results serialized in document order. The XPath 1.0 spec says: \"The document order of a node... A node-set is converted to a string by... in document order.\" Also \"//\" abbreviation. I'm confident enough; might verify via a quick fetch of the spec if cheap.\n\n\n\n4. **DuckDB recursive CTE / order** — I can verify via docs fetch if cheap; otherwise phrase from SQL standard knowledge (\"SQL result tables have no defined order; ORDER BY required\"). For recursive CTEs specifically, I recall DuckDB's docs mention... hmm, actually I recall a DuckDB blog \"Recursive CTEs\" (by Gabor Szarnyas) that says recursion produces rows... Let me fetch the DuckDB docs on recursive CTEs to check whether they say order is not guaranteed.\n\n\n\n\n\n5. **Arrow flatten order** — arrow docs; maybe fetch the C++ compute docs? Might be more than needed. I can phrase generally: arrays have a row-index order; flattening/exploding preserves index order. pyarrow: `StructArray.flatten()` yields arrays of the same length (row-aligned).. ListArray: `values` buffer plus offsets; row i's slice is `values[offsets[i]:offsets[i+1]]]`. So \"walking the tree\" in row order is the natural traversal; depth-first over nested lists requires pointer chasing across buffers but within a buffer is index-order. Actually — in Arrow, a nested ListArray's `values` buffer contains all leaf values in row order (all rows' elements concatenated in row order) — so \"all leaf scalars in document order\" is literally a single buffer! That's a great concrete example: **Arrow's ListArray already stores \"every leaf in row-major order\" as one flat values buffer**; the offsets tell you row boundaries. So \"every node at every depth in the order the tree has them\" = preorder, but \"every leaf value\" = the values buffer, already flat. The gap is exactly the internal nodes (and their interleaving with leaves). A query for \"every leaf\" is cheap (one buffer); for \"every node in preorder\" needs a walk that interleaves internal and leaf rows in the DFS pattern.\n\n\n\nHmm wait — \"document order\"/preorder for a JSON tree = interleave every object, key, array, scalar... The root array `[1, [2,3],4]`: preorder nodes: [1,[2,3],4], 1, [2,3], 2, 3, 4. Leaf scalars in row order: 1,2,3,4. Internal nodes interleave. On a flat columnar layout (SoA/offsets), emitting all nodes in preorder = merging per-row and per-depth — the expensive gather. Emitting \"all scalars\" = one flat scan. Emitting \"all nodes by buffer, depth-major\" = cheap concat. Emitting set (no order) = same as any.\n\nSo there are really these distinct cheap-ish semantics on a columnar layout:\n1. all leaves (scalars), flat\n2. all nodes, depth-major/buffer order (concatenate per-depth buffers), deterministic, cheap\n3. all nodes, row-major within each top-level row (per-row preorder, i.e., `tree[] | ..` — split the traversal per top-level element, then DFS within each): each top-level element's subtree preorder-ordered; across rows, row order. This is \"preorder per top-level row\" — which is what a columnar engine could do WITHOUT a global gather: for each top-level row, run DFS (pointer-chasing within that row's buffers... hmm, still expensive per row unless rows' subtrees are each flat).\n\nHmm — actually, wait. On SoA, is there a layout where DFS preorder over the whole tree is cheap? Only if the data is stored in DFS order already (a traversal-index permuted layout: \"table of (path, value) rows in preorder\"). That's exactly what jq's boxed iterator does naturally (the interpreter walks the tree in memory). jq's \"ceiling\" is per-node interpreter overhead, not the traversal itself. So the real tradeoff: jq's order is free because its data structure is a tree (pointers); a columnar layout sacrifices that to be flat; DFS order over flat data costs a gather (or a precomputed permutation column, which is computed once per load... but for a *query result* the permutation must be computed per query).\n\n\n\nGood — this is a solid survey framework. Let me also think about other systems worth including:\n\n- **jq's `..` vs `paths`**: jq `paths` emits paths (arrays of keys/indices), also in DFS order; `paths(scalars)` emits paths of scalars. `[paths]` gives you the DFS order as path list — the \"sort key\" for restoring order from an unordered result.\n\n\n\n- **Icon / Prolog / nondeterministic search** — the draft already grounds itself in Prolog. Prolog: clause order = depth-first, left-to-right; cut (`!`) commits. This is an *ordered* nondeterminism — order is semantic (it determines which answer you get first; `findall` preserves clause order). So the \"set of answers\" framing in draft.md still has a defined order — the draft table says \"an expression denotes a set of possible answers, and evaluation explores all of them in a fixed order.\" Good: the draft already commits to \"fixed order\" for nondeterminism generally! `..` is listed as \"full tree traversal\" with \"depth-first\". So the question Q7 is: for `..` specifically, do we relax that to \"set only\"? The draft's own \"Query is search\" section already defines order as part of the semantics (\"evaluation explores all of them in a fixed order\", and the table assigns search meanings to operators where `..` = \"full tree traversal\" and the example says \"every node, depth-first\"). So the current draft text ALREADY promises DFS. Q7 is asking whether to keep that promise for the flat-layout fast path. My research should note this: the language's own search semantics is already ordered (Prolog-style); `..` is the one place where the flat layout tempts a relaxation. Good context for the brief.\n\n\n\n- **JSONPath implementations disagreements** — concrete evidence that \"order unspecified\" causes real interop pain: e.g., Python jsonpath-ng vs jq vs others differ on `..` order? Actually many JSONPath impls do document order. The pain is more about dedup/multiple-match semantics (e.g., `$..book[0]` ambiguities, filters yielding all vs first...). RFC 9535 was written partly to nail these down. Worth checking RFC 9535's exact stance on order — I'll fetch it.\n\n\n\n\n\n- **XSLT**: `//` and nodesets; XSLT 1.0 `xsl:for-each select=\"//x\"` processes in document order. Also XPath 2.0+ sequences: order matters explicitly; `//x[1]` selects first in document order (unlike 1.0 gotcha — in 2.0, predicates apply to the whole sequence). Nice illustrative contrast for how order semantics interact with `[1]`-style position access. Worth a mention.\n\n\n\n- **SQL `ORDER BY` on recursive CTEs / graph traversals**: Postgres recursive CTE: \"the rows are returned in the order the traversal visited them\"? Hmm — actually Postgres docs say: \"for a recursive CTE, the rows are returned in breadth-first search order?\" No. Let me recall. Postgres docs on WITH RECURSIVE: \"the recursive query evaluation... produces working table... the rows are... in the order...\". I recall Postgres docs note: \"When working with recursive queries it is important to be sure that the recursive part of the query will eventually return no tuples...\"; regarding order: \"If ORDER BY is used in the recursive term it is ignored\" — yes! Postgres: \"ORDER BY in the recursive term is ignored; you can't use ORDER BY there\" — I recall that. And the overall result order is unspecified unless outer ORDER BY. That's another concrete data point: **even a system that supports recursive traversal (Postgres) gives you no order out of the recursion; you add ORDER BY at the top.** Also Postgres has a \"search depth-first\" / \"breadth-first\" clause since v14?? Hmm — Postgres 14 added `SEARCH DEPTH FIRST BY` and `SEARCH BREADTH FIRST BY` clauses for recursive CTEs! That's a *direct* precedent for \"explicit order restoration after an unordered recursion\" — you must *opt in* to depth-first ordering, with a SEARCH clause, which the implementation uses to track a sort order (an extra column). That's a perfect concrete example for the research: **Postgres 14+ requires `SEARCH DEPTH FIRST BY` to get DFS order out of a recursive CTE; without it, order is unspecified.** Let me verify this — I'm fairly confident (PG14 release notes: \"Add SEARCH and CYCLE clauses for recursive queries (Peter Eisentraut...)\" yes, PG 14.). I can cite it from memory but let me maybe verify with a quick webfetch to be safe. The cost pattern: you annotate the query with what order you want, and the engine tracks it (by adding an internal ordering column). The price: an extra column tracking the path/order, i.e., materialization + sort. This maps 1:1 to toylang's choice: promised DFS order costs the engine a traversal/sort; opted-in DFS (SEARCH DEPTH FIRST)-style costs the caller an annotation plus the same engine cost, but keeps `..` fast for callers who don't need order. Hmm — actually that's a really important design alternative: **a `..` that returns unordered, plus an opt-in order-restoring combinator** (e.g., `.. | depth_first` / a `SEARCH DEPTH FIRST`-like marker, or `sort_by(path)`).\n\n\n\nWait, but does that actually work for toylang? The corpus agreement constraint: if `..` alone returns unordered-but-deterministic-per-backend, backends disagree → corpus breaks. Unless \"unordered\" is defined as \"any order\" and `..` can't appear in corpus cases without an order-restoring combinator — hmm, but the combinator restores a *defined* order (DFS), which the backends can agree on; the unordered `..` alone is banned from agreeing harness? Actually the corpus requires agreement; so corpus cases would have to always attach the order restorer (making it non-optional in practice...). Or `..` alone could be defined as \"some fixed order agreed across backends\" — but then it's not unordered, it's just *a different cheap order* (level/buffer order). Hmm.\n\n\n\nLet me reconsider. The three real candidates:\n\n**A. Promise DFS (jq-compatible).** Cost: on the columnar backend, DFS requires gather/traversal or precomputed permutation per query. This is \"one of the most expensive operators\" — but it's *still* O(n), and vectorizable?? Can DFS traversal be a GPU kernel? Pointer-chasing gather is not embarrassingly parallel (dependent loads), but there are GPU tree-traversal techniques (e.g., for fixed arity/balanced trees; bounding-volume hierarchies traversal kernels). For arbitrary ragged JSON, DFS on GPU is hard but not impossible (work-efficient via path-stack; irregular).. The honest claim: DFS order costs a gather/irregular memory access pattern; the flat-layout advantage (contiguous SIMD-friendly accesses) evaporates for `..`.\n\n\n\n**B. Promise a defined cheap order (buffer order / level order / \"layout order\").** Deterministic (corpus-safe), cheap, but not jq-compatible; callers who want DFS must sort by path (which restores preorder — verify that claim!). This is a real option the maintainer hasn't listed — it's worth naming because it satisfies determinism and speed, at the cost of compatibility. But it has the \"fast everywhere except it's subtly wrong for jq programs\" positioning problem — though *visible* wrongness only for order-dependent consumers.\n\n\n\n**C. Promise no order (set).** Cheapest;the caller must define what order it needs (explicit `sort_by(path)`, or min-path for `first`, or per-row split). Breaks jq porting silently; corpus harness demands either a defined order or `..` excluded from corpus→ not really viable given the agreement architecture, unless \"no order\" comes with an order-restoring required wrapper.\n\n\n\nActually let me refine: \"unordered set semantics\" for `..` specifically — what does it even mean at the type level in this language? `..` produces a `Stream`/`Vec` of nodes. A `Vec` in toylang has an order (it's a sequence; printing, indexing, zipping all depend on it.). \"Unordered set\" would be a *new* kind of value (a set/bag) — the language has no sets! So \"promising only the set\" means `..` returns... what type? A `Vec` in unspecified order? That's an oxymoron for a typed language whose `Vec` semantics include order (the printer preserves it; `.field` on a Vec is a column in order; `+` concatenation is order-defined...). So there's a type-system argument: **in toylang, there is no unordered collection type; a `Vec`'s order is load-bearing in dozens of operations (printing, concatenation, zip under Q2, first, indexing...). \"`..` promises only the set\" would either introduce a new set/bag type (new machinery, Q4's regex-over-types...), or return a Vec with an undefined-but-present order (violating principle 1: don't erase boundaries — the distinction \"this Vec's order is meaningful/meaningless\" would be a boundary the type flattens away...). This is a strong point worth making — grounded in the design's own principles and type model.\n\n\n\nLet me also double-check the jq order details via the manual, RFC 9535, and maybe the Postgres docs. Let me fetch a few sources in parallel.\n\n\n\nAlso I want to double check the exact definition of jq `..`. The jq manual: \n\n\"`..`: Short-hand for `recurse(.)`.\" Hmm — actually I've also seen \"Recursive Descent: `..` is short-hand for `recurse(.)`\" — but recurse(.) applied to X emits X, X., X, X. — wait `recurse(f)` emits  ., f, f|f, f|f|f...? That would emit X, X., X., X... — that's wrong; `recurse(.)` would be infinite for objects. So `..` must be `recurse(.[]?)`. Let me just fetch the manual.\n\n\n\nLet me fetch:\n1. jq manual (section on `..` and `recurse`).\n2. RFC 9535 JSONPath — ordering.\n3. Postgres docs on recursive CTE SEARCH order clauses.\n\n\n\nLet me fetch these three.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ece7c8b001YSNdEdyTey4sFH","sessionID":"ses_fa1333961ffeFEYswdsRulsd6g","messageID":"msg_05ecd243800122YM24o6d9zhK3"}}
